      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>THE ST. HELENS POISONING MYSTERY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Inquest and Police Court Proceeding's.</head>
        <p>Inquest and Police Court Proceeding's.</p>
        <p>FURTHER ADJOURNMENTS.</p>
        <p>The interest in the trial of Mr. and Mrs. Burndred on the charge of murdering Sarah Ann Jones byadminister- ing poison grows keener every day, and since the announcement of Mr. Davies, the analyst, that he had discovered a considerable quantity of arsenic in the child's stomach,followed by the exhumation of the body of the prisoners' child,Joseph Burndred,the public mind has been highly excited by the prospects of still further sensational developments. On Tuesday the man and woman were again taken from Walton Goal to St. Helens to be brought before the magistrates, and at the railway station and Town Hall crowds gathered to catch a glimpse of them. Im- mediately the pair had left the train and reached the exit of the station they were hurried into a cab, which started off for the Town Hall. The crowds of people which had been scattered all over the Town Hall square made a rush for the front of the police office, but their efforts were rewarded by nothing more than a passing glimpse of the occupants of the cab as they were driven rapidly into the police yard. From here the prisoners were taken to the parade rooms,and thence to the cells. Shortly after their arrival the Burndreds were inter- viewed by their solicitor, Mr. H. L. Riley, and also had an opportunity of speaking to their daughter. Burndred's mother is still alive, but living in Stafford- shire, and his sister came to St. Helens on Tuesday to see him on behalf of his mother. Chief Constable Woods informed a Press representa- tive during the morning that he had received a com- munication from the analyst regarding the condition of the second body, but he declined to publish it. There are various rumours with regard to what the police found in the pg_ol at Tickle's brickworks, and in some well-informed eirters it is confidently asserted that an important discovery was'inade.</p>
        <p>THE POLICE PROCEEDINGS. ANOTHER REMAND.</p>
        <p>The police proceedings were exceptionally brief. The magistrates were Megsrs. NV. J. Thomson, Colonel J. W. M‘Tear, Councillors Martin and Crooks, and Mr. B. B. Glover. Mr. W. H. Andrew, the Town Clerk, said: In this case I have been asked by the Directors of Public Pro- secntions, of London, to take up the matter. The prisoners are charged with the murder of Sarah Ann Jones, and they were before the court on Monday, September sth on that charge,and remanded to Walton Gaol until to-day. It was thought desirable on behalf of the Crowe that further inquiries should be made, and that no evidence should be given to-day,and,under these circumstances, I ask that both prisoners be remanded for seven days—to this day week. The Magistrates' Clerk: You had better put In- spector Strong in the witness-box to give evidence. Mr. H. L. Riley appear on behalf of the prisoners, and lam content that they should be remanded, with- out a repetition of evideace which has already been recorded. Prisoners were then remanded until next Tuesday.</p>
        <p>THE RESUMED INQUEST. POSTPONED TILL THE 28TH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse immediately followed with his pro- ceeding, the jury of sixteen all being present. Mr. Brighouse said: Gentlemen of the jury, you will not be able to-day to resume your inquiries into the respective deaths of Sarah Ann Jones and Joseph Burndred for this reason: I have received the follow- ing message from Mr. H. E. Davies, analyst. The letter is dated the 10th of this month, and is addressed to myself. It says :—" I shall not be ready to give evid- ence on Monday or Taesday next. At present lam un- able to say if I shall he completely ready for the follow- ing week but I shall let you know towards the end of the week.—Yours faithfUllV, HERBERT E. DAVIES." The Coroner continued :—Tt is absolutely impossible to'go on with the inquiry until we know the result of the analysis on the viscera of Joseph Bnrndred, and therefore I propose to adjourn the inquiry until Wed- nesday, the 28th of this month, at eleven o'clock in the morning. Whether we shall then be able to go on with and complete our inquiries into either or both of the deaths will depend on circumstances. The only business I have to-day with you is now to bind you over to attend on that day. The inquiry was accordingly adjourned until the 28th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SEPTEMBER BIRD NOTES.</head>
        <p>SEPTEMBER BIRD NOTES.</p>
        <p>Bird-life deserves our closest attention in September. After the blaze and heat of summer—when our feathered friends became quiet and dull and were little seen—the freshness of early autumn says ".1. J. C." in the "Man- chester Evening News," brings life and activity once i more. September is a bright and cheerful month n the country, full of bird-notes and the flutter of wings. Some species are singing again—all are constantly utter- ing their call-notes. Many have gathered together in flocks for the winter, and our summer visitants are fast taking their departure to the south. This all tells plainly that summer is over, and already, the piping, blustering winds of autumn are beginning to roar through the trees, bringing the leaves and fruit whirling and pattering to the ground. • Let us briefly consider some of our autumn songsters—those birds which we may expect to hear in our country rambles round Manchester at this season of the year. Taken in order of merit, the robin comes first. It is in excellent voice, and warbles sweetly from dawn till dark. We meet with the robin everywhere just now—in our garden trees and hedges, in the lanes and woods, in wind-swept bushes high on the hill- slopes, even at the very margin of the sea. And wher- ever we come across it it is singing; even at the country railway stations we find it perched and voice- ful, now high on the telegraph wires, now down on the gleaming metals, or maybe displaying its rosy breast from the edge of some empty truck in a siding. The robin, indeed, is a remarkable bird in early autumn. The thrush is a regular autumn-songster, but as yet is only trying its voice. Next month he will be in good form, and if the" back-end" of the year be mild and open we may expect the thrush's song to be well main- tained throughout. The wild notes of the mistle thrush may be heard in the later months, but the blackbird seems not to sing at all in autumn in this part of the country.</p>
      </div>
