      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>, MR. PIERPOINT M.P., AND ELECTION</head>
        <p>, MR. PIERPOINT M.P., AND ELECTION</p>
        <p>PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>Speech at the Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the annual bowling main was played on the Conservative Club green, the following being the prize winners :-1, J. Holland, clock; 2, R. Sewell, walnut timepiece ; 3, T. Perks, case of cutlery ; 4, P. Bradburn, barometer; 5, E. Fowler, electro-plated tea- pot; 6, S. Hewitt, umbrella; 7, T. Coombes, handbag; 8, T. Turner, set of carvers. _</p>
        <p>In the evening the prizes were presented by Mrs. Pierpoint, who was cordially thanked on the motion of Councillor ROBERTS seconded by Alderman Strrros. The Alderman said ladies always had great influence— influence that surpassed everything a man could do— and whatever anxieties or difficulties he had had in his mind previously as to the result of the next election they had been removed, as he knew that by the influence and assistance of Mrs. Pierpoint they need not trouble anything at all about it, and he was sure they would win again by a large majority. There was one thing he would like to say. He had heard remarks very often that it would be very much more pleasant if they could have the member amongst them a good deal oftener than he was. (Hear, hear.) Well, if they would only con- sider the matter they would see that that was unreasonable, because Mr. Pierpoint could not be in London and Warrington at the same time. (Hear, hear.) He had to perform his duties—which were more onerous than any of them would imagine—and if he performed them properly and to the satisfaction of his constituents it was impossible for him to be in Warrington as well. It was all very well that they would like him to be at tea parties, receptions, &amp;c., like someone else who aspired to hold- ing the same position, but they.must look at the matter in this way: Supposing Mr. Pierpoint n'eglected his duties in Parliament and came to Warrington to take part in all the petty little affairs that happened in the I town, what would be said then? They would say that he was not representing the town in a proper manner, and was neglecting his duties. Mr. Pierpoint was doing his duty to them, and he did not think they could find a better representative. (Hear, hear.) Mr. R. 13IERPOINT, M.P., responded. Mr. Sutton, he said, had spoken to them about work in the House of Commons. Well, it was very tiring work—he did not say hard, but tiring—for it meant constant attendance while supporting the Government. He read the other day a letter written by a retiring member, in which he said the two happiest days of a member of Parliament's 'life were the day on which he got in and the day on which he got out. (Laughter.) He was inclined, to some extent, to agree with him regarding the first day, as it was a very happy time, because it ministered to one's natural pride to be elected representa- tive by a great constituency. (Applause.) But he did not agree concerning the second time. He had been now so long in the House of Commons that he had become accustomed, and almost wedded to the place. It was his natural place to go to,and although as be had said it was drudgery, and although it took him almost entirely from other occupations, still, he prized his membership so much ; he prized the honour so much, and be prized so much being actually in—if he might use the expression—one of the greatest theatres or transaction in the whole world, that he would be loath indeed to give it up. It would rest upon them, and those who were with them at the next election, whetherjhe would again represent them or not. He thought he would. (Hear, hear). In fact he felt sure that he would, if those who worked for him, for them- selves, for the party, and, might he say? for the good of the country, did their best again when the time came. (Applause). They must be ready for the call now—though he did not think the fight would be so soon as some had predicted—be ready to do their work well, and to the best of their ability—(applause)—not for his personal sake, but for the sake of the party, for the got:pi of the party, and, if he might put it so "a little bit for our sake." (Applause). Votes of thanks were passed to the Bowling Commit. the and to Mr. Sangster. the club manager, for the splendid condition in which he has kept the green.</p>
      </div>
