      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>aold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>There were good congregations at this church on Sun- day, the preacher at the morning service being the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees who took for his subject "Weeds." In the evening, the newly appointed minister, the Rev. J. Manning, preached on " The life that is worth living." The anthem "How lovely are the messengers " (from St. Paul,") was well rendered by the choir. The offer- ories for the day were in aid of the church funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Mission.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Mission.</p>
        <p>Harvest ,thanksgiving services were held in the above place of worship on Sunday. The interior of the build- ing had been neatly decorated with fruit, corn, flowers, vegetables itc.' and presented a. pleasing appearance. The preachers for the day were Mr. A. Perkins in the morning, the Rev. G. H. Carnson in the afternoon and Mr. J. Upson in the evening. Special anthems were given by the choir, and each of the services was much enjoyed and well-attended. On the following evening a fruit banquet was held, when the proceeds were in aid of the new building fund. Mr. J. Barlow presided and there was a good audience. A miscellaneous programme was submitted and refreshments were handed round at intervals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The u anal meeting was held on Sunday, when there was a fair attendance. Mr. W. Crosskill presided. Speaking upon the need of P.S.A. meetings, he said they largely met the needs and feelings of working men, who experienced a strong feeling of homeliness and brotherliness in the meetings. Mr. W. H. Hill, of Man- chester, was the speaker, and spoke upon the lawyer's question: " What good thing dan Ido ?" Being good, he said, was commendable, but doing good was a step further, and brought with it a great blessing to the giver as well as to the receiver of good. The world around us stood in need of help, especially the working class. Therefore let them grasp the opportunities for doing good.—The Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) welcomed six new members, making a total of 497. A very happy meeting was brought to a close in the usual manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Alex. Harvey of Widnes, took the services on Sunday last, preaching in the evening from the text "In the year that King Uzziah lied I saw the Lord" (Isaiah 6. 1.) King Uzziah had proved his power to lead and his sagacity as a ruler and as an administrator, and had filled the whole of Isaiah's vision. It was not until the King's death that Isaiah realized that behind his power was the greater power of God. A very small object was able to shut out a beautiful landscape and the human and the material were ofton allowed to obstruct the spiritual vision. For all our needs we should go direct to the unlimited power to be found only in God himself. The preacher pointed to the crucified and risen Christ, who had the power to save and to keep.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Buckley-Street Methodist Free Church, Warrington.</head>
        <p>Buckley-Street Methodist Free Church, Warrington.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday,services of thanksgiving for the ingathering of the harvest were held. The preacher at the morning and evening services was Mr. T. Bold.A musical service was held in the afternoon, solos being rendered by Miss L. Fairhurst (contralto), Kr. A Greenough (tenor), and Mr. M. Cook, (bass). Mr. E. England, who presided, gave an address. The accom- panists were Miss L. Plinston, and Mr. A. E. Trow. Special anthems were rendered throughout the day by the choir, and the services were well attended. On Monday evening a" fruit banquet" was held in the schoolroom, solos being rendered by Mrs. J. Hannan, Miss 1,. Plinston, Messrs. J. Hannan and T. Cook, Mr. Whittaker gave a ventriloquial sketch, and also several selections on the concertina. During the interval fruit was handed round.A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to all who had contributed to the evening's enjoyment. .</p>
        <p>SALE OF PASSIVE RESISTERS' GOODS. 1 Mr. A. Hutchinson, auctioneer, conducted a sale of goods seized from passive resisters, in the Free Church Schoolroom, Frodsham, :the other evening. The goods ! consisted of clothing, books, and gold watches. The resisters were Messrs. William Booth, William Noden, George S. Frith, and W. E. Thornton. The auctioneer made no charge for his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</p>
        <p>"Yes. 1 aon't approve of servants having absurd names, like Honey, so 7: calf her Margaret." Roddv resented the voice and manner. "May I ask, madam, why you think I am intereSted in Honey, 2! Margaret, as you call her 2" "You :are li. itaderick Strang, a:z yoi not ?!,' „,, looking at the card. Roddy bowed. r - "You are Margaret's old lUaster, then, and I took her on your recommendation." "On my recommendation?" Roddy repeated, with a stare of amazemen%</p>
        <p>"Certainly, for no other reason. I have your recommendation here. I was about to write to yo-a on the subject." With that she handed Roddy a letter. It was his own paper., his own address, his own handwriting. He turned the leaf. Yes, there was his own signature. lie read it like one stupefied. "Honey Dree has been in my -employment for eleven years; and throughout that time I have fourt her invariably sober, clean, honest, and attentive to her duties. She is a woman of thoroughly respect- able character, good-tempered and obliging, and well ckualifed to do every class house-work. There is no 'nonsense about her,"</p>
        <p>. . _ The whole business fla shed upon Roddy as he read. The letter was a joke. scrawled one evening when the romping spirit was upon them, and 'Honey and the girls had coaxed him to write their characters Honey ran off with hers, laUghing.to Roddy as sL went: "I may use this some day:"</p>
        <p>•-•-• • And here' it -Phis again. "Is that not your recommendation?" Mrs. Netson- Frail asked, seyerely.</p>
        <p>•„. What could Roddy say? He hesitated, and then jerked out "Yes." in a timid way. ' "Then, sir, as you are here, I. will say to you what I was about to write. You told me there was no nonsense about Margaret." "I never saw any nonsense about her," Roddy responded, meeker than ever. ."Perhaps you are of opinion that a girl who comes as a parlourmaid should read Tennyson and George Meredith with the help of gold-rimmed eye-glasses." "I really don't see why she shouldn't." "And lay a napkin for herself in a Silver napkin. ring." • _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>lddy tingled with proud pity. He had given Honey that napkin-ring years before. "And when she is offered her wages declines to have them, and wants me to send them to 'the Hospital for Children in Great Ormond-street." "Did she really? Dear little woman!" _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Netson-frail stared suspiciously at her visitor. Her voice was harder when she broke the awkward silence.</p>
        <p>"I think it is as well that Margaret is leaving to-night. If she did not need to earn a living, why did she come masquerading into a lady's house? And I don't think much of gentlemen who use affectionate terms in referring to their female servants, and write false references when they want to be rid of them."</p>
        <p>"It was not a false reference," Roddy retorted, hotly. "You were more fortunate than Honey if you had her as your parlourmaid." _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Netson-Prail rose to ring the bell as she replied: "Margaret did her work excellently, but I decline to be burdened with girls who want me to send their wages to a hospital, and use silver napkin-rings. • Good evening." Roddy failed to catch a glimpse of Honey on his way through the hall. She was to leave directly. Mr. Penburst must have sent a telegram for her, as she said, and buoy might leave at any moment. He would see it out, Roddy determined, however long he might have to watch the door from his place on the dark pavement. The hour's wait seemed interminable to Roddy, but at last a cab drove up. Two boxes and a bag were piled on the top by a porter; then Honey came out. He heard her give the direction, "Benmare, Lotoe Avenue." The house-door closed behind her; the cabby arranged his wraps and raised his whip. All was ready for a start, when Roddy, springing forward, shouted: "Not that address, ca oy," and, quickly giving new instructions, opened the door, and sprang in beside Honey. The girl gazed at him in absolute silence, then, raising her hands to her face, burst into tears. Roddy took her hand tenderly. "Honey." Sobs were the only reply. "Honey, I was unjust to you the night before you went away. I ask your pardon." "You were more than unjust; you were brutal." The girl raised her head as she spoke. • By the light of the lamps they passed Roddy could see her attitude of picturesque defiance. • "Yes, I Was brutal. You must forgive me and come home with me now."</p>
        <p>"No, no! Why did you interfere with me? I want to go away. where no one will ever see me again." "I cannot let you do that. Honey. Our home is like a grave without you. The girls are longing for you. I—l am longing for y,i?u, Honey." "Yes, I understand. Yott alwa:ys said I made the house so comfortable. You miss the comforts, I suppose. Surely you can advertise for someone who will give you these!" "You can be bitter when you like, Honey." "And you blind." A light came into Roddy's eyes as he held the trembling hand. Had there been a great omission somewhere, and was it his doing? Quick as the wind he formed his plan. "Blind to what, dear? To your love for me? Tell me, Honey; is it that I was slow to speak? Then hear me speak now, Honey. I loved you all the time. You were a girl when you.came to me, Honey, and grew into the house, so that I didn't understand. No, I didn't understand till you left us, and then I knew what you were to Me. I was so many years older, you know, that I thought it was unfair to speak to you; but I will speak now if you will let me. Will you marry me, Honey? Come home with me and be my wife."</p>
        <p>There was a heaving of the breast, a catching of the breath.</p>
        <p>"Quick, darling," he urged, "we are almost there. Answer me. Was that the wound, and will you let me heal it" _ _</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes, yes, yes. I thought you had wearied of me, Roddy, and it broke my heart. I never thought of the years between us. I only wanted your love." "And you have it, darling," Roddy whispered, drawing her to him and kissing her lips, "my love and my loyalty to the end." The door of the cab was pulled open before the last words were spoken, and the lovely truant, silenced by her new joy, was led gently along the garden path to where the ruddy hall-lamp gleamed her welcome home. What a welcome it was! What cries of delight and wonder from the young folks when they heard that Aunt Honey was to be their mother! What plans for the wedding, and scornful rejection of any need for Honey to be married from another place! "This is ,your home, Honey," Roddy declared tesolutely. 'And you shall be married from here." The excitement was at its height when Honey cried out: "Oh, Roddy, you've made me behave disgracefully! I'm engaged to Mrs. Penhurst, and she's expecting me now What am Ito do?" This difficulty was met by a telegram intimating that Mrs. Penhurst might expect a letter from Mr. Strang next mcirning, and latef in the night the letter was concocted, explaining that circumstances had arisen which would make it unnecessary for Miss Honey Dree to take a parlourmaid's position. Miss Honey Dree would, in fact, soon have a house of her own to manage as the wife of Mr. Roderick Strang. She had none the less applied for the position in perfect sincerity, and if she had gone to Mrs. Penhurst as a parlourmaid she would have done her work just as faithfully and thoroughly as she would do the more congenial work now before her. Further, that had it been necessary for Miss Honey Dree to go as parlourmaid, she could not have wished for a mistress whom she felt to be so kind and considerate as Mrs. Penhurst, nor for a master so sympathetic as Mr. Penhurst. In due course the pleasantest of replies came to this com- munication. Honey claimed it as a rightful posses- sion, aiming the severe reproach at Roddy that his un- necessary interference had deprived her of the chance of becoming the happiest parlourmaid in London.</p>
        <p>Sunny days went on towards the wedding, whiel; was quietly celebrated. The change in his lin. seemed to make a new man of Roddy. Every day saw him younger in looks and clearer-eyed. for Honey, she was steeped in radiance. Nor wa his marriage the only change in Roddy's life. The aged uncle whose heir he was, and who had steadily declined to see him during his lifetime, died at last, leaving Roddy in the happy possession of a handsome country house, with an income oi many thousands a year.</p>
        <p>Some months after the events that have been related, Roddy Strang was driving home from the railway station of the little Sussex town within a few miles of which his residence lay. The carriage had been sent, as he hati expected some friends to accompany him from town; but they disappointed him, so Roddy was its solitary occupant. As he approached the lodge gate he saw MO- something had happened. An empty dog-cart was pulled up at the side of the road. Someone was helping a lady through the gateway, And as he came nearer he saw them pass into the lodge. "What's wrong?" he cried, jumping from the carriage, and viewing with anxiety -the broken shaft of the dog-cart and the trembling horse, reinej to a tree stump. The lodge-keeper came running out at the soun,l of the voice, curtesying. "The lady was thrown from the dog-cart, sir, and I asked the gentleman to take her indoors." "It isn't much," a big man in tweed knicker- bockers struck in. "The horse shied at a motor car that went flying past. but the trap fell on the bank. My wife i 3 scratched a bit, but it's more fright that, hurt. I don't know how we're to get back to the hotel with that broken shaft." *lf iinnft 'you won't thank of it." Roddy relined</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>(Copyright.) THE PARLOURMAID.</head>
        <p>(Copyright.) THE PARLOURMAID.</p>
        <p>Bv HUGH S. MACLAUCHLAN, Author of "Providence Hansard," "Tried by Fire,'</p>
        <p>"E ve's. Dowry," &amp;c,</p>
        <p>"A gentleman, sir, to see you." Roddy Strang rose reluctantly from the big study chair in which he had been d ' czing and lifting a card from the tray. read: "Mr. H. M. Penhurst, Benmare, Lotos-avenue, W."</p>
        <p>"Never heard of 'him," he reflected; and then; learning that the visitor was in the drawing-room, he shook off the dregs of drowsiness and marched away for the interview. What there was of Roddy Strang was middle-sized and thin, with a spareness of hair that hinted at more than forty years. There were about him the suggestions of an athletic training. He carried his coat well, and seemed fairly satisfied with himself. A close observer might have detected indications of nervous trouble about the eyes and mouth. The fingers had moved restlessly when he handled the card, which might have been due to the inter- ruption of his rest. There was nothing about his prospects to make him nervous. Himself the pos- sessor of a comfortable income, with the certainty of succeeding to a very much larger one, Roddy Strang had many reasons to view the world with a smiling face. "Mr. Penhurst ?"</p>
        <p>An elderly gentleman rose and bowed</p>
        <p>"I have called," he explained, "to make some inquiries into the character of Honey Dree, who was for some time in your service." "Honey Dree!", Roddy repeated the name with startled emphasis, opening his eyes upon his visitor.</p>
        <p>"Yes, that is the name she gave. She mentioned you as a reference, and so I have called." "Mentioned me as a reference! A reference for what?"</p>
        <p>"She has applied for the post of parlourmaid," the visitor went on. "She said she had been with her last mistress only three months, and my wife wanted to know more of her; so the young woman gave your name, buc with some reluctance. Is it the case that she was in your service ?" "She lived here for many years." Roddy spoke in a dazed way, as if he were thinking aloud; but Mr. Penhurst, with head bent over a list of questions, did not notice the abstraction.</p>
        <p>"Then you will of course know everything about her. Can you recommend her for the position of parlourmaicl ?" _</p>
        <p>"Yes," she would make an excellent parlour- maid," came in a choked voice from Roddy Strang. "Is she clean in her habits ? "</p>
        <p>Roddy's face twitched. "Yes," he replied, this time more energetically, with a touch of defiance.</p>
        <p>"And sober ?.6</p>
        <p>"Sober! Yes, I believe she is sober." The words fell bitterly from Roddy Strang's lips, but a glance at the visitor's face—its gentleness, its goodness— dismissed the feeling of resentment almost before it had shewn itself. "I must put up with this," he felt. "Honey has brought it on herself." "Is she honest ? "</p>
        <p>"Honest! " Roddy half rose from his chair, but some unexpressed feeling drove him down again. He answered almost meekly: "Oh, yes, she is honest," repeating the assurance in a rambling way when he found the visitor's eyes fixed curiously on his own.</p>
        <p>"I fear my questions are distressing you ? " "No. no! Go on. If Honey wants to become your parlournaaid, you must put these questions." "May I ask what position she occupied here ? " "She was not my parlourmaid." "I feir this is a fall in position for her?"</p>
        <p>Roddy bowed. "My wife told me that she spoke like a well- educated person, and that her manners were refined." "I am glad Mrs. Penhurst thought so," Roddy replied, with a quiver of half-amused gratitude in his voice.</p>
        <p>"And you think we may safely give her the place?" "Quite safely, if she will accept it." Mr. Penhurst rose to go. Roddy went with him te the door, his face as expressionless as stone. He spoke and looked as if unconscious of what was happening. Only-for one woment did his actual self return. Mr. Penhurst had already reached the gate when Roddy, running down to where he stood struggling with a rebellious latch, whispered in earnest tones :</p>
        <p>"Honey will never go as your parlourmaid. She is an accomplished girl. Mr. Penhurst looked gravely back. "She has applied for the position. She wants to come to-night, and I shall wire for her now." "Then I can't help it. But don't forget that she has been accustomed to gentle treatment." Mr. Penhurst turned to go with a look of kind assurance. "I think she will have gentle treatment from us."</p>
        <p>And so, with a dreamy hand-shake, the two men parted. When the visitor . had gone, Roddy shut himself into his stwly. and sank into the big chair. He tried to clear his head. What had happened? Some- one had been there, telling him that Honey wanted to be a parlourmaid. and, asking for her character. What were the things he wanted to know? Was she sober, and clean, and honest? • Yes, someone had asked that of Honey, the Honey who was as dear to his girls as life, and he had answered like a machine, as if he had been Honey's master, and not one of the parties to a life-long friendship, with the beginnings of something more. Stay. it was her own fault. Why did she leave him, and what did it matter to him if she went as a parlourmaid, or even as a kitchen-maid? It was an insult to him to send any gentleman on such an errand. He would let her go, and see how she liked it. As he made this resolution, Roddy rose and went into. the drawing-room. His eldest daughter was there, singing to her own accompaniment. He crossed the room to where a big portrait stood in a bronze frame, and lifting it, took it with him into the study. It was a group—two girls and a boy twin- ing round• the central figure of a woman, a dark woman, with lustrous eyes and cluster- ing hair. Not much .else could be seen of her, for the younger people obscured her form; but her eyes seemed to light up the picture. They were eyes to study, and marvel at, and remember. Mr. Penhurst's card lay on the table, where Roddy had placed it. Roddy picked it up abstract- edly and turned it over. Some words were written on the back. Mr. Penhurst must have made a note on the card and presented it ,by mistake. The words were: "Honey Dree. Refer Mrs.'Netson-Prail, Mingary Mansions, Chelsea, S.W." "I'm an ass," cried Roddy, jumping from his seat. "To think that I .never asked *here Honey was! I'll drive there at once and see what she means by all this tomfoolery." . Roddy acted with nervous haste on the sudden resolution. He made a rush for his coat and hat, and before his daughter had time to make an inquiry had hailed a hansom and was rattling along towards Chelsea.</p>
        <p>His thoughts went back to the day when Honey came to him, eleven years ago, a girl of seventeen. Honey was a distant relative of his wife's, the only one she had, and when his wife died two years later, Honey took up her duties as if she were born for them. The children called her aunt; but she Was not their aunt, only Honey. And such a home she made! Everything sweet and orderly, with no extravagance. What wonder was it that the chil- dren looked to her for everything, or that Roddy lost every touch of anxiety about home affairs ?Then there came times when Roddy looked at Honey with other eyes than those of a relation by marriage, and he recalled the many changes this, new attitude had made in the girl. At first she had not concealed her pleasure at the tender turn he gave to an occasional remark. These were infrequent, and the time came when she would shrink into silence when she heard them and go to her room, 'where one of the girls would find her an hour later staring out of the window into the darkness with dry eyes. One day, only, three months before, he had resented her absence from dinner and taunted her with losing interest in his house. She must have some other attractions on which to expend her pleasantness, he said. When he returned from business on the following afternoon, his daughter met him at the door with a white face. "Honey left this for you," she said, handing him a letter. She packed up all her things this morn- ing and drove away in a cab." Roddy tore open the note with a sinking heart. It bore the words: "I will make your house unhappy no longer. Please don't follow me or try to interiere with my arranges ments. Good. bye." The hansom pulled up with a jerk, rousing Roddy from his reverie, lie jumped out and soon found Mrs. Netson-Prall's flat.</p>
        <p>The door opened, and Honey stood there, dressed in cap and apron. Honey faltered when she saw Roddy, but only for a moment, although her steady voice was betrayed by her changing face. "Do you wish to see Mrs. Netson-Prail ?" she asked haughtily, without a sign of recognition. Roddy flushed with annoyance. "Yes," he re- plied, and Honey took in his card, then led him along the passage, saying more gently as she went: • "Why did you ere 4' Leave things as they are." "But you are going away?" "Yes, directly." • Then the door opened, and Roddy faced Mrs. Netson-Prail, a tall, pleasant-looking woman. She rose to receive him With his card in her hand. -</p>
        <p>"Have you come to see-why Margaret is leaving ?" she demanded, in an unconsciously imperative voice. "Margaret !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>"You must have toe lady removed to my house at once, and my men will look after the trap." "I am sure my wife will be very much obliged to you for your kindness," the big man said, grate- fully, and with that they passed into the lodge, where a tall, handsome woman, looking white and ruffled! sat in the arm-chair. •</p>
        <p>_ • "This gentleman wishes to drive you up to his house in his carria,,e,•my dear." The lady turned' smilingly to acknowledge the attention, checked herself for a moment with a puzzled look, then cried: "Mr. Strang, is it not?" At the sound of her voice Roddy was back in the flat in Mingary Mansions, on the day that Honey left Mrs. Netson-Prail. The cap and apron flashed before him,. and the angry eyes of the indignant mistress. For a moment he regretted his hasty offer of hospitality, but as he did so the precious memory of that drive home came to his rescue. Honey rested in his arms again, telling her sweet secret, and with the coming of that vision every thought of littleness vanished like air. "Mrs. Netson-Prail ! lam glad to have been at hand when this happened. You must come up to my house at once. My wife is a splendid nurse. I know she won't let you leave until you are quite recovered."</p>
        <p>This unexpected meeting soon restored the colour to Mrs. Netson-Prail's face. There was little sign of suffering about her as she was handed into the carriage, and viewed, with admiring eyes, the prospects in the grounds, and the picturesque front of the gabled house, as it opened out to her on the crown of a slope of velvet grass. "What a delightful place !" she cried, beaming. "I am so glad the trap was upset. It is all so kind of you." •</p>
        <p>She suddenly paused, as if struck by the recollec- tion of an unpleasant circumstance. Looking anxiously at Roddy, "I hope you did not think me rude about the parlour-maid?" she asked. " "Not at all. You were only sensible." "It was trying, you know. And she was such a splendid girl. She's spoilt me for everybody else." Roddy laughed, and Mr. Netson-Prail, who had hardly recovered from his surprise, made his first remark.</p>
        <p>"She raves about Margaret. I never saw the girl. Lady Margaret she calls her." "Where is Margaret now?" "Oh, she's married," "Delighted to hear it. A good marriage, I hope ?"</p>
        <p>"She liasn't done badly," -Roddy replied, handing his unexpected guest out of the carriage. "My wife will tell you more about it when we get insiae.". To Roddy's disappointment, his wife was out. She had been (slled away to see a sick child in the village at the her end of the park drive, and few home duties were strong enough to make her resist an appeal like that. The housekeeper was full of suggestions as she led the way upstairs, while Roddy, having carried out his further intention to send a cart to the hotel for the luggage, saw Mr. Netson-Prail comfortably settled in ins den•with a whiskey, and soda and a cigar, and scribbled a note to his wife telling her of the circumstances that necessitaftl her immediate return. _ _ _</p>
        <p>After a brief rest, and the elementary attentions of Mrs. Strang's maid, Mrs. Netson-Prail found her way to the drawing-room, where she stood at the long window, watching the dying day. The scene was peaceful beyond words. The great trees were settling under the wrap of twilight. A cluster of fading gleams of sunset clung to the rim of the sky, growing fainter as she watched them., There was no sound of wind, only the good-night calls of nesting birds, the rumble of a distant train, and then the sound of horse-hoofs on a smooth road. A trap pulled up at the porch, which Mrs. Netson- Prail commanded from a side window. She saw a woman fling the reins aside and step lightly to the ground. Mrs. Strang, no doubt. What an active person she seemed to be! 'And she thinks I'm injured," Mrs. Netson-Prail reflected. "Well, there are some scratches. I had better look interesting." With that she 'settled down on a couch beside the fire. _</p>
        <p>She did not hear the door open and a soft foot move across the carpet She heard nothing until a gentle voice sounded beside her. "Mrs. Netson- Prail!" and a happy face looked down upon her with a welcoming, anxious smile. "Margaret!" The word burst from Mrs. Netson-Prail's lips as she still sat, amazed and half erect, upon the couch. Honey—for it was she—broke into a soft peal of laughter, which died into her old smile when •Mrs. Netson-Prail, struggling to rise, cried: "Oh, what have I said? Are you Mrs. Strang?" "Yes," Honey replied, "but never mind. You shall call me Margaret if you want to. You never said it unkindly to me, only to Roddy, and he ,was such a goose that night that he deserved it. Now you must lie still," she added, as Mrs. Netson-Prail made another attempt to rise. "My husband has placed me in medical charge of you, and I mean to be very strict." Mrs. Netson-Prail had not yet found words. She lay as if stunned with astonishment, while Honey took calm possession of her, examining the scratches on her face and hands without resistance, and quietly sending the maid for her special remedy. "I think you can go down to dinner," she re- marked, with the soft laugh. "I can't tell you how glad lam to see you here, and Roddy is glad too."-</p>
        <p>"And to think that I called you Margaret, and that you are the mistress of a house like this. and —and--that I sent you away for being a lady!" The tears rose to• Mrs. Netson-Prail's eyes as she spoke, but Honey's soft hand was on her lips before they dropped. "I won't listen to it! " she cried. "You did quite right. I must have been an intolerable nuisance. Roddy thinks so. Don't you, Roddy?" she added, as the door opened, and her husband came in, lead- ing Mr. Netson-Prail. "My dear," said Mrs. Netson-Prail, rising from the couch and moving to*ards her husband, "there's such a surprise. Mrs. Strang and I are old friends. This is Mrs. Strang." The blush of radiance deepened on Honey's face as she stepped forward with extended hand. "Don't believe it," she said, with a merry tremble in her voice. "I'm only Margaret."</p>
        <p>Criix END.]</p>
        <p>CAMBRIDGE LOCAL EXAMINATIONS._ Forms of entry for the Higher, Senior, Junior, and Preliminary Local Examinations to be held in December next can now be obtained from the local secretaries at the several centres. The examinations will commence on Monday, December 12th. The forms of entry for the Senior, Junior, and Preliminary Local Examinations are to be returned to the local secretaries or' or before October 3rd; those for the Higher Local Examination on or before October 31st. The regulations for the examina- tions may be obtained from the local secretaries at the centres of examination or from Dr. KEYNES, Syndicate Buildings, Cambridge. 13th Sept., 1904.</p>
      </div>
