      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FORTHCOMING ROYAL DIARRIAGIS.</head>
        <p>FORTHCOMING ROYAL DIARRIAGIS.</p>
        <p>The whole of Germany from the Kaiser downwards is full of delight at the engage- ment of the 'Crown Prince to the Grand Duchess Cecilie . of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, which was announced last week. The future Kaiser, who is now only twenty-two years of age, has in the past proved himself mercurial in temperament and very impressionable with the fair sex, and the Americans were quite under the impression that the Kaiserin-to-be was coming from the States. That they had good reason for their hopes must be granted, as at the feet of at least three American ladies the Crown Prince has laid his heart. It -is a matter for congratulation that none of these ladies encouraged him, as he is second only to the Kaiser in impetuosity and strong-headedness and consequently does not always get on as well as might be with his imperial father. A royal marriage always has its political bearings, and in this case they are eminently satisfactory. The Grand Duchess Cecilie, in addition to being an heiress, is a thoroughly German princess, which has given great pleasure in Germany and will help to bind the various factionary elements together, especially as her brother, the Grand Duke of Mecklenburg Schwerin, recently married Princess Alexandra of Cum- berland. Thus it is hoped that all traces of the Hanoverian dispute may be merged in the union of the families by these new ties. Again, the Crown Prince's fiancee is Russian upon her mother's side, and this suits the present German policy admirably. The Grand Duchess Cecilie is tall and pretty with brown hair, a fair complexion, and a face full of fun. She is only just eighteen and has not yet entirely lost her reputation of being a "tom-boy." She is one of those bright, merry personalities which make themselves loved wherever they go. The Prince, too, is very popular and a keen soldier, and there are signs that, despite weaknesses, he has the makings of a very strong man. It is said in Germany that the wedding will take place on the iBth of next January, which is the anniversary of the foundation of Prussia and the reconstitution of the German Empire. The ceremony will take place at Mecklenburg-Schwerin, and then the Crown Prince and Princess will enter Berlin in bridal state.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BECK INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>THE BECK INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>It is undoubtedly a matter for nationa: congratulation that the Home Secretary aftei an inexplicable delay has granted a specia: commission to .sit on the case of Mr. Adol: Beck. The object of this commission .is tc discover who is to blame for his wrongful convictions in 1896 and 1904,</p>
        <p>for his innocence</p>
        <p>has been proved</p>
        <p>in every respect.</p>
        <p>. . That someone must have known that he</p>
        <p>was not Joht</p>
        <p>Smith of</p>
        <p>1877 conviction</p>
        <p>is Obvious to the merest child</p>
        <p>who reads the</p>
        <p>evidence, an d the result is that the Beck case,</p>
        <p>of which now the whole world knows the de-</p>
        <p>tails, is a blot</p>
        <p>on our national THE MASTER OF THE ROLLS. escutcheon, the more so because we ' have always prided ourselves on our British justice and took it upon ourselves to have an all but open breach with France over the Dreyfus case. Nothing short of a definite apportionment of the blame and suitable punishment will now satisfy the country, which has felt itself stirred to the depths over the matter. That the investigation will be thorough is guaranteed by the fact that the Chairman of the committee is . Sir Richard Henn Collins, the Master of the Rolls, who has the reputation for taking his responsibilities very seriously and being both a conscientious and a strong judge. The son of a Q.C., he himself started practice in 1867 and built up a big connection in Manchester and Liverpool before his fame spread south. In fact he was never very well known to the general public until his elevation to the bench, but he appeared in two very famous cases—Sharp v. Wakefield, which settled the question that there is no freehold vested in the publican's licence, and the Clitheroe abduction ease: He became a judge in 1891, and six years later was made a Lord Justice of Appeal, and in 1900 he succeeded Lord Alverstone as Master of the Rolls. With him are Sir Spencer Walpole, K.C.8., and Sir. John Edge, K.C. That the committee only consists of three members is by no means a matter for regret.</p>
        <p>•.° \l\ l 111 ' t,/,.. 7: ','.;, paexf..., •,‘'.;•: . • r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WAR 114 ESSEX.</head>
        <p>WAR 114 ESSEX.</p>
        <p>The autumn manceuvres this year have been conducted on entirely new lines and in consequence excited considerable interest all over the country. The object of the plan formed by Sir Neville Lyttleton and the new War Office staff was designed to test not the question of facing an invasion of MAJOR-GEN. WYNNE.</p>
        <p>GENERAL FRENCH.</p>
        <p>MAJOR- GEN. PAGET. GEN. BRUCE HAMILTON. England by a foreign power, but the useful- ness of Mr. Arnold Forster's "striking arm" force which is the great feature of his army System. This invading force under General rr\ench was supposed, to be landing suddenly</p>
        <p>and unexpectedly in a /oreign country and making a dash to seize the capital, and it must be admitted that the results were not very encouraging, as Major-General Wynne, who commanded the Red or defending force, took up such a strong position that General French did not even give him battle, but withdrew and beat a retreat back to the coast. The Blue army embarked last Mon- day at Southampton on ten large transports for an unknown- place on the Essex coast. This proved to be Clacton, which was reached at daybreak on Wednesday, and the landing began at 7.30. The Blue army consisted of iz,ooo men with horses, guns, waggons, and stores under Sir John French with Brigadier- General Eyre Crabbe as his chief of the staff, and Generals- Paget and Bruce Hamilton as his divisional commanders. General Scobell was at the head of the cavalry, and in less than three hours the whole of the troops were landed with astonishing quickness, while by evening everything down to the last waggon and crate was ashore. Then after an armistice, called by the Duke of Connaught; who was in charge of the manceuvres, Sir John French made a characteristic dash on Colchester and found no opposition offered to him. General Wynne was concealing his position most skilfully, and it was ultimately found that he had taken up an unassailable position on the old Roman road from Braintree 'to Witham, making use of the old Roman positions and holding not only the railway, but the coast from Maldon. General French had to own himself foiled, and beat a retreat to Clacton, where he 're-embarked his men at the beginning of this week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>-",' ' ' __limiilllL_ 7, – –</head>
        <p>-",' ' ' __limiilllL_ 7, - -</p>
        <p>THE RETURN OF THE DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>The good ship Discovery, which has been away three years in the Antarctic regions, and has planted the British flag at the fur- thest southern point ever yet touched, reached Spithead at noon on Saturday, and made Portsmouth Harbour later in the day. She was greeted with the utmost enthuciasm by a great crowd, and the town has been feteing</p>
        <p>THE DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>OAPTAIN SCOTT. -</p>
        <p>Captain Scott and his brave crew. After a splendid voyage home they were all in excel- lent condition, and the Discovery can show an almost clean bill of health. But this does not appear, from the statements of Captain Scott and Sir Clement Markham, to be due to the tinned foods on board, which were so bad that they had to be left untouched and fresh seal meat used in their place. This is to be a subject of inquiry, and Captain Scott ascribes the only sligh t outbreak of scurvy to the tinned meat. The ship is full of interest- ing trophies of the voyage. On board are four Siberian dogs, a Semoyede dog, and a white parrot, which are the pets; but more interesting to science are the wonderful speci- mens of animal and vegetable life which have been collected. The collection of "emperor puffins' " eggs, the first ever taken, is one of the chief features, and this hitherto unknown bird used to provide poultry for the crew. The specimens have been taken up to the British Museum under close guard, and emi- nent scientists are inquiring into the details, especially certain fossils, which prove that animals, and, probably, men, used to inhabit the Polar regions. The Discovery is due in London at the end of the week, and the crew may be sure of a warm reception. The King has lost no time in honouring them with a congratulatory message, and orders that a special Polar medal shall be struck, and Cap- tain Scott has been promoted to the rank of captain in the Royal Navy.</p>
