      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DEATH OF LOUIS DEIBLER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF LOUIS DEIBLER.</p>
        <p>A well-known figure in France passes away in the person of Louis Deibler, the famous executioner, who, it is calculated, must have launched very nearly five hundred people info eternity with his guillotine during his long tenure of this gruesome public office. Deibler, who was •• nick- named "M. de Paris," lived With his son in the Rue de Villancourt and was a strange figure at eighty-one years of age, with his wizen- ed face and in- evitable old- fashioned top- hat. Though an</p>
        <p>executionei, the FRANCE'S LATE EXECUTIONER. son of an execu-</p>
        <p>tio ner, and the father of the new exeeutioner, he was an intensely religious man, and would often pray over the dead bodies of his victims. He had one significant peculiarity in his religion—he always took the sacrament with his gloves on 1 Starting life as a carpenter and cabinet maker, Louis Deibler emigrated to Algiers, where in 1858 he became assistant executioner. Five years later he succeeded his father as executioner at Rennes, and when, in 1871, provincial executioners were abolished, he went to Paris to assist, Roch. In 1879 Roch died, and he succeeded him. His first execution in his new capacity was probably his most sensational, as Leprade, a horrible scoundrel who had murdered his relations under the most revolting circum- stances, was so violent on the scaffold that Deibler had to stun him before he could guillotine him.</p>
        <p>.14f,) \\.. • \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</p>
        <p>capital try for Leigh, Johnson kicking a penalty goal. Result—Wakefield, 2 goals 1 try (7 points) ; Leigh, 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
        <p>HUNSLET V. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>At Hunslei, before 15,000 spectators. In the opening half Albert Goldthorpe dropped a fine goaif or Hunslet and Evans scored an, excellent try for Leeds. At half- time Leeds led by a point. Afterwards Everson scored a lucky try. Hunslet played finely after this, and, after an exciting finish,they just emerged victoriqus. Result Hunslet, 2 goals 2 tries 1,10 points) ; Leeds, 1 goal 1 try (5 points). SWINTON v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Played at Swinton, before 10,000 spectators. A fine open game ended in favour of the visitors by a single point. Just before aange of ends Harris scored an unimproved try for the Rangers. Swinton had the best of matters in the second half, but their only score was a goal, dropped by Molynaux, the Rangers being lucky to win. Result—Rangers, 3 points ; Swinton, 2 points.</p>
        <p>(C'opyright.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>HOCKEY.—If one firm of manufacturers more than another keeps abreast of the times in the matter of Implements for Sport, that firm is GEO- G. BussE z &amp; Co., of London. who are leading the way again with their Hockey Clubs and Balls which bear th.ir well-known trade mark. Ask the local Sp,rts Dealer for G.G.B. &amp; Co.•s Special Hockey List. If not procurable apply to 36 &amp; 38 Queen Victoria it., London. Factory—Peckham, S.B. (64)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>A DISAPPOINTING GAME ENDS IN A DRAW. The 6,000 spectators who assembled on the Wilders- pool enclosure on Saturday,to witness the match between Warrington and Halifax did not see as fine an exhibition as the match between these two finalists for the cup last season, gave promise of. Iu the first place both teams were but poorly represented. The visitors were without Morley and Nettleton, and Warrington missed Harris and Fish in the three-quarter line and Boardman and Naylor in the forwards. There was keen disappointment at the absence of Fish in this the first home match, and the spectators had many mis- givings as to the ultimate issue. The teams turned out as follows : _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Warrington—Hallam (captain), back; Brooks, Isher- wood, Dickenson, and Kenyon, three-quarter backs; Hockenhdll and Davies, half backs ; Preston, Shugars, Cook, Belton, Thomas, Jolley, Harmer, and Lunt, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Halifax—Little, back; Wedgwood, Williams, Drum- mond, and Hartley, three-quarter backs ; Joe Riley and Gledhill, half backs; Jack Riley, Bartle, Swinbank, Mallinson, Morton, longhorn, Hammond, and Winskill, forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. J. H. Crompton, Bradford. Touch j udges, Messrs. R. Yeates, Salford, and F. Little, St. Helens. Little kicked off for Halifax against the wind. Belton returning, a scrimmage was formed near the half-way line, and Warrington were penalised. Little made a , splendid attempt at goal, the ball just going outside. From the drop out Warrington took up an aggressive position, but Jack Riley broke away, and Hallam brought off a splendid save. A flying kick by Warring- ton put Hartley in possession, but Dickenson replied with a good kick. Hockenhall secured from the pack, and, kicking across to Dickenson, tackled Wedgwood in possession. Passing by the home backs was mulled by Isherwood. Halifax were having the best of matters, but the home forwards played up strongly. A grand dribble by the Halifax forwards and a mistake by Kenyon almost ended disastrously for Warrington, Hallam kicking dead in the nick of time. A grand touch kick by Dicken- son put Warrington in a better position. From the succeeding scrimmage Hockenhall kicked across,and Brooks rolled Little in touch on the goal .line. A passing bout between Davies, Hockenhall and Kenyon, ended in the latter mulling, and a fine opportunity was lost. Play now became much more keen, and Warring- ton made strenuous efforts to score. Jack Riley was injured in a tackle, and had to be carried off the field. Halifax were penalised, and Preston tried for goal, but the ball was short. Little put in a ponderous kick. landing play at half-way. Wedgwood saved nicely, catching the ball near touch. Davies kicked acrosq,and Wedgwood was pushed in touch by Dickenson near the Halifax line. Halifax assailed the Warrington line, but Hockenhall brought relief with a nice kick. Passing between Davies, Hockenhall, Dickenson, Isherwood,and Kenyon looked dangerous until Little threaded his way through several Warrington players and brought relief. Half-time :—Warrington nil, Halifax, nil. Dickenson restarted and for a time Warrington prest3d, and Brookes almost got over. After a passing bout Lunt and Hockenhall also were grounded on the line, Joe Riley intercepted a pass on the line and gave Halifax great relief. Langhorn made a grand run, fully deserving a score. Dickenson however, was Warring- ton's saviour by a smart tackle, and shortly afterwards he followed this by a long kick in touch. Passing between Hockenhall and Kenyon found Little napping and he was hauled down on the goal line. Good forward play got Halifax out of their dangerous position and Wedgwood and Drummond worked the ball down the field. The ball rolled over the line and Hallam touched dead. Dickenson's drop out foand touch at centre and for a time play was very even. Gledhill made a vain attempt to break away Hockenhall grassing him. Dickenson tried for goal from a penalty but the ball went wide. Isherwood adopted Association tactics by ,e heading the ball. Both sides strove hard to score b without success and time was called with a blank score sheet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>BATLEY V. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>BATLEY V. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Batley opened the season on the home ground by de- feating Runcorn in somewhat easy fashion, the visitors failing to do more than register a penalty goal. Pressure by them, however, was almost continuous, and on two or three occasions Batley's defence was all but pierced by Jolley and J. Richardson. The tables were turned on the change of ends, and very quickly William Davies opened Batley's account by scoring in the right hand corner, but •Hainsworth failed to add the extra points. Then Turton drlpped a goal, and this was followed by a second try to Davies, Hainsworth again missing the goal. Runcorn after this reached the home quarter-line, but the Batley for- wards rushed the leather down the field, and from a pack Oakland scored the finest try of the day, planting the ball between the posts. Turton kicked a goal. A minute before the conclusion of play, Goodall dropped a Foal, and thus left Batley winners by 3 goals and 3 tries (15 points) to Runcorn 1 penalty goal (2 points). Teams Batley—F.Fozard, back ' • W. T. Davies, J. B. Good- all, R. P. Johnson, and L. Turton, three-quarter backs ; J. Oakland and W. Hogg, half backs; .1. H. Phillips, F. Swallow, J. Furniss, P. Judge. W. Wolstenholme, K. Hainsworth, J. Wade, and A. Kitson, forwards. Runcorn—S. Houghton back ; Jones, Hesketh,Warder, and Inglesileld, three-quarter backs;Jolly and j.Richard- son, half backs; Walker, Moulton, A.Richardson,Dixon, Padbury, Dobson, Higgins, and C. Houghton, forwards. Referee, Mr. Gibson, Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. H. Sewell, Leeds, and A. Fieldhouse, Holbeck.</p>
        <p>WIDNES v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 3,000 spectators. The stat was delayed by the late arrival of the Bradford team. Widnes won the toss, and Marsden started for Bradford against a strong wind, and a kick by the same player took play to the Widnes line, where the visitors were penalised, and Tilley cleared to the centre. From a scrummage at the Bradford quarter flag, the ball was passed to Hughes, and dribblina it over the line he scored. Tilley failed at the goal kick. Following the kick-out a scrummage was formed at the centre, and passing between Brea.; Dunbavin, Feather and Laidlaw, ended in the latter scoring near the posts. Mosby failed at the goal kick. Widnes now played with great determination, and from a pass by Kennedy Ike Taylor scored. Tilley again failed at goal. Then Dunbavin dashed along the line, and kicking across, Connell scored at the corner. Mosby failed at the place. Half- time :—Widnes, 2 tries (6 points), Bradford 2 tries (6 points.) In the second half, with the wind in their fa.vonr, Bradford troubled the Widnes defence, but it proved equal to the task. Ragers looked like getting over on one occasion, but he missed the ball when close to the line. and Aspey cleared. Just before time Widnes were penalised in front of goal,and Mosby attempted a place. The ball struck the upright, and bounced back into the field of play, and Leyston cleared. Result :—Widnes 2 tries (6 points), Bradford 2 tries (6 points). Teams :</p>
        <p>Bradford—Gunn, back • Connell, Mosby, Eagers' and Dunbavin, three-quarter backs; Marsdeti and Brear,half- backs ; Laidlaw, Hutt, Feather, Turner, Barker, Green- wood, Westmoreland, and Francis, forwards._ _</p>
        <p>Wines—Tilley, back; Barber, 'Peacock, Taylor, and Hughes, three-quarter backs; Howard and Kennedy, half backs; Aspey, Argent, Curtis, Cockram, Leyston, M'Cabe, Rigby, and Webster, forwards. Referee, Mr. G. Fowler, Leeds. Touch judges, Mr. Houghton, St. Helens, and Mr. H. Lowe, Broughton Rangers. ST. HELENS v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Played at St. Helens. In the first half Wigan, playing with the wind, had much the better of play. A goal was kicked by Vickers, and afterwards\tries were scored by Collier, Carey, and Jones. In the second half St. Helens strove hard, and Rothwell scored for them, Jones kicking a goal. Result :—Wigan, 1 goal 3 tries ; St. Helens, 2 goals 1 try. SALFORD V. HULL.</p>
        <p>Played at Weaste, Salford, in fine weather, before 5,000 people. Salford started, and Norris and Bedford scored tries. Lomas kicked a goal for Hull and Jackson landed two penalty goals. On resuming Lewis scored for Salford, as also did Haton for Hull. The game was evenly contested. Result :—Salford 2 goals 3 tries (13 points) ; Hull, 2 goals 1 try (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WAKEFIELD TRINITY V. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>WAKEFIELD TRINITY V. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>• At Wakefield before 5,000 spectators. The game was of a fast and exciting character, Wakefield 'winning on the call of time. For the winners Metcalfe kicked two goals, while Dixon scored a try. Ganley scored a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 8,000 spectators. Early on Gordon placed a penalty goal to r Hull, and Ferguson equalised, this being the whole of the scoring in the first half. The second half was most, stubbornly contested, but had the Oldham forwards not overran the ball they must have s cored. Owen 'at last crossed, Fetguson failing. Result—Oldham, 1 goal 1 try (5 points) Rovers, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>REVISING THE LISTS.</head>
        <p>REVISING THE LISTS.</p>
        <p>Concluding Proceedings at Warrington.</p>
        <p>A COMPLIMENT FOR THE ASSISTANT OVERSEER.</p>
        <p>The revision of the voters' list at Warrington was completed on Saturday. At the conclusion of the buslnes3, Mr. William Holbrook, Conservative agent, observed that on behalf of the Conservative party he wished to thank his Honour very much for the courtesy he had shown tbem , and, at the same time, to express the greatest satisfac- tion at the way the list had been prepared. In Mr. W. H. Woodcock, the assistant overseer, and his excellent staff they had men capable of doing the work thoroughly and well, or they would have had far more objections. In going through the list he found there were only two printer's errors, and two other errors which required correction. Therefore he thought they would all agree that this was the best list they had ever had in the borough, and if he might say so, one of the best in the county. In fact he did not think it could be improved upon anywhere. The number of claims and objeotions was far less than last year—and it was very light then. That was proof of good work in the preparation of the list.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Howes' inn., in seconding the vote of thanks, on behalf of the Liberals, said he wished to express his thanks for the all round courtesy extended to him on that, his firstappearance at the revision court. There had really been very little work for them to do on account of the list being so well prepared. Mr. Foard acknowledged the compliment, and said he was personally very grateful to both sides for the great assistance they had given him. There was no doubt that the list had been steadily improved year by year. He knew, also, the difficulty of printing it, and when they considered the number of names, with their peculiarities, it was surprising that there were so few mistakes. It was almost impossible to conceive a list prepared with more accuracy than this had been. His thanks were, therefere due to Mr. Woodcock for having prepared such a list. As compared with a few years ago, it involved next to no labour to the revising barrister. Mr. W. H. Woodcock, the assistant overseer, in acknowledging the complimentary remarks which had been made about the list, said this was the fifth he had prepared, and as had been said, there had been an improvement each year. He thought it had reached something like the high water mark, as out of a total number of 11,693 names, a total of 263 claims and objec- tions was indeed small, especially considering the fact of Warrington being a manufacturing town with a large moving population, and the present keenness of the political agents. As a comparison he might say that when he took over the work in 1899 the claims and objec- tions numbered 732 in a list 10 per cent. less than it was now. The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SOME POWERFUL PROOF.</head>
        <p>SOME POWERFUL PROOF.</p>
        <p>Of How Bile Bean Cures Last.</p>
        <p>Bile!Beans cure not for a day or a week, but for good. An impartial judge recently selected a number of testi- monials published several months ago—in some cases two years ago—sad wrote to the persons cured, asking if the cure had been permanent. So satisfactory were the replies that they were forwarded to the Bile Bean Co. for inspection. Here are a few of them : Miss E. Noyce, 64, Savona street, Battersea Park Road, London, S.W. writes :—" It is quite true that my mother is cured, and is still well, by taking Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. Nothing did her any good, and the doctor could do no more for her, and then someone told us to try bile beans. I can only say that they made my mother a new woman." (Cured of indigestion. &amp;c.) Eliza Mason of 16, Grafton-street, Grimsby, writes: —"I have to be very thankful for having taken Bile Beans, for I thought I was broken up in health,but they set me right, and I am enjoying better health now than for years. (Cured of headache and debility). Alfred Curtis, Thornford, Sherbourne. writes :—" I thank God I am well, though I will be soon seventy-six years of age. For almost five years doottors just kept me alive, that was all. I coula not lie down in bed for my breath was so bad I could hardly move about. Before I was through my first box of Bile Beans I was up and able to walk in the garden. In three weeks I could walk through the village; the people all wonder- ing at the change in me. lam happy to say I can now walk to Sherbonrne, four miles distant. In assure you it is Bile Beans that have made this change in me. They cured me of biliousness, liver complaint, and palpitation." There were many more to similar effect. Original letters were submitted to a prominent firm of auditors and chartered accountants, who certified correctness of all extracts. A book containing all extracts and copy of auditors' certificate, will be forwarded to all who send post card for same to the Bile Bean Co.'s Leeds Depot. If you suffer from indigestion, headache, liver trouble, biliousness, constipation, piles, or debility, remember that Bile Beans curethese ailments. Obtain- able from chemiSts everywhere at is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box.</p>
