      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FORESEEN:</head>
        <p>FORESEEN:</p>
        <p>AN IRISH STORY.</p>
        <p>BY HOPE STANFORD,</p>
        <p>Author of "Down tile Way," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CHAPTER I.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER I.</p>
        <p>My mother died when I was twelve years old, and my father's sister, Barbara, tame to keep house for us in our quaint country Rectory in Devonshire. A handsome young woman of twenty-three, with pleasant manners and a charming little brogue, is the recollection of her left in the minds of neigh- bours'and parishioners, but in those days I did not criticise, but only loved her. The remembrance of her which I retained most vividly was in connection with Captain Freeman, an officer quartered at Exeter, who drove a tandem, with which-0, thrilling delight !—Aunt Barbara and I went once or twice for a drive, when we thought we could do so without risk of the scolding which our unwise venture would certainly have provoked. Even at that age I was conscious that I was useful to Aunt Barbara. Many a time have I beard her say: "I'll come if Dick can be spared to come with me," when the Captain proposed some tempting expedition, and while, to do her justice, she never once told me not to tell, I quite understood that these pleasures were of the stolen order, and would at once come to an end if I alluded to them in public. Now and then Captain Freeman came to call at the Rectory, and was well received by my father, who was a genial man and hospitable, but took little notice of what went on around him. He was, I imagine, the only person in the neighbourhood who did not confidently reckon upon a match between the Captain and handsome Miss Barbara O'Shane.</p>
        <p>As far as I can recollect things went smoothly for about six months, and then a change came. There were no more meetings in the .country lanes, when I was sent on before to look for birds' nests, no more drives at full speed behind spirited horses, no more idle searches after glow-worms in the evenings. What really happened or with whom the fault began I never knew; but Aunt Barbara grew pale and silent, Captain Freeman's visits ceased, and I felt a very distinct sense of being defrauded when all sorts of stolen delights came to an end. One August evening I was coming through a little copse which joined our garden, when I heard voices a short way off,*and turning a corner I saw Aunt Barbara and Captain Freeman just in front of me. That they had been quarrelling was evident, but they seemed to have come to an end of what they had to say, and I even fancied were glad to see me. "This is all, then, Barbara?" I heard him say as ,I came up. "Yes, that is all, Captain Freeman," she answered, firmly; and without even a last touch of the hand they parted. and went different ways; and although I turned several times to look back, I am sure Aunt Barbara never (lid.</p>
        <p>After that day we saw no more of Captain Free- man, nor was his name ever mentioned in the house, and I soon began to notice a change in my aunt's manner,. T hive no doubt that she scarcely expected her ffrewf 11 to he taken as final, for there vas a watching, 1,-? ening look on her face, which I can plainly recall no".', which never after left her, and by-and-bye people began to remark that Miss O'Shane was altered, and seemed to have aged all at once, and I heard the word "jilted," and went home and looked it out in the dictionary, but did not feel much the wiser.</p>
        <p>Well, soon after that my father died. -and Aunt Barbara went to lire with another brother, my Uncle John, who was agent for a large estate in Ireland; and I went to my mother's relatives, who sent me to school in Germany, and cared so little about me that I only came home for holidays once or twice in several years. Uncle john sent me many invitations to go to Ireland. but it was too far; beside which, people said that Aunt Babrara was rather odd, and that was made an excuse for keep- ing me away from them. Nine years passed, and as there seemed nothing for me to do in England, it was decided that I should go and try my fortune in Australia; but before I went I determined that I would go and see Uncle John and Aunt Barbara. for it would probably be many years before I came back to the old World again. Kilmeadon—the estate to which my uncle was agent—was in a wild and disturbed part of the country, and as rents were hard to get, and the absentee landlord intended to make changes of which Uncle John did not approve, he had decided to give up the agency in a few weeks' time, and settle himself with his sister in a small home of his own near Dublin.. •</p>
        <p>I was prepared to find great changes in Aunt Bar- bara. I expected that instead of the handsome woman whom I had not seen for nine years I should find a faded and fanciful invalid, whom it would be a duty rather than a pleasure to meet, and who would probably be only faintly interested in me; but to my astonishment Aunt Barbara, blooming and handsomrn as ever, rushed out to meet mc on my arrival, and took me to her heart as she had taken me nine years before as a delicate and motherless boy. - "Well, how do you think your aunt is looking, Dick?" asked my uncle, as we took a turn round the garden before dinner; and I hastened to express my surprise and admiration. "Yes; she's a handsome woman, and the hearts of the boys are never their own, as you may say, when she's by; but she hasn't a word for one of 'em in the way of marrying, and to tell the truth it's best so. She's not quite herself, Dick, and I couldn't let her go out of my own keeping." "Not herself, uncle?. I heard that before I came; but she looks as healthy and handsome a woman as you'd wish to meet." "You may say all that and yet there'd be some- thing left to say," he answered. "Mind me, Dick, before you've men in the house many hours she'll tell you something that's going to happen; no mortal soul can tell you how she knows' but it'll come true, you'll see; she sits and listens for knowledge. I've seen hr myself many times go out- side the door and kneel down and put her ear to the ground, and a while after she'll tell me something that must have come to her through some sense we haven't got, and, barring accidents, what she says comes to pass. I shall be glad for her sake when we get away from here. I hear the new agent's appointed, but I don't yet know his name. Poor fellow! I wouldn't give much for his chances if he comes here and airs his English ways." Then my uncle went on to tell me of the dis- ordered state of the country, and the lawlessness of the peasants, and my blood ran cold as he told me of one place after another where men had been shot down without a moment's warning. I watched Aunt Barbara with much interest during the evening. A neighbour or two came in,. and she was in high spirits—a little unnaturally so, I thought—but there was nothing special to remark about her xontil the subject of the new agent was brought forward; then; making an opportunity for coming close to me, de touched my arm,' and put- ting Per finger on her lips said very low: "Hush, Dick! not a word. I'll tell you by-and-bye." Then she turned away again, leaving me completely mystified.</p>
        <p>Uncle John went out to the garden gate with his guests, and called to me to follow; but, looking at Aunt Barbara. she made me a sign to stay behind. "Come here. Dick; come quite close; no one must hear," she said, the moment we were alone. "I have something to tell you—a secret between you and me. Captain Freeman is coming!" I looked at her in surprise. "Captain Freeman?" I repeated, forgetting for the moment to whom her words referred, for it was many years since I had heard his name.</p>
        <p>"Yes, Dick; Captain Freeman. We quarrelled, you know; but he is coming back. I always knew he would." The excitement of her manner and the strange look in her eyes recalled my uncle's words, and convinced me that at leas i: on the subject of her old /over her mind must have lost its balance. Was it for him she had waited and listened through long years Was it thikhope which had kept her happy raid blooming? I could not doubt it, and the fancy was so harmless and pathetic that, if possible, it, heightened the interest I felt in her. Uncle John came back immediately, and neither on that night or the next, day did Aunt Barbara allude to the bubject again. We were sitting smoking in the porch the follow- ing evening. and Aunt Barbara was with us, when a teighbour came in. "I was wanting to see you, O'Shane,:' he said. •I'm rather in a mess about my lease,and as you've nothing more to do with that sort of thing, I want sou to Fay a word to the new agent for me." -</p>
        <p>"I can't do that at present ; even know his name as yet," answered my uncle. "That's awkwarl. I'm in a hurry, and made iure you could help me," said the visitor with ~exation.</p>
        <p>_ While they were speaking I had glanced idly at Aunt Barbara, and was surprised to see that some- -t-ling had moved her deeply: her colour went and time, her hands clasped each other nervously, and ,veral times her lips open 3d to speak, but she efrained. Now, as if she could no longer keep tack her words, she said suddenly: " rne new agent's name is Captain Freeman." We all looked at her in surprise, but remembering ear assertion of last night, mine was the least. "How do you know ? " asked Uncle John, a little anwillingly, I thought. "I cannot tell you," she answered; "but I do know."</p>
        <p>"Of course, you heard it somewhere and forgot to tell me," he said, with assumed earelessnees, and then lie made some excuse and took the visitor away.</p>
        <p>°I never heard it, Dick; no one ever told Bald Aunt Barbara, very low. "But all of a sudden I knew it. I told you last night that he was coming." "The new agent may have the mune name as your old friend, but most likely is a diffezmt man," I said but she interrupted me: "No, Dick; I tell you it is the same!" Then she sat very still, and the listening look came into her face, and we said nothing more until Uncle John came back. He asked me to go for a walk with him, and I was not sorry to get away.</p>
        <p>"I told you what a queer way poor Barbara has of knowing things befoiehand," he said. "It would be strmge if it turns out that she is right about the man."</p>
        <p>"Very strange." I said; but it was evident tiAt as yet he had not canneeted the name with that of her former lover, if he. had ever heard of him, and I thought it best at present not to suggest it.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 11. • The next morning Uncle Joha called me into his office after breakfast.</p>
        <p>"It's a very. queer thing. Dick, but the new agent's name is Freeman. I've had a note from him this morning; he's corning over next week. It would be queerer still if he turned out to be poor Barbara's old lover, which is, I suppose, what she expects. I didn't think of that last night." . 'She says it's the same man," I answered. "She has spoken to you about it then?" "Yes; she told me the first night I came that he was coming. I was with them a great deal in those old days." "I tell you what, Dick, you'll have to take her away with you to Dublin before he comes. Whether he's the same man or not, the meeting is sure to upset her, and she'll say or do something odd." I was quite ready to do as he wished, but before we had time to talk over any arrangements Aunt Barbara came in.</p>
        <p>"You've heard from Captain Freeman, John," she said, confidently. "He's coming soon, I know; does he tell you the day? I shall want good notice, for I wish to do him hoaour; he has been long away." "You are quite right about the name, Barbara,' answered my uncle; "but we do not know anything more about the man, or whether any of us have seen him before."</p>
        <p>She smiled quietly. "It is my old friend, Arthur Freeman," she answered; and somehow I no longer doubted that she was right. Two or three days passed, and nothing more was heard of the new agent, nor was his name mentioned between any of us. Uncle John had proposed to his sister that she and I should precede him to Dublin; but this she had firmly declined, and we had not far to look for the reason. There had been a good deal of additional restlessness in her manner of late, and her spirits and appetite had failed. Sometimes she would sit for an hour at the open window listening intently, and if I spoke would merely raise her hand to silence me, but there was nothing which enabled me to do more than guess her thoughts. Once or twice when I was out alone the neigh- bours talked to me about the new agent, of whom report spoke anything but favourably. He had left the army years beforo and had since led a wandering life, of which apparently he had now tired, and had accepted the offer of a distant relation—Lord Kil- meadon—of the post of agent which my uncle was resigning. That discontent and disorder were rapidly increas- ing in the neighbourhood became every day more evident, and there were several who had been heard to say that "the fine English gentleman had better look out for himself." Uncle John's ways had been easy enough in all conscience, and Lord Kilmeadon's revenues had been small for many years past, but even under his rule there had been murmurs, and he foresaw that it would need a man of stout heart to come after him.</p>
        <p>From what I could gather Captain Freeman had received divers warnings of the state of affairs, but he either disbelieved in the danger, or was reckless of it, and had expressed his intention of going his own way. No day had been fixod for his arrival, much to my disappointment, for my viAit was fast drawing to a close, and I was naturally anxious to see him, having no doubt in my own mind that he would prove to be Aunt Barbara's old lover. We were all sitting together one evening. An acquaintance had come in and was talking to Uncle John a little apart, while Aunt Barbara sari by the open window, her thoughts evide.ntly far away, when she turned round suddenly and called, "John 1" "Yes," he answered, without moving from his place. "John," she went on, speaking loudly and insistently, "Captain Freeman is coming to-morrow night." "How should you know? I have heard: nothing from him," said my uncle, hurriedly. "I cannot tell you any more, but he is coming; he will ride along the north road as it gets dark." It always annoyed my uncle extremely when his sister shewed any eccentricity before strangers, so now he tried to pass the matter over lightly, saying that we must take care to be prepared for him in case he came, and then went back to his talk again. Aunt Barbara turned to Me with a smile. "It is quite true, Dick; he is coming to-morrow night," she said and then the subject dropped. The next morning when I went down to breakfast I noticed a startling change in Aunt Barbara. She had gone to bed looking a little worn, perhaps, but apparently in her usual health. This Morning her face was white and shrunken, her eyes heavy and staring and her hands trembling. Uncle John was in a hurry to keep an appointment 2 and did not seem to notice her, but went out almost as soon as she came into the rcom. Hardly had the door closed behind him when she turned to me.</p>
        <p>"Dick, I did not think I should live until the morning; last night was so awful ! " she said slowly. "I hardly know what it was, I cannot tell you what frightened me, but it was not for myself. I seemed to stretch out my arms all the night long against something vague and terrible,. and I awoke crying Arthur! ' " She stopped for a moment in which I had time to remember that it was her old lover's name. "Dreaming again. for these things were like dreams," she sent on, "the same horror came back. I was out on a road which, I seemed to know, and along the side of it there was a low wall which I vainly tried to build hic,her. Over and over again the stones rolled away from under my hands, and all this time I heard a sound of horse's feet, and it seemed life or death that that wall should be built before it passed, and I could not do it! And awoke. Then I slept and dreamt again, and you were with me Dick, it seemed; but you were a boy again, and we stood out in the wind, and I said: Listen ! ' and you answered that you heard nothing. And I said, Kneel down and put your ear to the ground.' Then you said: A horse is coming!, and I ran forward and cried: Keep back! Keep back!' but the sound died on my lips, and I fell forward on the road." "Do not tell me any more now," I interrupted, drawing her hand through my arm. "Come out into the garden, you are worn out by a bad night." "No: I cannot tell you any more now; as the day goes on it may get clearer in my mind," she answered; and then, as we walked up and down in the fresh morning air, she crew calmer. "You remember that Captain Freeman is coming to-night, John," she said to my uncle when he came in; and he looked from her to me in distress. "Wall, Barbara," he replied at last, "if you think he is coming all you can do is to make ready for him. Inglis and O'Reilly are coming in after dinner, and would be glad to meet him. Late in the afternoon Aunt Barbara called me out to her in the garden. "I can't make it all out, Dick," she said, putting her arm through mine and leaning heavily, as if exhausted. "He is coming; all day long he has been coming. and yet something tells me he will not come. Jclin says: Make ready for him,' but it is no use. I know it is no use, but yet every time I listen I hear his horse's tread. Dick we used to put our ears to the ground to hear 'father' coming, when you were a boy. I have tried that, and he seems nearer then, but still he does not come! Do yoil think, Dick, he never will ? " "I think perhaps he may be here •at any minute, when you are not thinking of him," I answered, as confidently as I could. "And after all, you know, Aunt Barbara, it may be a different man with the same name. • "That is so foolish. Dick! I know it is Arthur Freeman. But although I know, I cannot make teady for him—something stops me, and I cannot understand!" _ _</p>
        <p>Dinner was over. My uncle's friends had arrived and were lighting their pipes, when I went out quietly to look for Aunt Barbara. Her manner had been absent, and she had eaten nothing, and I grew every hour more uneasy about her. It was evident that Uncle John attached no importance to her statement that the new agent *as corning, for he had not once alluded to it, and since the morning the had been equally silent. It was a dull, still evening, and already almost dark, when I went out, expecting to find her at her favourite seat on the lawn; but she was not there, and I wandered down the drive to the gate opening on a road which stretched away for miles, sometimes hidden in a hollow, then climbing over a hill, but still "within knowledge," as one may say, until the didtance was too great to trace it. Aunt Barbara was there as I had expected, but i'n• a moment I stood still in alarm, for she was neither pacing up and down, nor leaning over the gate, but was down on her knees with her ear to the fround, listening for that ghostly footfall which tiad haunted her dreams. I was afraid to disturb.her. and was going back . to the house to call Uncle John and tell him what I had seen, when, as I re .ched tht! door, Aunt Barbara was beside me. She walked straight into the dining- Slom and spoke at once, firmly, but with excite- ment. • “John, he is com;ng along the porth road, as I cold you, but he is a lona way off vet. There is</p>
      </div>
