        <p>Thousands of the world's best peo- ple have found instant relief and speedy cure by the use of Cuticura Soap, Oint- ment-and Pills in the most torturing and disfiguring of Itching, burning and scaly humours, eczemas, rashes, itch- Ings and Inflammations.</p>
        <p>Thousands of tired, fretted mothers, of skin-tortured and disfigured babies, of all ages and conditions, have certi-. fled to almost miraculous cures by the Cuticura remedies, when the best med- ical skill had failed to relieve, much less cure. Cuticura Treatment is local and con- stitutional complete and perfect, pure, sweet and wholesome. Bathe the affected surfaces with Cuticura Soap and hot water to cleanse the skin of crusts and scales and soften the thick- ened cuticle, dry without hard rubbing, and apply Cuticura Ointment freely to allay itching, irritation and inflamma- tion, and soothe and heal, and lastly take Cuticura Resolvent rills to cool and cleanse the blood, and put every function in a state of healthy activity. More great cures of simple, scrofulous and hereditary humours are daily inside by Cuticura remedies than by all other blood and skin remedies combined, a single set being often sufficient to cure the most distressing cases when all else fails.</p>
        <p>Sold throughout the world. Cuticura Resolvent, 2s. 6d. tin form of Chocolate Coated Pills, is. lid. per vial of 60), Ointment, 2s. 6d., Soap, Is. Depots. London, 27 Charter- house Sq.; Paris, 5 Rue de la Pais; Boston, 137 Columbus Ave. Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Sole Props. SOP Send for ** Skin and Blood Purification."</p>
        <p>danger threatening him, and you must ride out and stop him. "0, come, Barbara!" said my uncle, soothingly; "there is some mistake in your mind. You don't know all this, it is only guessing. Anyhow, if he is coming he's in no danger; he'll get here safe enough." "Listen." said Aunt Barbara, coming more fully into the light, and looking away into the distance as if she saw what was hidden from us. "Last night I strove and strove, but I could not build up that low wall by the Green Rock before he came. There is death for 'him there. Ride out, John, and turn him back ! "</p>
        <p>...... Her voice was imperative, and Uncle John took a few hurried steps forward, while one of the guests, supposing himself unseen, significantly touched his forehead.</p>
        <p>"Come, Barbara, Dick and I will go with you into the drawing-room; we can better talk about it there," said my uncle; and with a hasty excuse we went out.</p>
        <p>"You are lettinc, your ideas run away with you, my dear," he said, kindly, but firmly. "I cannot rilie out to the Green Rock; my horse is tired, and Mike has gone home. Captain Freeman would not come at night without notice. You have worried volirself and got fancies into your head. I must go back to the dining-room, but you had better sit here and rest. and let Dick read to you a bit." Aunt Barbara threw up her hands in despair. "His blood is on your head!' she criel. "Oh, why have I wasted these precious minutes when I might have run to save him? Too late! Too late!" She sank back in her chair and closed her eyes. "She has, never been so bad as this before, Dick. I'll send away Inglis and O'Reilly and come back to her, and then if you will you had better fetch the doctor," said my uncle, anxiously. I went willingly, but it was a long way, and it was more than an hour before Dr. Bryan and I got back. When we came near my uncle's gate we both stopped suddenly in our brisk walk, for there, in the middle of the drive, on the same spot where I had found Aunt Barbara before, we saw her again, down on her knees with her ear to the ground—listening. To run forward and raise her was the work of a morn.nt, but she fell back in my arms. "Too late, Dick! Too late! He will not pass the Green Reck ! Arthur! Arthur!" she cried, and I believe that it was in that cry that her life passed away.</p>
        <p>"Dick," said my uncle an hour later, "Bryan and I are going to the Green Rock to see if by any chance her words are true. Will you come?" We went silently together in the moonlight. The distance was three miles at the least, and along as lonely a road as you could find. The tightening of my uncle's arm in mine told me when we were near- in.; the spot, and suddenly from behind a steep bank there came towards us a riderless horse. "She knew ! " we exclaimed in a breath, and a few yards further on we found Captain Freeman lying by the roadside with a bullet through his head. [THE E.]</p>
        <p>FLAT SUNK IN THE MERSEY. On Monday the flat Lark, belonging to Mrs. Owens, of Birkenhead, was being rowed up the Mersey to Runcorn when it sank below Hale Head. The captain (John Hampson), his wife, and the !land were thrown in the water, and were rescued with difficulty.</p>
