      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SIiPTESIBER 17th, 1904.</head>
        <p>SIiPTESIBER 17th, 1904.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>TH E disintegration "MANLY STRA.IGHT- of the Unionist party FORWARDNESS." goes on apace. It</p>
        <p>and hundreds of thousands besides Unionists; and to his policy I intend to adhere as the only one which will retain the greatness of the Empire." Mr. BELL calls upon Unionists to study Lord BEACONSFIELD'S "speeches and writings." Then they will see how far their present leaders have strayed from the path of rectitudo. "If our party are to retain the confidence of the nation, the leaders of that party must return to that policy from which they have departed—namely of manly straightforwardness." DISRAELI has been extolled for his courage and astuteness ; but the particular virtue which moves the eloquence of Mr. BEI&amp; does not usually figure in eulogies of the" Asian mystery." It is pleasing to think of the Unionist party seeking a remedy for :their growing infirmities by searching DISRAELI'S speeches and writings for the healing balm of "manly straightforwardness." Mr. BALFOUR, we fear, gives little of the time he can spare from the golf links to this instructive pursuit. On one occasion in the House of Commons, when the memory of DISRAELI was cited for his enlighten- ment Mr. :BALFOUR curtly remarked, "Lord BEACONSFIELD is dead."</p>
        <p>It is not the absence of "manly straight- forwardness" from the Fiscal policy of the Government that troubles the soul of Mr. BELL He is alarmed by their Irish policy. He fears they are about to knuckle down to Lord DUNRAVEN. "Are the Unionist Government going to give Lord DUNRAVEN'S 'Devolution' (a sugar-coated name for Home Rule) to Ireland, or are th3y not? When that same nobleman formed a committee to discuss land purchase we were told it was an irresponsible committee; but within a few months after, the Government brought in and passed into law a bill embodying the recommendations of that com- mittee. Are we sure they will not do the same in this case ?" Mr. BELL'S studies of DISRAELI'S career ought to supply him with a ready answer to this question. What statesman was it who op- posed, as revolutionary and contrary to sound Conservative principles, the Liberal Reform Bill of 1866, and then passed household suffrage in the following year? This was one of DISRAELI'S most famous exercises in " manly straightforwardness " ; and yet, with a strange blindness, Lord SALISBURY denounced it as the betrayal of the Tory party. Of course, Mr. WYNDHAM does not:dream of betraying Mr. BELL. He is only following the natural course of evolu- tion in the Irish policy of both parties. Had Lord BEACONSFIELD lived long enough, he would pro- bably have blamed the Unionists for not seizing earlier opportunities to " dish " their opponents. To LORD DUNRAVEN belongs the credit of having initiated an attempt to settle the land problem by a reasonable scheme of purchases. He has the idea now that some plan of devolution may widen the basis of local government, and knit together for tbe common interests the political elements which are now div'ded by traditiona animosities. He may expect too much from his experiment ; buc it is none the less likely to be made, and to be conducted with a similar applica- tion to the principle of devolution in other parts of the United Kingdom. That Mr. WYNDHAM and his colleagues will set their faces like flint against this manifestly logical development of their own policy is most improbable, in spite of Mr. BELL'S indignant appeals to the shade of Lord BEACONS- FIELD.</p>
        <p>The Conservative Labour Party has been re- pudiated this week by the Tzades and Labour Council, and as it has previously been repudiated by the Liberal Party and only distantly acknow- ledged by the Conservative Association, we expect almost at any moment to hear of its demise from exposure and neglect. Mr. Kelley, the secretary to the Lancashire and Obeshire Federation of Trades Councils has stated that he did not know a single official of the Conservative Labour Party who was a prominent trades unionist, in :fact he never heard the officials' names before the associa- tion was formed. Councillor Stott describes these officials as "absolute nonentities" and advises his fellow-unionists not to be led ast ray by them. Nevertheless the Conservative Labour Party takes possession of the Bridge Foot open space pretty frequently in order to enlighten the elec- torate on questions of the hour. On Thursday, Mr. Fearneley,chief spokesman of the Conservative Labour Party, endeavoured to explain away the severe comments of Mr. Kelley, but beyond admitting that they had no prominent trade union workers in their ranks and no paid officials be did not give mach information likely to improve the standing of his party locally. We should like to ask how many members the party has enrolled during its four months' existence, and if to uphold trades unionism and encourage loyalty arnoug unionists are its only objects? So far as can be judged from the utterances of its advocates, the Conservative Labour Party in a by-product of Toryism and is less concerned on behalf of Labour than it is in seeing the blue flag waving over the stronghold of vested interests.</p>
        <p>It is a waste of time for Mr. Fearneley and others to argue that the party which has done most for temperance reform is the Conservative party. Nobody takes the idea seriously for a moment, but if it amuses Mr. Fearneley and his friends well and good. It can do no harm. People who are serious studtaits of temperance and licensing reform have only to turn to the recent Licensing Bill to find ample proof that the Con- servative Government of to-day is just as much a friend of the brewer and the publican as any of its predecessors. Genuine temperance reformers aim at giving the power to the people to decide what is best for their needs and not placing it in the bands of Quarter Sessions and other authorities constituted largely of the titled and landed classes. Our friends of the very new party do not appear to even appreciate the immensity of the evil due to the consumption of strong drink. One of them, Mr. Macartney, argues that more disease and deaths can be traced to intemperance in eating than to over-indulgence in intoxicating liquor. This is, about as sensible a contention as Mr. Fearneley's, and it is not little remarkable that any man should seriously advance it from a public platform. Perhaps it is just as well, after all, that a new party has been created if only for .the ammmoda- tion it gives to such peculiar politicians.</p>
        <p>What would no doubt have proved a rich ora- torical treat was nipped in the bud. IVIr. Fearneley had just concluded his speech when a bell-man who had listened more or less patiently made his way to the front of the crowd, ..nd having rung his bell vigorously to command silence, announced his intention of addressing the gathering. The only encouragement he received was a volley of groans and sarcasms,but he persisted heroically and would no doubt have given full expression to his political views had not Mr. Fearnelev intervened. Despite the bell-man's assurance that whatever he said would strengthen the Tory speaker's case,Mr. Fearneley was obdurate, and after some little per- sHasion the would-be orator withdrew. Such is gratitude!</p>
        <p>can be traced every day in the correspondence of the " Times." The followers of MT. BALFOUR. and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN reproach the Unionist Free Traders with " dis- loyalty," and the Unionist Free Traders not unreasonably retort by asking whether loyalty means devotion to men or to principles. In Monday's " Times " Mr. HENRY BELL, of Bradford, introduces a new complication by proclaiming his loyalty ',to Conservative principles as typified by the policy of Lord BEACONSFIELD, Of glorious memory. "He it was who, by his manly straightforwardness, made me</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Pierpoint has no desire to be divorce,i from Parliament. As he remarked on Saturday he has "been so long in the House of Commons that he has become accustomed and almost wedded to the place." But we hardly agree with him further that it is "his natural place to go to." It seems to us most unnatural that he should remain there seeing that so far as Warrington is concerned he might as well represent Timbuctoo. The only</p>
        <p>thing of any note that we can recollect him doing while in the House of Commons wall, to record a vote against Mr. Shackleton s Bill and that achievement we do not sup- pose the electors will forget in a hurry. No) the House of Commons is not Mr. Pierpoint's "natural place." Can he be forgetting the claims of Cyprus? We had thought a grateful Govern- ment mould have realized ere this that Mr. Pier point was created by Providen:m to minister to the wants of Cypriotes, and have relieved him of the 'drudgery' of representing Warrington. What the Government has failed to do in the latter direction the electors must take the first oppor- tunity of performing. It would be cruel kindness to send Mr. Pierpoint back to Parliament.</p>
        <p>The intention of the local education authority to cope with the pressing need for additional school accommodation can no longer be doubted. Ie another column, the necessary legal notices are given of their intention to build two new schools. One is to be erected in Orford Avenue in close proximity to the Barracks and another is to be built near Lower Wash-lane for the benefit of children in the Latchford ward. Ratepayers who have just been struggling to pay their rates of 7s, 10d. in the 2 will receive the information with mixed feelings. The building and equipment Of two new schools will involve an expenditure of something like £20,000 and there are more build- ing operations toifollow. That they are required,no one who is acquainted with the educational needs of the Borough: will deny. Warrington managed to get along without a School Board, but it is now clearly established that the voluntary system Of the past was but a very poor substitute. The expenditure of public money was not saved, but merely postponed to a later date. We have new arrived at that period when the accommodation difficulty must be r 3solutely faced, and the deficiencies of past years made good. New schools are required in every part of the town, with the possible exception of the central portion, and the expenditure involved will be heavy. The only hope of the ratepayers for any relief is that the industrial undertakings of the Corporation may show han.d", some prcfits and provide something substantial towards the rapidly increasing expenditure.</p>
        <p>Ratepayers in the country districts who hay? had little cause to complain of the amount of their rates are already beginning to feel the additional burden cast upon them by the Education Act. M Penketh Parish Council meeting on Monday even" ing,Dr.Murray informed the astonished councillors that there would be a further increase in the rates. of 4d. or sd. in the 2 for the current half•year.Tliiii is necessitated by the County rate call which 19 the heaviest on record. In Penketh alone the amount required is £lOO in excess of last half year. The Education Ac,'; is mainly responsible for the increase, but County expenditure generally has been steadily going up year by year Mid there is reason to believe that it will continue to rise. The days of low rates are over. It is therefore all the more incumbent upon the representatives of the rate" payers to see that a commensurate return is tained for the increased expenditure. The admin.' istration of country districts has considerably improved since the advent of County and Parish Councils but there is still a real need for that effieiency and economy, of which we hear so muck' at election times.</p>
        <p>The regrettable frequency of suicides in South' West Lancashire has drawn from Mr. Coroner Brighouse a somewhat strong comment on the regularity with which juries return the familiar, verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind. Opinion on the subject is divided, but it appears to us that the Coroner's remarks will not have been in vain if they cause jurymen to consider more carefully an aspect of such cases which in many instances, no doubt, has been passed over in a perfunctory way. The law, says Mr. Brighouse, holds a man to be of sound mind until the COT trary is proved. Tender regard for the fee:" ings of bereaved relatives often inspires trl " insanity " verdict, and all that need be 9,8,1 a is that it is incumbent upon jurors to fully real'4 the responsibility which rests upon them. Whether the number of suicides is materially affinted 139 the findings of Coroner's Courts is an open que9". tion. Persons taking their own lives in a rash moment worild hardly, we think, reflect upon the probable verdict. Mr. Brighouse was, however, justified in calling attention to a matter which deserves more serious thought than is given to it by the majority of jurors.</p>
        <p>The estimates of losses at Liao-yang, including the hand-to-hand fighting near An-ping, make the battle one of the bloodiest on record. With the accession of William and Mary commenced the real history of the British Army, whose experiene,_e has been a varied one. During the eighteenth century it was a common saying among Con- tinental officers that England possessed "fl ar133,,Y of lions led by asses." The clumsy Duke of in 1799, lost 10,000 men in five engagements le Hollaud. Dettingen, too, cost 10,000 wee' Blenheim put a period to France's invincibLe reputation when she lost 12,000 killed and 14,(Uu captured; and in an hour and a half at Ramillee she lost 15,000 men. Malplaquet was called " terrible slaughter,' and accounted for 36,000 n3en' In 1783 Walpole said: "There are 50,000 wee slain this year in Europe, and not ete.„e Englishman." In Braddock's disaster in taw American campaign of 1755—an eighteenth tory Isandhlwana —10,500 men were lost. On ti e ottler hand, against the East we have not fared s,ie badly. Clive, at Plassey, n-uted 50,000 foot an' 14,000 horse. Civilized Europe however, Iles, Guessed the most anpaling sacrifices. The terrible tile of Moscow may be told in a sentence by saYieti tit of the 400,000 who formed Napoleon's Grsh Army only a few thousands re-crossed the Nielhe,i° December; while at Waterloo, "the first an- -1:-pit of fields, king-making victory," Napoleon lost 4,),000.</p>
        <p>„</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
