        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE.</p>
        <p>2. "The Parlourmaid " by H. S. Maclauchlan. St. Helens Poisoning Mystery. Mr. R. Pierpoint M.P., and Election Prospects. Our Local Churches. Training College Examination. 3. Events of the week (Illustrated). Football and Cricket. Foreseen: An Irish Story. Revising the Lists. Education Business. 6. Northern Union Meeting. Police News. Runcorn and Widnes. 7. Women's Chat and Fashions. Billiards. Penketh Parish Council Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>Christianity and Politics. An open meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. Sooieli was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, cdi Sunday, under the presidency of Councillor Bartell' Mr. A. Griffiths effectively sang "If with all hearts" and " The Holy City," being accompaned the piano by Mr. Frank Hickman. The Rev. arvey, of Widnes, addressed the meeting Christianity and Politics," and wished his hearers:', understand that he was not there to press the Cl ii of any party, but to urge them to take an interest l the well-being of the community at large. Ile °°ll3: tended that as Christians it was their duty to tekcof part in the national and municipal (Yovernment ith the cduntry. He made no excuse for dealing this subject, as he was going by the teachings Bible, passages relating to which might be fouou the books of Isaiah and Jeremiah. Social questi°l4l,o should take a foremost part in politics. Alluding the housing Of the working classes, he reminded 1110 hearers that in this country there were 70,000 Peof:, who were living five in a room, 26,000 six in a roil 0,000 seven in a room, 3,000 eight in a room, 3110 2,000 nine in a room. He considered that this scba one of the greatest problems which would have t° of r 9 worked out before we could expect better merebeart society, for after all, environments played a big P in the lives of our fellow men. If -people take an interest in the social, as well as the sPirtel of side of life, then they must not grumble at the stli-log affairs. In conclusion, he urged his hearers to their br pa religion into eir politics and make them purer a better. would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>Coming Events. To-morrow, in connection with the Harvest Thanksgiving services •at Bewsey road Wesleyan Church, the sermons will be preached by the Revs. John Gunuell and R. Wilkins Rees. The subject of Mr. Rees' sermon in the evening will be" The Lesson of the Seed." Special hymns will be sung and the church will be decorated for the occasion. At the Baptist Church. Litchford, to-morrow (Sun- day), evening, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, pastor, will preach on the subject" What Science has to say about Sin." The annual meeting of the Warrington Free Church Council will be held on February 14th. The chief apeaker will be the Rev. J. G. G-reenough, M.A., ex- President of the National Council of the Evangelical Pree Churches. The Rev. Herbert Dunnico will resume his ministry at Golborne-street Baptist Church to-morrow (Sun- day). At the morning service he will speak on." The i Ironies of Life," and n the evening his subject will be "General Booth and his Army." The Spiritualists of Warrington will hold their harvest festival in the Druids Hall to-morrow. Mrs. Powell-Williams will be the speaker. ASK for CUNNTIDIGIIIII'S HIGEI-CLASS 111SERX WATERS.—Advt. Personal. The health of Mr. Sam Woods, president of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation, and secretary of the Trades Union Congress, is slightly better, though far from satisfactory to his numerous friends. Mr. Woods is at present staying at his residence at Bryan.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. L. Riley,the well-known St. Helens advocate, has retired from practice as a solicitor, and hopes to be called to the bar on November 17th next. Some time ago Mr. Riley secured the degree of L.L. B. from Victoria University, and he is at present a student at Gray's Inn. Mr. Riley will continue to reside at Eccleston Park and his solicitor's practice will be carried on by his son, Mr. Pennington Riley, in con- innction with Messrs. P. C. Kelly and G. W. Picton, In honour of his recent coming of age, several im - Portant presentations were made on Thursday after- noon to the Earl of Rocksavage,heir to the Marquis of Cholmondeley. The presentation took place in the drawing-room at Cholmondeley Castle. The Cholmondeley and Barrow tenantry presented him with a pair of guns and a loving cup and an illumin- ated address. From the Frodsham and Rocksavage tenantry the Elrl received a beautiful silver centre piece, a half-hunter gold watch, and a silver matchbox; and from the children of Cholmondeley 'schools a silver cigarette box. fraE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 5:3, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advi. The Blue Coat Charity. The trustees of the Warrington Blue Coat Charity School will at their next quarterly meeting (October 17th) proceed to elect eight children (6 boys and 2 girls) to vacancies now existing in the institution. No candidate will be eligible unless an application has been made to Mr. Henry Greeuall (clerk to the trustees). The Coming Kubelik Concert. There has already been a big demand for tickets for the Kubelik concert in the Parr Hall on Friday, September 30th, and those who are desirous of hear- ing the world-famous violinist are advised to book their seats as early as possible. Kubelik has just accepted a £lO,OOO offer for a forthcoming American tour. His selections at the Warrington concert will include Paganini's "Concerto D Minor" and Kubelik's reputation alone should ensure a crowded house. Miss Muriel Gough (soprano), of Conti- nental fame, will sing "The sea hath its pearls" (Maud V. White) and Bolero "I Vesperi Siciliane " (Verdi). Miss Katherine Goodson, a brilliant pianist who was taught by Paderewki's master and, in addition to playing at 15 Saturday popular concerts at London Philarmonic, has toured three times in America, will also contribute to an excellent programme. Herr Ludwin, Schwab will be the accompanist. The arrangements for the concert are in the hands of Messrs. R. H. Pearson, of War- rington, and Mr. J. P. Johnson, of Kendal, and seats may be booked at Messrs. Pearson's music warehouse, Bold-street.</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Rev. A. Inglis. There was an interesting gathering in Holy Trinity Schoolroom last evening when the Rev. Arthur Inglis, M.A., who is severing his connection with the parish to take up the assistant curacy of Winwick, was presented by the congregation with an illuminated address and a purse containing forty guineas. The Vicar (Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) presided and the presentation was made by Mrs. William Dutton. The address bore special testimony to the recipient's work in relation to the Sunday schools and the Young fen's Association and Bible Class, and to his loyal support of the Vicar in all branches of parish -work The subscribers concluded by offering Mr. Inglis their hearty congratulations on his approaching marriage, coupled with their best wishes for his future welfare and happiness.—ln returning thanks, the Rey. A. Inglis said he felt it a great trial to leave the parish where he had been so happy. He had merely tried to do his duty loyally and faithfully, and the Vicar and Mrs. Carleton had been to him the best of friends. Mrs. Dutton was thanked for making the presentation on the proposition of Mr. J. Halton seconded by Mr. Rowson.—The Vicar piid a tribute to Mr. Inglis's good ‘Vork and said ha had won the esteem and regard of everyone in the parish.—During the evening an enjoyable musical programme,arranged by Mr. Percy Randall, was submitted. Annual Visit to the Ship Clio. On Tuesday a deputation from the School Atten- dance Committee consisting of Councillors Ellison and Lord and Mr. T. May, with Mr. Murray, director of education paid their annual visit of inspection to this ship. They were accompanied by , Coun- cillors Davies and Rogers and Mr. E. Plinston, mem- bers of the committee, and also Alderman Bennett and Councillor Ashcroft. The party were taken on board at 2.30 in the afternoon, and in the absence of Captain Langton, were met by other officers of the ship, who at once placed themselves at their disposal to give all information regarding the working of the ship. The visit is always a most interesting one both for the boys and visitors, the former being allowed a little relaxation from their ordinary work in order to give an entertainment for the benefit of those present. A thorough inspection of the ship was then made and all details of the education and work per- formed explained, which were of. a most interesting character. There are 247 boys on board, only 8 being from Warrington, one a very young boy only recently sent there. From inquiries made at this stage as to the future career of the boys, the visitors were told that at least 9 out of every 12 were sent out as sailors on merchant ships, and ultimately drafted into the Royal Navy; the great desire being not to send them back home with the risk of again getting into their old bad habits. In fact "their whole training seemed in the direction to qualify the boys to become British sailors. There is an excellent band on board numbering 18 boys, and a special room set apart for their training. The drill exercise which was gone through reflected the highes credit on those in charge of the ship. Th discipline on board is the principal feature, and it w e noticed how willingly the boys obeyed all orders; their rough nature apparently being subdued. The gun exercise waq well performed, and will he of great service to the lads when they arrive on board a man-of-war. Each boy on leaving the ship is provided with an excellent outfit, and his whereabouts noted d9wn and a record kept. After some excellent advice gyven•to the boys by Councillor Ellison and others the -visitors departed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>AIL,,_ , , . –</head>
        <p>AIL,,_ , , . -</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF</p>
        <p>TONICS.-63. SA.NKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Death of a Former Archdeacon of Warrington._</p>
        <p>Churchpeople in this district will regret to hear of the death of the Right Rev. John Wareing Bardsley, M.A., D.D., Bishop of Carlisle, who passed away on Wednesday. Prior to his consecration as Bishop of Sodor and Man,Dr. Bardsley worked in several Liver- pool parishes. One of the first acts of the late Dr. Ryle, the first Bishop of Liverpool, was to appoint him to the new archdeaconry of Warrington. The appointment gave rise to general satisfaction, and shortly afterwards a handsome testimonial was pre- sented to the Archdeacon, accompanied with an address expressive of the high esteem in which he was held by the clergy. After results fully justified this appointment. The Archdeacon's honesty of purpose, combined with wise discretion, his consistent loyalty to evangelical 'principles, associated with a large- heartedness not always found, and his whole-hearted devotion to his Bishop, proved him to be a man in every way suitable Mr the high position conferred upon him.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park. The following programme will be played in, the Park to-morrow at 3.15 by Crosfield's Band, conducted by Mr. W. Halliwell :—March, "The Cossack," W. .Rimmer ; selection, "II Seraglo," Mozart ; recit, " Comfort ye my people," Handel ; chorus, "And the , glory of the Lord," by request; grand selection, "Memories of Mendelssohn," W. Rimmer ; grand fantasia, "Gems of Tschaikowsky," C. Swift; hymn; "Onward Christian soldiers," arranged by W. Halliwell ; "God save the King." Fire Last Night. About 7.30 last evening a fire broke out at the works of Messrs. Peacock Brothers, pinafore manufac- turers, West-street. The fire brigade was summoned and turned out smartly under Deputy-superintend- ent Turner. On arrival it was found that a long low building used as a workroom and tea room and containing sewing and other machinery, was in flames. The property is situated in a very congested neighbourhood, but fortunately there was not much breeze, and adjoining buildings were undamaged. The brigad.e also succeeded in prevent- ing the fire spreading to the other sections of Messrs. Peacock's premises. After an hour's work the flames were subdued, but not before the workshop had been gutted. The damage amounts to several hundred pounds. How the fire originated is not known. Funeral of Mrs. John Sampson. The remains of the late Mrs. Sampson, wife of Mr. John Sampson, licensee of the Norton Arms Hotel, Latchford, were interred at the Warrington Cemetery on Sunday afternoon. The Rev. H. 13. Firth, vicar of St. James' Church, Latchford, performed the last rites. The chief mourners were Messl s. J. Sampson (husband), W. T. Sampson and J. T. Sampson (sons), the Misses Clara, Amy and Gertrude Sampson (daughters), and Mr. W. Darlington, St: Helens (son- in-law). Wreaths had been forwarded by the follow- ling relatives and friends: Mr. John Sampson, Mr. and Mrs. Hallwood, Mr. and Mrs, W. B. Roberts, Mr. and Airs. S. Wakefield, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Walker, Mr. and Mrs. W. Greenhouse, Mr. and Mrs. Billington, Mr. and Mrs. H. F. Greenough, Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Fleming, Mr. and Mrs. H. Miller, Mr. and Mrs. Dutton and family, Mr. J. C. Shaw, and the Misses Shaw, Mr. and Mrs. W. L. Walker, Mrs. Whitehouse, Mrs. Sampson (Liverpool), Miss Deakin, the Warrington District Homing Society, "Jack, Amy and Gertie," Mr. E. Morris, Mrs. Hardman and Beaty, "Harry and Alice," "Clara and Walter," " Walter and Agnes," and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Property under the Hammer.</head>
        <p>Property under the Hammer.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Thomas Sutton and sons conducted two property sales at the Blue Bell Inn, Horsemarket-st., on Wednesday evening. There was a good attend- ance and bidding was fairly brisk. The first sale was of houses in the Cockhedge and Brick-street neigh- bourhood. Lot one consisted of leasehold messuaae or cottage No. 11, Garden-row, Cockhedge, held for the residue of a term of 1,000 yea's from the Bth day of September 1824. This lot was withdrawn. Lot 2 comprising two leasehold messuages or cottages at the corner of Crossley-street, and Brick-street, War- rington No. 37 and 39 Brick-street, held for residue of a term of 2,000 years from Ist October 1770 was sold to Mr. John Hudson, of Warrington, for £475: In the second sale the first lot was a freehold cottage, yard and garden and two crofts containing in. 2r.30p. situate in Winwick on the east side of the highway from Warrington to Winwick. Mr.E.L.Bent, of Earles- town,was the purchaser at £330.L0t 2,which consisted of freehold plot or land containing 3r. 15p. situate in. Winwick on the east side of the highway from War- rington to Winwick together with eight substantially built cottages fronting to the highway and the wheel- wright's shop etc., at the rear thereof, was sold to Mr. Thomas Hill, of Warrington, through Mr. H. J. Long- ton, solicitor, for £1,500. Lot 3 comprised two lease- hold cottages Nos. 17 and 18, Kendrick-street, Warrington (contents of site 246 square yards) held for a term of 998 years from Ist October, 1874. Mr. Thomas Unsworth, of Warrington, was the purchaser at :£392. Lot 4, freehold shop and dwelling-house, two dwelling-houses No. 13 Heathside and Nos. 67 and 69 Legh-street), and stables adjoining (in Heath- side) Warrington, held for a term of 999 years from Ist November, 1872 was withdrawn.—Messrs. ;Jeans 1' and Son were the solicitors to the vendor.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS ;UNREAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Marriage at _Holy Trinity Church!</head>
        <p>Marriage at _Holy Trinity Church!</p>
        <p>At HOly Trinity ehurch ox i Wednesday morning the marriage was solemnized of Miss Minnie Garnett, daughter&gt; of Mr. T. J. Garnett, 40, Arpley-street, Warring,ton,and Mr. Robert William Despard, Victoria Cottage, King's Island, Limerick. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton•officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, was prettily attired in cream voile, trimmed with silk insertion and lace. She wore a tulle veil and wreath of orange blossom and carried a lovely bouquet of white chrysanthemums, lilies, white heather and smilax. The bridesmaids were Miss Emily Garnett (sister of the bride), and Miss Amy G-arnett (cousin). Mr. F. Oarnett (brother of the bride) discharged the duties of best man. Mr. Percy Randall presided at the organ and played appropriate selections,including Mendelssohn's ".Wedding March." After the ceremony breakfast was served at the house of the bride's father, and the newly married couple left by an early train for London and the South Coast. The bride's travelling dress was a brown cloth costume with hat to match. A number of useful and valuable presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SUGAR is DEARER.—Advt.</head>
        <p>SUGAR is DEARER.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Bazaar.</p>
        <p>"The British Isles" Bazaar in aid of the funds of the Warringtoa Liberal Association is rapidly becom- ing an absorbing topic of conversation. Warrington is used to bazaars but it will discover something— nay many things—out of the ordinary when the Parr Hall is opened on October sth. There is every reason to believe that the bazaar will be the biggest, best, and brightest ever held in the town. Tha stalls are certain to be heavily laden with goods of all descrip- tions, and handsome purchases—even bargains—can be guaranteed. A scheme of decoration by Messrs. Le Matstre and Company of Manchester has already, been prepared, and the splendour of their representa- tion of scenic gems from the British Isles will need to be seen to be realised. We have previously indi- cated that the entertainments and side-shows are to be out of the common for a bazaar. The best known vocal and instrumental artistes in Warrington will assist in the chamber concerts, and at least two bands will discourse sweet music during the time the bazaar is open. One attraction we are at liberty to mention even now,and that is" The Treasure Temple of the Queen of Sheba," where Her Royal Highness will not only receive the homage of visitors but will graciously demonstrate her marvellous healing power —handed down through the ages--by a gentle touch that thrills like a stream of fire and yet does not burn. Next week we shall announce other attractions and give particulars of the opening cemmonies. Our readers will be well-adviscd to save both time and money in order to enjoy the delights which are in store for the sth, 6th, and 7th October. Presentation to a Works Manager. An informal gathering of the men employed by the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., at their Warrington works was held in the yard a few minutes prior to the usual dinner hour on Thursday,to present Mr. J. F. Davy, the late works manager, with a gold watch and silver rose bowl, subscribed for by his brother officials and the foremen and workmen. The presentation was made by the joint managing director, Mr. John A. Ransome, who publicly wished Mr. Davy every success in his new employment, and asked his acceptance of the watch as a tangible evidence of the good-will which he was leaving behind after six years' service. He also asked him to accept the silver rose bowl as a token to his wife of the regard in which both were held.—Mr. Davy warmly responded, thanking them heartily for the gifts, and assuring them that they would be highly valued by himself and his wife as representing the good-will which he had been fortu- "nate enough to wan during the course of his six years' service amongst them. He assured them that they would always have at heart the continued prosperity , of the company, and thanked them all sincerely for their good wishes on behalf of himself and his wife. The watch was a gold lever hunter, engraved with Mr. J. F. Davy's monogram on the outer case, and inside with the following Inscription :—" Presented to John F. Davy by the staff and employees of the Warrington works of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Com- pany Limited." The rose bowl was of silver,elegantly designed and chased, and mounted on an ebony stand and bore the following inscription :—" Presented to Mr. and Mrs. John F. Davy by the staff and employees of the Warrington works of the Richmond Gas Stove , and Meter Company Limited." Election of Guardian at Earlestown. The contest for the election of a guardian to the Warrington Union, to fill the vacancy caused by the death of lfr. John Clarke,who represented the viaduct Ward, Earlestown, took place on Monday last. There were three candidates, Mr. N. Siddeley, retired trades- man, Mr. A. King, ironmonger, and Mr. M. Seton, commission agent. Considerable interest was taken in the contest, but it was felt all along that Mr. Seton would secure the seat, in consequence of the wavering attitude of the Free Church Council, who at firs adopted Mr. King and finally decided to support Mr Siddeley. Mr. Seton had a host of warkers and a number of conveyances were placed at his disposal for the purpose of bringing voters to the poll. The voting stations were at the Earlestown District school and Mr. Sutton jun., officiated as returning officer.— The result of the poll was declared shortly before 9 o'clock, when it was found that Mr. Seton had secured 310 votes, Mr. Siddeley 154, Mr. Kin.. l 37. Mr. Seton was therefore declared elected'. Each of the oandidates addressed the waiting crowd outside, and an acrimonious dispute occurred afterwards amongst the candidates and their workers, when 'personalities were freely indulged in.</p>
      </div>
