      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Animated Pictures at the Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>Animated Pictures at the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>An entirely new and exceptionally interesting series of animated photographs will be exhibited at the Parr Hall next week by the New Century Picture Company, which has gained such a high reputation during its long stay at the Picton Hall, Liverpool. The memorable scenes attendant on their Majesties' recent visit to Liverpool will be depicted, the com- pany having photographed the various functions by special co.nmand. So admirably was the work done that the pictures were produced in Liverpool the same evening,and the Bishop of Liverpool and the Lord Mayor and Lady Mayoress witnessed the exhibition. One view portrays his Majesty in the act of laying the foundation stone of the new Cathedral, and, there is a unique panorama of the ceremony and crowds of spectators on the Mount. That the company's collec- tion of films is th.oroughly up-to-date is evidenced by- the fact that the audience will be able to witness stirring incidents in the Russo-Japanese campaign which have been animatographed by special per- mission of H. I. M. Mutsuhito, Emperor of Japan. The attack on Port Arthur and the first modern naval battle are included in the series. Events in the life of Napoleon form the subject of another special feature,and a number of comic views will be displayed. The Volunteer Band, under the conductorship of Bandmaster Richardson, has been engaged, and it may be relied upon that the enter- tainment will amply repay a visit. The pictures will be shown for six evenings, commencing on Mon- day, and there will he matinees on Thursday and Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—AdVt</head>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—AdVt</p>
        <p>I Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co.. Ltd. The following report of the directors of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Limited, will be submitted at the thirty-first annual meeting of share- holders, to be held at the company's office in Warrington, September 29th :—" The profits for the year ending June 30th, 1904, amount to £46,747 175.5 d, and the balance brought forward from last year is £7,190 13s. 3d., togetherls3 938 10s. Bd. An interim dividend at the rate of 6 per cent. per annum on the preference shares and 2 per eent. on the ordinary shares was paid in March last for the half-year ending IDecember 31st, 1903, amounting to £13,256 ss. The directors now recommend that for the past half-year a dividend at the rate per annum of 6 per cent. on the preference shares and 4 per cent. on the ordinary shares be declared, making a total distribution fiat the year of £32,596 17s. 6-d. The directors further ' recommend that £lO,OOO be added to the reserve fund, that the sum of £4,986 12s. 4d., which represents the increase in the capital expenditure account for the year, be written off, and that the balance of £6,355 Os. 10d. be carried forward. The colliery leases and goodwill account now stands in the balance sheet at £lOO,OOO, having been written down from £315,559 17s. 9d., at which it originally appeared. The directors now recommend that this item be extin- guished altogether by a transfer of £lOO,OOO from the reserve fund, leaving the balance of that fund, after making the addition recommended in the foregoing patagraph, at £llO,OOO. The business of the com- pany, both in coal and iron, during the past year has suffered from falling markets and a slackness of trade in common w;th most of the other industries of the country. In compliance with the articles of associa- tion, Ur. G-. F. Pearson retires from the Board,and is eligible for re-election. The auditors, Messrs. Blease and Sons, also retire, and are eligible for re-election. —(Signed) J. S. flarmood Banner, Chairman."</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS. I 63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.--Advt. Warrington Photographic Society. The annual general meeting of this Society was held at the Old academy on Tuesday evening last, Mr. F. V. L. Mathias being in the chair. The secretary's report showed that the past season had been highly successful in every way, and the membership also showed satisfactory increase. The treasurer's balance sheet was presented, the financial position showing a balance on the year's working of X,3 us. 71d. compared with a deficit the previous year of 6s. sd. The Chairman proposed a vote of thanks to the committee, which was carried unanimously.—Mr. F. W. Purse made special mention of the energetic and successful manner in which the hon. secretary (Mr. G. Nicholls) had carried out his duties and attributed the very satisfactory position of the Society to his very capable management, and this was heartily endorsed by the meeting Mr. .1. Fairhurst, the past president, having intimated his desire to withdraw from 'office, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to him cor past services. On the proposition of Mr. Nicholls, seconded by the Chair- man, it was resolved to ask Mr. Roger Parr to accept the office of president. The vice-presidents elected were: Messrs. Alderman Bennett, E. J. Hall, J. Harding,?. V. L. Mathias and W. Winstanley.—For the office of secretary Mr. G: Nicholls was again unani- mously requested to be elected, but owing to business arrangements he was unable to accept the office, and choice fell upon Mr. Smithson, who was ultimately elec- ted. The following were re-elected for their respective offices. Hon. treasurer, Mr. F. Garside ; hon. lanternist, Mr. H. Warburton; hon. assistant lanternist, Mr. J. V. Moore; hon. auditor, Mr. P. Dalton. For the committee 10 members were pro- posed and a ballot being taken resulted in the election of the following. Messrs. F. W. Purse, G. Nicholls, D. S. Birrell, Mrs. A. C. Smithson, and Messrs. Bishop and Dolan. The revised rules of the Society were read to the meeting, and adopted. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman (Hr. F. V. L. Mathias) who in reply expressed the hope and belief that the Society would continue its prosperous career, and asked every member to make the usefulness of the Society known to all photographic friends. FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.--Advt.</p>
        <p>Court Martial at Orford Barracks. Major W. E. S. Tyler, of the South Lancashird Regiment, presided at, a 'eonrt martial at Orfore Barracks on Tuesday,when Private George Joseph Bill, of the King's Liverpool Regiment was charged with stealing a gold safety pin, a white shirt, two ties, two pairs of socks and a handkerchief, the property of Lieutenant F. J. Lloyd, of the 3rd Battalion King's Liverpool Regiment, by whom prisoner was employed as servant. He pleaded not- guilty.—Captain C. J. Stevenson prosecuted on behalf of the military authori- ties and Hr. A. Browne defended.--Lieutenant Lloyd deposed that when he went to Altcar in June for the annual training,he took the gold safety pin with him. He did not miss it until about the, end of July—a fortnight after his return to Warrington.—when he wanted it. He questioned prisoner about it, and Bill stated that he had not seen it lately. Witness had not seen it since. He identified the shirt, socks, and other articles, with the exception of one tie, which was identical with one he possessed and which was miss- ing. The articles were found in prisoner's kit towards the end of August by the military police, who made a search.—Evidence was also given by Private E. Savage, South Lancashire Regiment, Lance-Sergeant J. Dwyer, Detective Nieac o etc (Warrington Borough Police) and Private Williams. —Mr. Browne for the defence, contended that no evidence had ben given of amithing otherwise than of innocent possession of the articles in accordance with prisoner's duties. There was no carrying away and no intention to steal. With regard to the shirt the evidence was not satisfactnry that it was not given to him by Lieutenatft Lloyd, - while the Lieutenant could not swear to one of the ties; which was not inconsistent with prisoner's statement to Detective Meacock that he bought it „For 2s. With regard to the gold safety pin there was no evidence that it had ever been in prisoner's possession. He submitted the charges were not proved.—Majors Stevenson and Hartley spoke as to the prisoner's character, the latter stating that he found prisoner a good and faithful servant.—The President announced that the case was closed.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK. BKID GE-STREET.—A.</p>
      </div>
