      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>[ALL RTGIIPS RESERVED].</head>
        <p>[ALL RTGIIPS RESERVED].</p>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of " Scnrch Lights on Land, Labour awl Social Problems." SOCIAL PRO3LEMS AT OSWESTRY.</p>
        <p>"There is not one single source of human happiness against which there have not been uttered the most lugubrious predictions. It would be extremely useful to make a collection of the hatred and abuse that all those changes have experienced which are now admitted to be marked improvements in our condi- tion. Such a history might make folly a little more modest and suspicious of its own decisions."—Sydney Smith.</p>
        <p>When I am stranded for a few days in a strange town my natural curiosity prompts me to explore every nook and corner of the place, and then read its history, and find out what physical and moral causes had produced such unexpected results.</p>
        <p>1.-THE MAKERS OF OSWESTRY WERE INTENSELY CONSERVATIVE.</p>
        <p>They were bitterly opposed to new ideas, and fresh enterprises. They b3lieved in letting things alone, and in putting the brake on the wheels of progress. They never went in for any new building, so long as an old one could be patched, and mended, and made to serve their purpose. I did not learn these-things from books. I picked them up in the streets, and read them in the bricks and stones, and public buildings. Towns and cities have their distinctive habits, and their peculiarities of temper, and their besetting sins, and the man who observes and studies these things, can read them like a book.</p>
        <p>Oswestry Parish Chtirch is a good object lesson to illustrate my point. It has a big, square, old tower, that probably dates from the time of the Saxons. The tower was evidently built at two different periods, and it has been patched and mended, and buttressed, and restored ever so many times. It will need repairing again before long, for an enterprising tree has found a, home in the solid masonry near the top, and it is tear- ing the tower to pieces by its vigorous growth. The church itself is patchwork, crazy-quilt of archi- tecture: There are bits of all sorts and all ages in it. Originally there was very much tower and very little church. But the church has grown, and every fresh builder that came put in a new style or architec- ture of his own, to advertise the fact that it was built by instalments, and taken in in numbers, and it is not finished yet. I learned the same lesson in the narrow streets in the centre of the town. At the most dangerous corner in the place, they have pulled down some old property,but they have not widened the street. They are building a new bank on the site of the old obstruction,to perpetuate the old evils. Some day it will cost a pretty penny to pay for all this shortsightedness and after-wit, for the narrow streets will have to be widened when the land has reached a fabulous price. It would be easy to multiply examples of this con- servative spirit, in all parts of the town, but the most striking examples are to be picked up in the official records of the, place. I have but space to deal with two of them.</p>
        <p>IL-OSWESTRY OUGHT TO HAVE BEEN. ONE</p>
        <p>OF OUR SEATS OF LEARNING. In P 399, David Holbache gave forty-one acres of land to found and endow a Free Grammar School at Oswestry. This endo yment is one of the oldest of ,its kind in the kingdom, for Loudon itself could not boast such a charity till nearly a century later. Leyland tells us that David Holbache, the pions founder, was a lawyer, and a member of Parliameni,and he was also steward of the town and lordship. Origi- nally the school was managed by the Corporation, who collected the rents, and spent the money in maintaining a schoolraaster,and in keeping the school house in repair. Among the old documents I have seen, it is said that the schoolmaster shall enjoy all the rents and revenues from the said lands during his term of office, and forty shillings a year from the royal charity. He shall opAn his school at six in the morning in summer, and seven in winter, and he shall continue teaching till five ;meals or sickness excepted. He shall teach Greek, and Latin, and English, and his pupils must translate and write with their own hands, one such exercise every week: At his own cost,he must provide an able and efficient usher to teach the younger sort to read, and write, and under- stand the foundations of logic. The Corporation shall have control of the estate, and protect the timber and trees growing on it. The scrubs and underwood, and dead logs, are to be given to the schoolmaster for fuel. He may also receive one shilling for entrance fee, and twopence yearly for cock fight money, and all other gifts that his pupils or their parents may give him. Now I have shown you that very early in its career; Oiwestry had a free Grammar School, and forty one acres of land by way of endowment, and if the squires and rich people in the neighbourhood had followed David Holbache'sexample,they might have been able to set up a school that would have competed with Harrow, or Rugby, or Eton. But the "Proud Salopianes " of those dayshad no love for learning, and they regarded all educational endow- ments as useless and mi schievous investments. So the control of the Oswestry Grammar School was handed over to twenty trustees, who were known as bailiffs. These bailiffs were supposed to be good men and true, but many of them were sad rogues. -Valuable trees were cut down, and the timber was sold.but the money never went to the schoolmaster. Fields and meadows were let at half their value, and bribes were given to the bailiffs and their wives.</p>
        <p>A Comniission. of Enqniry was held in September 1631, when it was proved that Dorothy Cooper received a bribe of ten shilliugs, and twenty nobles, worth six shillings and eightpence each, for persuading her husband to sign a lease of the School land. Kathrine Blodwell, the wife of another bailiff, received bribes for a similar purpose. William Helm. and William Morris, received ten pounds for the sale of timber, and they divided the money between them. Three other bailiffs had taken trees away, and used them without paying for them. So the estate was impoverished, and the school was starved,and the schoolmaster was robbed by the very people who were elected to protect his interests. _ _</p>
        <p>The Commissioners decreed that the bailiffs should be for ever discharged from their trust, and.that the nomination of a schoolmaster should henceforth rest withthe Bishop. Those who had stolen timber must pay the full value of it, and two pounds damages, for some of this timber had been missing more than twenty years. When the school was handed over to the control of the bishop and his nominee, it fell into the hands of another pack of thieves. The bishop always appointed a parson as headmaster, and a poor lay drudge was the usher. , The parson got nearly all the money, and the usher did all the work. One clerical headmaster exchanged his school at Oswestry with some other parson, without the consent of the Mayor and Corporation, and as the bishop approved of this arrangement, there was a lively little</p>
        <p>quarrel among them. The-now corner died shortly-after this exchange of schools, and the bishop appointed his own nephew as head master. This nephew grew tired of this appointment, so he sold it to another parson for thirty pounds. Meanwhile the whole estate was going to rack and ruin. The bishop's nominees were quite as greedy as the bailiffs wives, and a trifle more dishonest. They cut down about two hundred trees, and sold them and put the money into their own pockets. They leased the land to people who stunk to it after their leases had expired, and claimed it as their own.</p>
        <p>When Dr. Danne was master of the school he found that nearly half the. land that was given by David Holbache had been deliberately stolen by these clerical thieves. He commenced a law suit at his own expense, and spent nearly four hundred pounds in compelling the land grabbers to give up their plunder. In 1796, he compelled Mr. Venables to give up nearly eleven acres of this stolen land, and Mrs. Hilditch restored nine and a half acres that had fallen into her hands through somebody's rascality and dishonesty. You will admit that this is a sordid and disgraceful story, but it is absolutely true, and any man can readit for himself in the official records of the town. The pity of it is that such plundering, and thieving of educa- tional endowments went on everywhere in those days of darkness and ignorance. 11.-OSWESTRY MIGHT HAVE BEEN A GREAT RAILWAY CENTRE.</p>
        <p>It is situatel on the great highway from London to Holyhead, and the old stage coaches used to pass through it, and stay there for the night. When I was motoring along that road at the last election, I found that all the milestones recorded the distance between those two places, and if you wanted any local information you had to look out for a finger post, or ask a policeman. When the Great Western Railway Company first applied for powers to make a line from London and the West of England to Chester, and Birkenhead, the plans were made for a station at Oswestry. But the local squires and land-owners were bitterly opposed to the scheme. They said it would ruin the stage coach traffic, and destroy the peace and quietness of the town. So the Railway Company passed them by on the other side. The line was made through Whittington and Gobowen two or three miles away, and the good people of Oswestry had to tramp that distance in all weathers to find a train. In later years a branch line was built from Gobowen to Oswestry to link the place with the outside world. But this was a bit of after-wit"" for which the town 'had to pay] very dearly. The engine sheds and the carriage works that the company intended to build were taken to Shrewsbury, and Oswestry lost them because she was a day behind the fair. She was not ready when her opportunity came. In later years she welcomed the Cambrian Railway but that can never make up for the loss of the Great Western. It is a small unremunemtive concern that will probably pay a dividend on all its stock, sometime before the resurrection, but it can never do much to increase the prosperity of the town. Oswestry received its first charter from Richard 11. in 1399, and its Grammar School was founded and endowed in the same year. It has controlled its own destinies for more than five centuries and it has allowed other towns to outstrip it in the race. Its population is less than ten thousand to-day, and it is stationary or declining. How far its inherent Conservative opposi- Mon to changes and improvements has retarded its growth and interfered with its prosperity I must leave you to judge. As I think of what it might have been, and whit it ought to have been, I an reminded of what! our greatest poet says:— There is a tide in the affairs of men, Which, taken at the flood, leads on t 3 fortune: Omitted, all the voyage of their life Is bound in shallows, and in miseries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WESLEYANISM IN WARRINGTON CIRCUIT.</head>
        <p>WESLEYANISM IN WARRINGTON CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>Quarterly Meeting at Lymm.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting in connection with the Warrington Wesleyan circuit was held at Lymm on Thursday. The Rev. G. H. Carnson (superintendent) presided, and all the ministers of the circuit were present, namely, Rev. R. Wilkins Rees, Rev. J. Manning, gev. J. Gunnell and Rev. Joseph Howard. The new minister (Rev. J. Manning) was heartily welcomed and briefly. responded. The circuit finances were reported as being in a healthy condition. A statement was presented by the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees as to the foreign missionary sermons and meetings to be held during the coining winter throughout the circuit. Mr. R. G.Henshall was re-appointqd foreign missions treasurer, the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees foreign missions secretary, and Mr. G. W. Weaver, assistant secretary. Mr. Perkins was appointed temporarily, temperance secretary. The Chairman spoke of the claims on the circuit of the Theological Institution and also of the Liverpool Mission and referred to the forthcoming foundation stone laying of the Charles Garratt Memorial Hall at the end of the present month. A further statemen tas to the new church to be erected at Stockton Heath will be presented to the December quarterly meeting. A special committee was formed of ivarious officials of the circuit for the discussion of the spiritual condi- tion of the circuit, in the hope that some recommends- tion might be made at the next circuit meeting. An invitation was given by the 1311 d-street friends to hold the December meeting there.—The invitation was accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LIVERPOOL WESLEYAN SYNOD.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL WESLEYAN SYNOD.</p>
        <p>Grants to Needy Circuits.</p>
        <p>Toe Wesleyan Synod of the Liverpool district, met on Wednesday at the Brunswick Chapel. Moss-street, the Rev. T. Galland Hartley presiding. The Rev. Albert H. Walker acted as secretary. There was a large attend- ance of ministers, of whom a considerable proportion have just come into the district, and of laymen. The home mission and chanel committee was elected, the new members being the Rev. H. Arnaud Scott and Mr. H. B. Williams, Alderms.nlDackles and Mr. Cory Monkhouse being unanimously re-elected. The Rev. J. T. Gurney was again appointed secretary of the home mission section, and the Rev. W. R. Maltby and Alder- man Duckles ca-secretaries of the chapel affairs section. Mr. Isaac A. Mack accepted the post of treasurer. The special grants to circuits in need were considered. The grants were as follow : —Mold, £3O ' • Carnarvon and Bangor, £5O; Garstang, £6O; Aberystwyth, £5O; Liverpool Mission, £100; Eloylake and West Kirby, £6O; Severn Valley Mission, viz., Oswestry, £5O ; Newtown (conditionally), £2O; Welshpool, .230 • for lay agents and other special home mission work, £120; total £570.</p>
        <p>_ 'Arr. Gurney reported the provisional arrangements made for the home missionary deputation. The deputa- tion consists of the Revs. Samuel Wilkes and Jabez Parkyn, who will address meetings through the district from Monday, 16th, to Friday, 27th January. Applica- tions for the services of the connexional •evangelists were received and approved, the application from Great Homer-street Circuit receiving the first place. By the direction of the conference a strong committee was formed to consider the position of the Garstang Circuit and the possibility of its being amalgamated with one of the adjoining circuits. The foreign missionary committee was appointed Mr. Posnett being appointed as an additional lay sscretary. The district foreign missi mary anniversary ta'„es place on April Ist, 2nd, 3rd, and 4th. at Liverpool. Reference was made to the sailing of nine missionaries for the West Coast of Africa on Saturday next, from the Landing-stage at 10 a.m. Thefollowing appointments were made :—Mr. James J. Mack to be treasurer of the children's funds; Mr. J. C. Stead, J.P., treasurer of the aged ministers' fund; the Rev. John Wilson, 8.A., B.D , to be probationers' secretary; the Rev. H. C. J. Sidnell, 8.A., education secretary : the Rev. H. Nialdwyn Hughes, 8.A., temper- ance secretary; and the Rev. P. Ainsworth, local preachers' secretary. The conference directed the ap- pointment of a district class leaders' committee, which -as accordingly formed, with the Rev. H. Harrison IGower as secretary. A conversation took place upon a regulation of the conference affecting Wesley guilds, and the education secretary was directed to ascertain and report to the May Synod the statistics of Wesley guilds throughout the district. The annual meeting of the Synod was fixei to take place at Chester between 7th and 14th May.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ENDING SEPTENIBER_ 10th,_ 1904,</p>
        <p>Daring the week, the following births and deaths were registered :—Warrington Registration District births, 52 ; deaths, 26. The excess of births over deaths was 26.</p>
        <p>The death-rate from all causes was equal to 19.8 per 1,000 against 21.3 the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 17.7 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 8.3 per 1,000, against 11.4 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 6.1 the corresponding period of 1903.</p>
        <p>The aies-OiTnortality were:- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-35 65—up. Total 13 2 1 1 6 3 26</p>
        <p>_ The deaths included-1 from whooping cough, 10 from diarrhcea, 1 from other tubercular diseases, 3 from cancer, 1 from pneumonia, 1 from heart disease, 1 from accidents, 8 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during tke we :—Scarlet fever, 21 cases. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending September 10th, 113. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 56 Faii. and therefore 3 deg. higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 70 F., was recorded on the 6th inst., and the minimum temperature 42F., on the 6th inst.</p>
        <p>The rainfall measured .47 inch. The general direction was S.W.</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health. HAMS, BACON &amp; CHEESE ARE DEARER.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>IRON AND STEEL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>THE IRON AND STEEL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Members to Visit the United States.</p>
        <p>The autumn meeting of the Iron and Steel Institute is to be held next month in the United States, and the arrangements are already well advanced. So far as we have been able toTather, there will be at least three Warrington members at the meeting, namely Mr. H. Owen, secretary of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company, Limited, and Mr. T. Atherton and Mr. C. E. Bunch, both of whom hold ::mportant positions in connection; with the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Limited. It is fourteen years since the Iron and Steel Institute met in the United States. On that occasion, Mr. F. Monks, J.P., attended, and it will be remembered that he gave a very interesting lectare on his return, in which he recounted his experiences and spoke of some of the lessons to be learnt from American methods of manufacture. Mr. Monks is unable to accompany the party on this occasion. The meetings commence on Monday, October 24th, on which date there will be excursions to power houses,bridges, industrial establishments, universities, the Navy yard, subway and other places of interest in New York. Tll3, annual general meeting of the insti- tute will be held in the evening, followed by a recep- tion. The daylight hours of the following day will be devoted to an excursion up the Hudson, stopping at West Paint, where the United States Military Academy is situated. In the morning and afternoon of Wednesday (October 26th) there will be two meet- ings of the institute for the reading and discussion of papers. On Thursday morning the party will leave New York for Philadelphia and on Saturday there will be a reception at Washington by the President of the United States. Subsequently the party will visit Pittsburg, Cleveland, Buffalo, and other industrial centre-s, where American methods of manufacture will be seen in operation. Arrangements are also being made for a limited number to visit the St. Louis exhibition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WAGES IN THE IRON TRADE.</head>
        <p>WAGES IN THE IRON TRADE.</p>
        <p>Operatives Condemn the Sliding Scale.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO TERMINATE THE AGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>Representatives of the ironworkers throughout the area of the Midland Iron and Steel Wages Board met at. Wednesbury on Saturday to report the judgment of their constituents as to the sliding scale by which wages have been regulated since 1887, when the Board was established. The decision is of great moment, the area concerned embracing Lancashire and South Yorkshire, in addition to North and South Staffordshire, Derby- shire, East Worcestershire, and Shropshire. The voting had been in progress in the districts for some time past, the questions which the man were called upon to answer being—(l) Are you in favourof continuing the Midland sliding scale? (2) Are you in favour of terminating the Midland sliding scale by giving the necessary three months' notice to the employers? Little doubt had been entertained of what the reply would be, as it was well known there had been for years a growing feeling among the men and their leaders that the existing arrangement was not fairly applicable to the altered conditions of the trade. What the workers have urged is that in recent years the course of events in the Midland iron trade has militated against them,while•developments in the North have favoured the operatives, thus placing the workers of the Midlands at a further disadvantage. Since the sliding scale was adopted, the number of Midland iron- masters producing marked bars has declined, and this has resulted in a lower average selling price in the district and also in lower wages, which are regulated by the selling price. Concurrently the tendency in the North had been to produce more high-class iron. Low- priced iron rails, which formerly depressed the average, have now been superseded by steel rails, and the mills have been utilised for the production of bar iron. The men claim that the inequality caused by these dis- i placements of trade shall be redressed. At present the rate of wages for puddling is Bs. 6d. per ton, or 6d. more than in the Northern iron trade; but it is Attended that in the North theconditions of work are much more favourable, and that the extra output is a great deal more than equivalent to the difference of 6d. Midland employers have for some time paid 3d. above the scale. The basis of the Midland sliding scale is that for every sovereign which the manufacturer receives for the iron produced the operative shall have is., together with a premium of is. 6d. per ton and an additional 6d. to cover certain "extras," of which Northern iron- workers have the advantage. The gelling price is ascer- tained at intervals of two months by an examination of the books of certain selected firms, in order to obviate the difficulty of inspecting the books of all the 158 firms associated with the Board. The agreement was that the employers should name half the firms on whose sales the ascertainment should be based and that the operatives should choose the other half. The operatives complain that this arrangement has not worked satis- factorily, and that it has led to fluctuations out of all proportion to the actual variations of selling pricas. Recently there was an attempt on the part of some of the employers to disembarrass themselves of the 6d. paid in lieu of Northern extras, and previously there had been trouble over the same point. The meeting on Saturda3 comprised delegates from the various ironworkers' lodges throughout South Staffordshire, East Worcestershire, and Shropshire. The operatives in the other districts are not subscribing members of the Wages Board, but the question as to the sliding scale was submitted to them a month ago. when they agreed to be bound by the decision of the Wednes- bury meeting. The number of the men is about 20,000. Mr. W. Aucwtt, president of the men's organisation and joint secretary to the Wages Board, toott the chair. At the close of the proceedings it was announced that the following resolution had been passed and ordered to be communicated to the employers' secretary, Mr. Daniel Jones :—" That three months' notice be given to termin- ate the present sliding scale agreement, and that the board's accountant be instructed to discontinue taking out the returns of selling prices from the books of the firms now comprised within the audit after the two months ending October 311904."! The decision was almost unanimous, only three lodges voting in favour of the retention of the present. sliding scale. Mr. J. Cox, general secretary of the Iron and Steel Workers' Association, afterwards stated that the opetatives were thoroughly tired of the tactics by which the employers had worked the Wages Board. Whether it would be possible to agree upon a new basis was it, some minds doubtful, but he had confidence in the ability of representatives of the employers and the operatives to find common ground of agreement. MR COX AND THE SITUATION. rnterviewed upon the present situation. Mr. J. Cox General Secretary of the Iron and Steel Workers' Association, said the vote of the workmen was just what he expected. The last attempt of the Employers to reduce Midland puddlers below the rates existing throughout the North of England, following upon many previous similar attedipts, had strained their relations nearly to breaking point. We are still firm believers in and advocates of an automatic regulation of wages, and our present action is taken because of this belief; to have a sliding scale which is inoperative nearly one-half of the time, brought such regulutions 'of wages into dispute. No future scale would be acceptable the returns of • which did not cover a largerarea of works—they would also have to be of a substantial character and fairly repre- sentative of the trade. Let a really representative basis be secured and there should be no difficulty in fixing the necessary premium. Fifteen years ago the scale was first fixed with a premium of is. 9d. The em- ployers terminated that after only a few months trial daring an abnormal condition of trade. It is an inter- esting and astonishing fact that over the last six or seven years (eliminating the mad rush in prices of 1900) that premium of is. 9d. would have given an average of wages for puddling equal to that obtaining in the North of England and Scotland. The Employers admit the right of the puddlers to this wage, but they refuse to give or continue the premium which over such a long period would have secured it. They prefer to talk about a premium of is. 6d., and be constantly suspending the scale to make up wages which the premium of is. 6d. is inadequate to give, and then expect itt, to look upon their action as philan- thropic! This is derogatory to all principles of sliding scales. We won't have wages as a philanthropic gift, but as a right, and that is why w.: are abolishing the present scale. The difference in policy between the employers and the, workingmen is this :—They are con- stantly endeavourinp by a re-shuffling of the firms from which the prices are taken to make the selling price " fit " an imaginary premium of is. 6d., whilst we are contending for a real solid representative basis of the selling price and then adjust the necessary premium which will place us on an equal footing with other dis- tricts. Many anomalies have also crept into the sliding scale basis which it is necessary to correct in the best interests of the trade and this can only be accomplished by a total abolition of the scale. Whether it would be possible to agree opon a new basis is in some minds doubtful, but I have confidence in the common sense of both employers and operative representatives and their ability to find common ground of agreement.</p>
        <p>There is one place where cleanliness is compulsory— the workhouse. For refusing to have her head washed on admission a woman at Tottenham on Thursday was sent to prison for a week. She pleaded that she had come direct from the sick asylum, and had had her head washed once that day.</p>
        <p>PROV[SIONS ARE DEARER.---Advt,</p>
      </div>
