      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A FORGIVING BROTHER. At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, William Caldwell,a young man living at Hart's-place,was brought up on a warrantoharging him with robbing his brother Arthur.—The Chief Constable said the prosecutor, who was a cab driver, went away for a short holiday last week. Prisoner, who was a bit of a " ne'er do well" was on the spree with.a number of boon companions of a like character. While the prosecutor was away prisoner stole his savings, amounting to £7, from a cash box. He and his companions spent the money at Doncaster races, no doubt in dissolute living—banking slow horses and patronizing fast dissi- pations. Prosecutor, who was a very steady and respectable man. took out a warrant for his brother's arrest. The result was that the prodigal brother,having spent the whole of the stolen substance, came back penitent and gave himself up to the police. Prosecutor, in the spirit of forgiveness, now wished the magistrates to let the prisoner off, and he was willing to withdraw the charge.—Arthur Caldwell, the prosecutor, said he did not want to proceed with the case for his mother's sake. He was prepared to pay the costs (Hs. 6d.). ' The magistrates agreed to the withdrawal, but warned the prisoner that if ever he came before them again he I would be severely dealt with. SAD STORY OF HOME LIFE.</p>
        <p>Bridget Nevin, of Winwick-road, was Charged at the Warrington Police Court on Monday with neglecting her two children.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C.—Prisoner at the outset asked for the ease to be adjourned,but the magistrates proceeded with the hearing. Mr. Browne said the evidence was so over- whelming and unanswerable that no object would be served by adjourning the case. The husband said his wife came home drunk at midnight on Sunday, and went out again at five o'clock that morning. Defendant's husband, who was a plasterer, recently obtained a separation on the ground that his wife was an habitual drunkard. Defendant then went to live with her brothers, but in May,one of them died, and she was such a nuisance that the other brothers requested her husband to take her away un- til after the funeral. He did so, and the woman was drunk almost the whole day. She persuaded her husband to allow her to live with him again, and she had hardly ever been sober since. Mr. Taylor, P.C.M. and Inspector Hackett had visited the house dozens of times. Defendant would go out all day leaving her two little children to the mercy of the neighbours, and the house was in a filthy condition. Twenty-seven tiokets, relating to articles which she had pawned since May, were produced. Defendant had beaten one of the children and ran her out of the house in the presence of Inspector Hackett.—The magistrates asked Mr. Taylor if a home could be found for the woman, and Mr. Taylor replied that she ought really to be sent to an inebriates' home for three years.—Michael Nevin, defendant's husband, said his wife had pawned everything in the house, including the bedclothes. She had thrashed her daughter for giving evidence mainst her on a previous occasion.—lnspector Hackett said he visited the house in June and defendant came downstairs in a helpless state of drunkenness. The house was in a filthy condition. On a subsequent visit he found defendant quite sober, and she said she was going to reform. -On August 20th, however, the house was not fit for pigs to live in. Last Wednesday the family appeared to be fairly comfortable, but when he spoke to the defendant he observed that she had had drink. He told her that her husband wished her to have another chance, and she broke out into such a fury that he thought she was mad. It was enough to frighten the children to death. The house and children were in a filthy state.—Prisoner told the magistrates that she wanted to be separated from her husband again. She was comfortable away from him.—Mr. Sexton, the defendant's brother, undertook to try and induce her to go into a home, and on this understanding the Bench adjourned the case for a week. WARRINGTON .POACHER HEAVILY FINED. -</p>
        <p>At the Daresb-ury Petty Sessions on Tuesday,Richard Hughes, 83, Plnmpton-street, Warrington, was charged with unlawfully being in search of game in Moore-lane, Moore, and also with being in possession of seven rabbits, nets, and pegs.—Police-constable Bailey stated that at two o'clock on the 3rd inst. he saw the defend- ant in company with another man. On going up to them they ran away. He followed, and the defendant dropped a net 63 yards long, eight pegs, and a bag con- taining seven rabbits. He afterwards caught the de- fendant. Superintendent Ennion read a long list of convictions against the prisoner, who is a native of Birmingham, from which it appeared that he had been Sentenced to long terms of imprisonment at the Birmingham and Warwick Assizes and other places.— The prisoner pleaded that he had lived an honest life for five years.—He was fined the maximum penalty of .E 5 with the option of two months' imprisonment. MA.GISTRATE AND ANTI-VACCINATOR.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Arthur Frederick Hassall, poultryman at Massey Hall,Thelwall, was summoned for neglecting to have his child vacci- nated. He admitted the charge.—Mr. De Prez, vaccination officer, stated that at the end of four months, the time allowed by the Act for children to be vaccinated, defendant refused to let his child be operated upon. He served defendant with a notice to have the child vaccinated within fourteen days, but the instruc- tion was not obeyed.--Defendant said that he intended to get an exemption order, but did not know there was a time limit. He had four other children who had not been vaccinated.—The Chairman (Mr. W. Long): Don't you recognise you have a duty to the public as well as to your children?—Defendant : No, sir.—The Chair- man: We shall have to show you.—A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL ASSAULT ON A SISTER.-SMART SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>_ At the Warrington Police Court, on Wednesday, be- fore Alderman Sutton and other magistrates, James Grady, Battersby-lane, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Battersby7lane at 10.15 on Tuesday evening and with assaultirtt Annie Butler.— P.C. Hewitt said he was on 'duty in Battersby-lane when be was told that a man was murdering his sister. On proceeding towards Butler's house he found a large crowd assembled. The woman was in a deplorable condition, and when witness asked prisoner why he had illused her be replied "If you'll take your coat off I'll do you the same." Butler's face was cut and scarred all over, and she stated that the reason prisoner had thrashed her was that his tea was not ready when he came in.—Complain-</p>
        <p>No Breakfast Table complete without</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EPPS'S</head>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
