      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Wilkinson and Thornton Messrs. Birchall, Wright, Hayward, and W. Roberts. AN OLD OFFENDER.—Elizabeth Kearns,32, Watkin-st., was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Silver-street at 7.35 on Saturday evening.—P.C. Plumb proved the case.—The Chief Constable said prisoner made her 27th appearance. She was before the court less than a month ago for drunkenness. Fined ss. and costs. _ _</p>
        <p>A Roiniar PRISONER.—WiIIiam Cotton, a stoker, last from Havdock, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Old Road at 10.20 on Saturday night and with assaulting P.C.'s Reddington and Tolly. He denied the assault—P.C. Reddington stated that he was on duty in Knutsford-road when he heard a disturbance taking place in Old Road. On proceeding there he found the prisoner in the midst of a crowd. He was very drunk and was threatening what he would do for his wife who lived apart. from him. A young man took hold of prisoner and tried to get him away, but prisoner broke from him, exclaiming " I'll do for that copper !" Witness took him int," custody and he became very violent, throwing himself on the ground and kicking out right and left. Witness received several kicks on the legs. Prisoner was handcuffed but he was so rough that P.C. Tolly and Train Conductor Holland had to assist in carrying him to the Police Station.—Prisoner kicked P.C. Tolly violently about the legs.—Prisoner said he had been badly used by the officers, his wrists and face being marked.—The Chief Constable: He can't expect us to provide a motor car for him. Prisoner, continued Mr. Talbot, had been before the Bench 21 times and had served several terms of imprisonment for assaults. He was one of the most desperate and dangerous characters in the town when in drink. He might have injured P.C. Tolly for life.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour, and the magistrates expressed their thanks to Conductor Holland. LIVELY STREET SCENE.—Kate Foxhall and Mary Ann Tarpey, sisters, 106, Pierpoint-street, were sum- moned for committing a breach of the peace by being disorderly in Catherine-Egreet on the 4th inst.—P.S. Smith said Foxhall was blackguarding her husband on Bewsey Bridge, and the man complained that Tarpey was creating a disturbance in Catherine-street. Witness proceeded there and Foxhall followed him and used bad language. He told her that if she did not go home he would lock her up, and she went away. Having dis- persed the crowd witness again went into Bewsey-road, but on hearing that the disturbance continuel he returned to Catherine-street with P.C.'s Johnson and Sankey. Defendants were standing in the middle of the street, shouting, hooting and waving their arms. On being told that the police were coining the defen- dants made off down an entry. Witness followed Tarpey and gotitheir names and addresses.—There was another charge against Foxhall of breaking windows. The magistrates bound defendants over to keep the peace and ordered them to pay the costs (7s. each.) BREACHES or THE PEA.CE.—nliam Ball and Sarah Jane Ball, 8, Orchard street, were charged with dis- orderly behavionr in Orchard street, on September 4th. Detective Clough proved the case, and defendants were bound over and ordered to pay the costs. Thomas Mitchell, 20, Ship Yard, and Peter Doyle 21, Ship Yard, were fined ss. and costs for fighting. WITHDRAVIN.—Peter Scully, a well-known character, was charged on a warrant with having used threats to- wards John Horn on May I.—Mr. Horn now stated that he did not wish to proceed with the case and the magis- trates agreed to a withdrawal on defendant promising to pay the costs (4s. 6d.) THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. John Fairclough (pre- siding), C. White, J. R. Young, and W. Roberts. HUNGRY . YOUTH'S DISAPPOINTMENT.—A youth named Thomas Conway, last from Manchester, was charged witti stealing a biscuit tin, value 6d., the property of James Rudd, grocer, 65, Hamilton-street.— Inspector Phillips said that prosecutor was in his parlour when prisoner and. two companions entered the shop. Prisoner acted suspiciously and when the youths badeleft prosecutor missed the biscuit tin which was empty. P.C. Plumb was sent for and arrested the prisoner who said he stole the tin because he was hungry and thought it contained biscuits.—Thetaagis- trates recorded a conviction and discharged the prisoner with a warning. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Thornton, Tinnion, Roberts, and Wilkinson, Messrs. H. Roberts, Pilling, Burgess, Reynolds and Jolley. A ROUGH PRISONER.—Bridget Taylor. of no settled abode was fined ss. and costs for having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street at 9.5 on Thursday evening.—P.C. Turner said several tradesmen complained of prisoner's conduct. She was so violent that she had to be taken to the lock-up in a furniture cart. ALLEGED SAVAGE ASSAULT.—John Henry Rowe, 26, Golborne-street, was summoned for assaulting Mary Ellen Whitney, 10, Hanover-street, on September 12th— Defendant did not appear—Mr. C. H. Unsworth (for Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the complainant and said the assault was a very cowardly and brutal one. On Mon- day, defendant, who was employed at the Castle Rubber Works, made use of an offensive expression to com- plainant's daughter, who had been to take breakfast to her father who was also engaged at the works Mrs. Whitney went to def endant's lodgings to have the matter out with him, and she was told that Rowe was in bed. She remarked to the landlady (Mrs. Clare) that defendant was a scamp, and defendant evidently heard the conversation for he came downstairs, rushed at Mrs. Whitney, and struck her in the face with his fist, injuring her eye. Mrs. Clare commenced to bathe complainant's face, which was bleeding, and defendant again struck Mrs. Whitney several times. It was only when Mrs. Clare pulled him away that he let her alone, and she was taken home in a cab.—The magistrates issued a warrant for defendant's arrest. CASE DISMISSED.—John Hennessey, 3, Pocket-st., was summoned for assaulting Winifred Hennessey, his wife, on. September 1 lth.—Defendant denied the offence and said his. wife had been drinking ever since she came out of prison about a week ago.—The case aas dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>NO WAGES REQUIRED.</head>
        <p>NO WAGES REQUIRED.</p>
        <p>What a statement! and how well calculated to gladden the hearts of employers of labour. But say a bit. We are not tAlking of the entire man or woman; we refer only to he stomach. We say only; but the word seems an insult to a part of our organism so important to our well-being that it has but to get out of working order, be it ever so slightly, for life to lose its charm. The stomach is a wonderful servant which, in health, works for us without ceasing, taking no wages for itt-Servic'e;- • merely asking to be fed and regulated according to a few simple natural rules. When all or any of those rules are violated, the stomach protests ; and its protest takes the form of indigestion. "For many years," writes Mrs. B. White, of 70, High street, Hastings, in a letter dated March Bth, 1897, "I suffered from acute indigestion. From time to time my appetite was poor, and eating always produced pain at the chest. I tried several kinds of medicine, but failed to find much benefit until I ' began to take Mother Seigel's Syrup. After the first bottle of that wonderful medicine, my appetite began to. improve. I not only desired to eat, but food agreed with me. Gradually the tormenting pain at my chest ceased, and my original good health was completely re-established." Strength and energy are not the same thing, though many people seem to regard them as such. Strength is physical ability; energy is the mental disposition to use it. Strength is opposed to -weakness; energy to laziness. But to have energy one must have strength, while to have strength the body must be nourished with plenty of good food. This, of course: can never be the case if the stomach, from any cause, is unable to do its work properly. When this unhappy condition is set up (as in the rush and scramble of modern life it very fre- quently is),Mother Seigel's Syrup is its n4y cure.We have seen what that medicine did for Mrs. Mite seven years ago. Let us now see whether the good it did was merely ---...--. i temporary relief or permanent cure. Here is another I --ID— letter from Mrs. White—dated April 15th, 1904 -in 1 FArLuREs IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to I which she says: "Though so many years have passed "Hemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of 1 The Late Mr. Jenner Fust. since Mother se gel's Syrup relieved me of indigestion failures recorded in England and Wales during the week; probate of the will of Herbert Jenner Fust, L.L.D., o the .• . . and its attendant ills, I continue to enjoy excellent ending Friday, Sep. 9th, was 128, viz.:—New Bankruptcy ' formerly a Doctors Commons advocate, and president health. My appetite is all that can be desired, and I Proceedings published in the London Gazette 62; , have no need to exercise special care in the choice of Deeds of Arrangement registered. 66. The respective fM C C (believed to be the oldest cricketer in diet." numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: time of his death at the age of ninety- All servants should be treated well; policy and Bankruptcies, 64; Deeds of Arrangement, 71—Total eight last July), has been granted to his son, Mr. humanity demand no less. But a servant like the 135; being a net increase in Bankruptcies of 226, and Herbert Jenner Fust, Local Government Board stomach that asks for no wages, works all the time a net increase in Deeds of Arrangement in 1904, to Inspector for the North- Western District. The testa- and is essential to our very life, surely deserves special date of 462. I tor's gross estate is sworn at £43,464. consideration. I</p>
        <p>A YOUNG man sent his father, an old farmer in the country his photograph, accomponied with a request for aid as he was poor. The old man looked at the photograph, and then responded: "You can't cheat me, you yeung dog. You can't be very poor to be livin' among them marble vases, 'nd statues, 'nd flowers, 'nd nice furniture, such as your photoi;raph shows." TIEE REASON WHY.--7A ,schoolmaster in a village school had been in the habit of purchasing pork from parents of his pupils on the occasion of the killing of the pig. One day a small boy marched up to the master's desk, und inquired "if he would like a bit of pork, as they were going to kill their. pig ? " The schoolmaster replied in the affirmative. Several clays having elapsed, and hearing nothing of the pork, the master called the boy up to him, and inquired the reason he had not brought it. "Oh, -please, sir," the bey replied. "the pig got better.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>_ „,„ --. •</p>
        <p>Itv 00 1</p>
        <p>This delicious and highly nutritive Food has been used with remarkable success in the rearing of Infants, and by delicate and aged per- sons for many years. It is distinguished bythe ease with which it can be digested and absorbed, and is quite different from any other Food obtainable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAfIIS.</p>
        <p>WHITE lies are not spotless. A PAIR of tight shoes can make one forget any other misery in the world. WHEN a girl finds her beau ideal of a man, she Wants him for her ideal beau.</p>
        <p>A GIRL of sixteen can say "all my life" in a more impressive way than can a man of eighty. A FISH seldom gets into trouble if it keeps it's mouth shut—and'' the same might be said of a man.</p>
        <p>"THEY say Burgess got a barrel of money when his uncle died." "Yes, but he has only got the bung-hole left." JUDGE: Do you know that drink drives a man to the devil? Prisoner: Yes, your honour; it brought me before you. "Mi. SMITH, do you want me to adopt rational dress ? " 'No, my clear • but I'd like you to wear irrational dress at rational prices." A MAN is in love with his wife so long as it doesn't occur to him that he is getting nothing out of his salary but his board and clothes. HER OBJECTION.—He "This story will make your hair stand on end." She: "I hope not. I should look such a fright !" "OUR orchestra has gone to pieces." "What was the matter?" "The leader trained us to play as one man, and then wanted to pay us that way." CANDOUR.—Here the Candid Lady drew near to the Floor Walker. "I have kleptomania," she said. "What would you advise me to take for •</p>
        <p>TODDIE : "I wish I'd a-been one of Noah's sons." Budgie: " Why ? " Toddie : "Just think of spendin' forty days in such a big menagerie as that!"</p>
        <p>Mus. DIMPLETON : "I want you to get another doctor." Dimpleton : "What's the matter with this one?" Mrs. Dimpleton : "What do you suppose he said about baby? He told me I must treat him like a human being." MRs. NEIGHBOURS: "I understand your cook has given you notice. What's the trouble?" Mrs. Suburban: "I don't know, but I think she doesn't like my cooking." MRS. HOON (indignantly) "I wonder why Mrs. Gabbleton doesn't mind her own business?" Mr. Hoon : "One reason is that she has no mind, and another is that she has no business."</p>
        <p>WIFE (at breakfast) : "I want to do somo shopping to-day, dear, if the weather is favour- able. What are the forecasts ? " Husband (con- sulting his paper) : "Rain, hail, thunder, and lightning MAGGIE : "Mamma, what is repartee ? " Mamma: "Repartee, my daughter, is a very clever answer when you say it to anybody, but a very rude answer when anybody says it to you."</p>
        <p>.7 -- THE BRUTE.—Mrs. Cobwigger—" Are you sure, Henry, you'll be perfectly satisfied to have me in the country while you stay in the town?" Cob- wigger : "Yes, my dear. The rest will do me a world of good." NussiNs (to his next door neighbour): "Hey, there! what are you burying in that hole?" Neighbour: "Oh, 1m just replanting some of my garden seeds." Nubbins: "Garden seeds eh; Looks to me like one of my liens!" Neighbour: "That's all right. The seeds are inside."</p>
        <p>"EXCUSE me, but it seems to me that I must have met you before. Are you not a brother or near relative of Major Jones ?" "No ; I am Major Jones hiniself.!' " Ah, indeed! That explains the remarkable resemblance."</p>
        <p>DR. ENDS: There is nothing serious the matter with Freddie, Mrs. Blackly. I think a little soap and water will do him as much good as anything. Mrs. Blakly : Yes, doctor, an' will I give it to him before or after his meals? THEIR DIVERGENCE.—" How did those • two delightful young people happen to drift apart?" "Most natural thing in the world. He was after a heart and she a banking account. Time deve- loped the fact that neither of them had what the other wanted."</p>
        <p>"COME and dine with us to-morrow," said the old fellow who ha-I made his money and wanted to push his way into society. "Sorry," replied the elegant man, "I can't. I'm going to see Hamlet.' "That's all right," said the hospitable old gentle- man," "bring him with you." A POOR fellow who owed meney, and was often sued, being rather out at elbows, a friend gravely remarked that he was much surprised to see him dress so shabbily, as he understood that he had a new suit every day.</p>
        <p>- . AS FAR As HE WENT.—" I asked you if I could sue on that claim," said the disappointed litigant to his lawyer, "and you said I could." "True," admitted the lawyer, "but I didn't say you could win."</p>
        <p>" THAT'S the best I can do for you," said the theatrical manager. "You've been idle all the season so far. Now, will you stay idle the rest of the season or take this small part ? ' "I'll take it," said Lowe Comerdy. "In this case a small role is better than a whole loaf."</p>
        <p>A COUNT TO THE FINISH. —A: "Well, and how did you sleep last night? Did you follow my ad- vice and begin counting?" B: " Yes ; I counted up to eighteen thousand." A: "And then you fell asleep?" B: "No ; then it was time to get up." LECTURER: "And what man is most apt to reach that elevation whence the earth may be viewed 'as one vast plain ?' " Voice (in the audience) : "The one that works in a powder-</p>
        <p>HE (telling a hairbreadth adventure) : "And in the bright moonlight we could see the dark muzzles of the wolves." She (breathlessly) : "Oh, how glad you must have been that they had the muzzles on ! " _ . _</p>
        <p>_ MATRIMONIAL ITEM.—" Ah, John," she said, just before the marriage, "I fear I am not worthy of you. You are such a good man." "Never mind, Martha, I'll change all that after the wedding." LEFT FOR THE ENGINE. —Uncle : "What are you doing, Tommy?" Tommy: "Drawing on my slate." Uncle: "What's that supposed to repre- sent?" Tommy: "A locomotive." Uncle: "Why don't you draw the carriage?" Tommy: "Oh, the locomotive draws them.' IN THE DARK. —Mr. Hisheollar "Your brother tells tee that young Sapleigh has monopolised the park:fox every night this week. I should think you would get sick of the sight of him." Miss Albany : "Why, Mr. Highcollar, I don't see Percy more than 10 minutes from the time he comes in till the time he goes out." "WHAT do I get on that?" inquired the hungry-looking individual as he tossed his over- coat on the counter of , the pawnshop. "You get some fresh paint on it," answered the pawnbroker, cheerily.; "that counter has just been painted." WHERE CHOPS COME FROM. —Sadie was eleven and Alice was seven. At lunch said Alice, "I wonder what part of an animal a chop is. Is it a leg?" "Of course not," returned Sadie. "It's the jaw-bone. Haven't you ever heard of animals theirlicking chops ? " An Englishman travelling in the North came to a ford, and hired a boat to take him across. The water being more agitated than was agreeable to him, he asked the boatman if any person was ever lost on the passage. "Never," replied the man; "My brother was drowned here last week, but we found him again next day.' HOPWELL : 'Living in the country now, I understand. What sort of a place is it?" Subbub :"I really don't know. It takes me so lono. to get home that I never arrive till after dark, and I have to leave before daybreak to reach the office in time."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EDUCATION IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Committee Meeting.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Education Committee will be held on Monday evening, when the business to be transacted will include the following :</p>
        <p>NEW APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee recommend that consent be given to the appointment of the under- mentioned teachers, their engagements to be determin- able, except in the case of the teacher appointed on supply, by one month's notice, to expire oa the last day of any calanderlmonth, given by or to the Committee, viz. :—Ladies' School of Industry, Mrs. Adah Griffiths, cert. assistant (supply), £B5; Silver-street Wesleyan (mixed), Miss Margaret( A. Griffiths, cert. assistant, £55; Warrington British (mixed), Mr. Joel South worth, cert. assistant, £9O; Wycliffe British (mixed), Mr. Richard E._Burtt, cert. assistant, £lOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>TRAINING OF PUPIL TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>THE TRAINING OF PUPIL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>It having been reported that the resolutian of the committee with reference to the schools selected as being suitable for the training of pupil teachers therein, was not approved by the Education Committee at their last meeting, the committee again took the matter into their consideration, and it was resolved that application be made to H.M. Inspector for permission to include the Silver-street Wesleyan school among those selected by him in which pupil teachers may be trained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PHYSICAL EXERCISES FOR CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>PHYSICAL EXERCISES FOR CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>A communication from the Board of Education— drawing attention to certain reeommendations made by the inter-departmental committee on the model course of physical exercises, and forwarding a copy of the syllabus of physical exercises for use in public elemen- tary schools which had been prepared in accordance with those recommendations—having been submitted,it was resolved, that the Director be requested to draw up, for the consideration of the committee, proposals for carrying out the suggestions contained in the recom- mendations and syllabus, and that a copy of the sylla- bus be provided to each of the head teachers in the elementary schools.</p>
        <p>THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL. SUGGESTED CONTRIBUTION FROM THE RATES.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Board of Education, with reference to the Warrington Grammar school and to the scheme for the supply of education other than elementary, suggesting (a) an amendment of the scheme for the election of the governors of the school (b) a contribu- tion from the committee to the funds of the school, was submitted, and after some discussion, it was reslved that arrangements be made for a conference between the governors of the Grammar school and the School Management Committee on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>PUPIL TEACHERS FROM OUTSIDE THE BOROUGH.</head>
        <p>PUPIL TEACHERS FROM OUTSIDE THE BOROUGH.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee recommend that for the future,the charge to be made for the instruction of pupil teachers from the administrative areas of Lancashire and Cheshire at the committee's classes be £3 per annum in the case of each pupil teacher,and that the grant payable by the Board of Education in respect of the same be retained by the committee. The Director reported that the County Authorities were desirous, if possible, of sending first year pupil teachers who are between the ages of 15 and 16 years to the pupil teachers' classes for half-time instruction, but, in view of the pressure upon the accommodation of the Second- ary school, the committee could not see their way to entertain this suggestion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL ASSISTANT MASTERSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL ASSISTANT MASTERSHIP.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee proceeded to con- sider the applications received for the post of assistant master at the Secondary school,in place of Mr. Buxton, resigned, and it was resolved that a sub-committee, consisting of Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. J. Upson and the Chairman (the Rev. Morley Stevenson, be appointed to go through the applications, and, after interviewing the candidates selected by them, to make the appoint- ment. SECONDARY SCHOOL AND TECHNICAL INSTITUTE SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee recommend that Kendall Williamson be appointed laboratory assist- ant at the Secondary school from the 30th August, 1904, at a weekly wage of 55.; that the salary of Miss Beatrice M. Watkins, assistant cookery mistress at the Technical Institute, be increased from £7O to £BO per annum, as from the 24th August, 1904; that the salaries of Miss M. B. Morton, L.L.A.,and Miss Elizabeth Frost, assistant mistresses at the Secondary school, be increased from £llO to £ll5 per annum, as from the 15th and 21st September, 1901, respectively, and there- after by £5 per annuminntil the maximum of £l3O is reached; that Miss Elizabeth Arnold, Miss Amelia, Harrison, and Miss Josephine M. Lawrenson be appoin- ted assistant teachers at the FairileldEvening Continua- tion school, at a salary in each case at the rate of ss. per evening, together with a bonus of 3s. 6d. for every student making 75 per cent. of the possible number of attendances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>SCHOLARSHIPS.</head>
        <p>SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee recommend that not MOTO than 75 scholarships, providing free tuition at the Technical Institute and School of Art, be awarded to certain of last session's students at the Evening Con- tinuation schools.</p>
        <p>NB W APPOINTMENTS FOR THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>A letter having been submitted from Mr. G. G. Sumner, intimating his inability to accept the appoint- ment of teacher of !applied mechanics, steam, and geometry at the Technical Institute, it was resolved that Mr. W. H. Bedford be offered the post of teacher of geometry in addition to machine construction, and that his salary be increased from £46 to £6O for the session; and that Mr. W. A. Littlejohn be appointed teacher of applied mechanics and steam at a salary at the rate of £3l 10s. for the session. The School Management Committee further recom- mend that advanced physiology and sick nursing be added to the subjects of instruction at the Technical Institute during the coming session ; that Mr. A. Hodgkinson, M. 8., Ch.B., M.Sc., and Mr. George H. Leigh, M. 8., Ch.B., be appointed lecturers for each subject, respectively; that in consequence, the • salary of the former be increased from £33 to £49 10s.; and the, the salary of the latter be at the rate of £l6 16s, for the session. It was further resolved that arrangements be made for evening classes to be held for the instruction of the teachers in the elementary schools in nature study, and that the Director be empowered to negotiate for a teacher for the classes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SCHOOL OF ART APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL OF ART APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>It was noted that a free studentship at the School of Art had been awarded by the Board of Education to I Thomas B. Whitby. t was resolved that Dobson Thomas Clark and Jane E. Pearson be appointed pupil teachers at the School of Art for the coming year, at a salary in each case at the rate of £l5 per annum • and Miss Emily Plinston be appointed to assist in teaching the pupil and acting teachers at the School of Art on Saturday mornings, at a salary at the rate of £l5 per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRITISH SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRITISH SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A circular letter from the Board of Education, approving the plans for the improvement of the light- ing, ventilation, and offices at the Warrington British school, was submitted to the Sites and Buildings Com- mittee and noted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>THF APPORTIONMENT OF THE COST OF PAINTING.</head>
        <p>THF APPORTIONMENT OF THE COST OF PAINTING.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Committee recommend that, in the case of those schools which are used by the managers for purposes other than those of public elementary schools, one-seventh of the cost of the annual painting and decorating of the schools shall be borne by the managers. A statement showing the amounts to be charged to the managers of the schools at which the annual painting and decorating during the present year has been dons by the committee, and the amount to be. paid to the managers who have carried out the work themselves, was submitted and approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL AT LATCEIFOR-D.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL AT LATCEIFOR-D.</p>
        <p>TO BE CALLED "THE BOLTON SCHOOL." - A letter, dated the 7th September, from the Board of Education, forwarding the formal approval of the site for the proposed new school in Latchford, was sub- mitced to the Sites and Buildings Committee and noted and it was resolved that the Law Clerk be directed to give the necessary notice, under Section 8 of the Educa- tion Act, 1902, of the committee's intention to erect a public elementary school in the Latchford district, and also one to supply part of Bewsey, Orford, and Fairfield wards. The committee then proceeded to consider the steps to be taken with a view to the appointment of an architect for the erection of the proposed new schools, and it was resolved, that a sub-committee, consisting of Mr. Councillor Evans, Mr. Councillor Tinnion Mr. Richard T. Fairclough, and the Chairman (the Mayor), be appointed to make the preliminary arrangements, and to visit some recently built schools in other towns, in order to make themselves acquainted with the latest developments of school architecture. It was also resolvecithat the new school to be erected in the Latchford district be called "The Bolton school, Latchford."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>E.PENSE OF DRAINAGE.</head>
        <p>THE E.PENSE OF DRAINAGE.</p>
        <p>A letter was submitted to the Sites and Buildings Committee from the managers of the Silver-street Wesleyan school, inquiring as to the responsibility for the payment for the alterations required by the Medical Officer of Health in connection with the drainage of that school, and the Director was requested to intimate, in reply, that this is an expense which must be met out of funds provided by the managers.</p>
        <p>BAKE A CAKE. -It's easy, it's economical, it's delic- ious—lf you use KEENORA prepared Cake Flour. It's a preparation of the finest and purest ingredients and simplifies the art of .cake-making so that a child could make the most delicic.ms cakes. Directions for making cakes are enclosed with each packet. Free half-pound packet on receipt of 2d. stamps for postage.—KEENORA C0.,38, Sir Thomas Street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. Meeting_ of the General Committee.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. Meeting_ of the General Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting Of the General Committee of the Northern Union was held at the Grosvenor Hotel, Manchester, on Tuesday evening, Mr. F. Lister (Bradford), presiaing. An application was made for re-instatement by D. Rees, formerly of Llwynypia, and now of Salford, who had been suspended by the Welsh Rugby Union for misconduct on the field. The application was adjourned to the next meeting in order that the Secretary may obtain full partimulars of the case from the Welsh Rugby Union. REGISTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>The following players were placed on the registers of the clubs first-named :</p>
        <p>Barrow.—W. Park (Dalton), A. Brase (Weston), J. Lathom (-A.skham), J. Bower and J. Poose (Roose). Batley.—K. Hainsworth (Holbeck). Bradford.—J. Cordingley (Baildon). Birkenhead.—T. Newbould (Brassey-street Mission), and A. Dunbavand (Castner Kellner). Brighouse Rangers.—T. Nicholl (Thrum Hall), F. Law (Pontefract), T. C. Pinder, H. Gibson, S. Sidebottom, J. A. Holroyd, G. H. Slack, E. Waldsworth, and W. Rawlinson (local). Broughton Rangers.—J. Ferguson (Broughton Sp. James's), and James Smith (Hartlepool Rovers). Dewsbury.—J. Sutcliffe and J. W. Dawson (Holbeck), H. Rushworth (Dewsbury Celtic), and .1. Wilkinson (Holbeck). Leeds.—J. Ames (Leeds Freehold), G. A. B. Cole (Hunslet), and T. Bowen (Holbeck). Morecambe.—D. Townson (Warrington) and J. Bartholomew (local). Lancaster.—G. G. Philipson and W. Craig (Holme). Normanton.—G. Dennett (Sharlston). Pontefract.—G. W. Masora (Wakefield). Runcorn.—J. R. Massey (Runcorn Reserves). Rochdale Hornets.—E. Morton (Halifax), and T. Smith (local). St. Helens.—A. A. Routledge (Rockcliffe) and W. Murray (Warrington). Swinton.—H. Byrnes (Broughton Hornets). Wakefield Trinity.—H. Measure (Hartlepool). Warrington.—J. Davies (Penygraig). Widnes.—J. Peers and T. Daley (local).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Warrington Players Suspended. REFEREES AND FOOTBALL REPORTING.</head>
        <p>Warrington Players Suspended. REFEREES AND FOOTBALL REPORTING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Northern Union League Committee was held at the Grosvenor Hotel,Manohester,on Tuesday evening, Mr. Joseph Platt (Oldham) presiding. The resignation of the Holbeck Football Club was accepted. The following players were dealt with as follows : Greenland (Morecambe) for rough play, foul and abusive language to the touch judge, and refusing to leave the field or to give his name and address when requested to do so, was suspended up to and including December 31. For kicking in the match, Leigh v. Warringt3n, on September 3, J. Preston of the latter club,was suspended up to and including October 1. In the same match for fighting, D. Davies (Warrington) and J. Blackburn (Leigh) also received suspension up to October 1. In the match Dewsbury v. Keighley on September 3, H. Myers was suspended to September 24. It had been brought before the notice of the Com- mittee that a eertain nesrspaper had desired referees officiating in First Division Leagu&amp; matches to supply reports of the games. The Committee decided that their officials should not be allowed to furnish such ep arts.</p>
      </div>
