tcher, C.C., of Northwich, who was unable to attend through sickness. The hall is blame the system we have adopted. an admirable adjunct to the school, and will provide The committee were unanimously of opinion that the much-needed class-room accommodation. Mr. William matter should be investigated. Handley, who conducted the proceedings, narrated some ' of the life history of the late Miss Elizabeth Emery,and ; said the hall was built in fulfilment of her great desire to do something for Bethesda; and'he, as sole trustee to the deceased lady, Ind pleasure in handing the build- ing over to the deaccrts. A number of addresses were delivered. NEW BYLAWS FOR WIDNES. , A letter has been received by the Widnes Corporation from the Secretary. of State, intimating that the statu- tory requirements in respect of the new bylaws framed by the Council have been complied with, and that the bylaws are now in force in the borough. The new regulations include provisions for dealing with street betting, sale of racing tips, music near houses, churches hospitals. &amp;c., noisy excursionists, noisy hawking, touting, noises from organs, shooting galleries, Sm., indecent language, thteatening language, fighting, indecent bathing, indecent shows, indecency in public, wilful jostling, loitering near church doors, throwing orange peel and other dangerous substances and any sort of refuse or paper or broken glass on the foot walks and streets, carrying soot and undressed carcases, playing dangerous games in the street, spitting and other matters. REWARD FOR BRAVERY AT RUNCORN. Before the business at the Runcorn Petty Sessions was commenced on Monday, the Chairman (Mr. J. Ockleston) presented a certificate of the Royal Humane Society .to Patrick Naughton. The certificate was awarded to Naughton for his bravery in risking his own life ,to save that of a boy who got into difficulties while bathing in the river at Runcorn some little time ago. The Chairman described Naughton's conduct as noble. It was, he said, well-known that the bulk of people were chary about risking their own lives to save those of others. In commending Naughton for his bravery he spoke feelingly because his boy was saved from being drowned on one occasion as was the lad in this case. He could not find words to express his feeling of gratefulness to Naurhton for saving this lad's life and he hoped Naughton would always have the luck to avoid accidents of any description. Superintendent Ennion on behalf of the Chief Constable thanked the Chairman for making the presentation.</p>
      </div>
