        <p>s Very pretty co,llnoil, quit unknown to many :...!LY fair players.</p>
        <p>Diagram 8 shows a very pretty cannon quite Unknown- to many good players. The player i 8 in hand, the red ball is in baulk somewhere Sear the pocket, and the white is in a very safe osition about half an inch from the cushion. /lost players, if told that the stroke was a sannon twice across the table, would strike the sue ball with left hand side, aiming to hit tht. 0 object ball pretty full. Snell a cannon would Lie a very difficult one as the side on the ball it struck the opposite cushion would be CheckL side, and the tendency of this would be to militate against the cannon being made. The Correct way of playing this twice-across- the-table cannon is -to strike the , cue nal with plenty of right hand side 'and to hit the cushion just a shade in front Ili the object ball instead of hitting the ball 'greet. Played in this way, the side on the cue ball will be running side when the opposite cushion is struck (provided that this cushion is 'truck at a point nearer to baulk than the first cushion was, and .this side will, of course, carryi the cue ball across baulk. I don't mean for a koment to suggest that this cannon is an easy Ike, but it is by no means a very difficult one 4nd if moderately good players will try it, they. "ill find that they can get it fairly often. Owing ,te the situation of the red ball there are at least three chances of getting the cannon, as if the ens ball does not cannon direct on to the red the sa, mien may be made off either the side or the clishicn.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 9.</p>
        <p>jxamples of how side on a cue ball may be either „Inning side or check side, according to tlit: Which the ball strikes the cushion after con- 7_"t with another ball.</p>
        <p>As already mentioned, side on a cue ball may 12,tove to be either running side or cheek side, 'eeorcling to the angle at which a cushion is titruck by the cue ball after contact with an ect ball. •</p>
        <p>position A in diagram 9 shows this very early. Should the cue ball to which right hand 11,1de has been given, strike the cushion below "le intersected line, the side on the ball will be Nnning side, but should the ball strike the tilshion above the intersected line the same side °A the cue ball will be check side. Position B in the diagram 9 shows how side (111 a cue ball may be check side off the first „,,tishion, and running side off the second cushion. 4,,_'he stroke is a very thin one played with ex- Zr,erae side (left hand side in the diagram). If :4e cue ball strike the side cushion at a point Rove the position of the object ball, the side on 13e ball will be check side off the side cushion, should the side be powerful enough to bring be ball back to the baulk cushion, the side on "ie ball will then be running side off this tnshion.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 10,</p>
        <p>that, - run-through in-offs into the right top pocket Is 'require left-hand side. In position A the side tOiiera,lly called check side, and in position 11 tst4:l:llg side. The side in each case is really iden- \_heing running side off the opposing angle. sl!‘any -players are often at la loss to sz), th_either the side used in playing different run- th.,..'uth in-offs is check side or running side, and (tt'a terms are certainly often misleading. For 44441p1e, in playing the run-through in-oils awn in diafram 10 (Positions A and</p>
