      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>The present season promises to be an extremely interesting one. In addition to an enormous number of professional matches, there will be a large number of amateur championships of various towns and counties. Already the following championships have been definitely decided upon :—Glasgow, Liverpool, Birmingham,Leeds, and Leicester, Yorkshire Cumberland, Westmorland, and Shropshire. As my readers are aware I have always strongly advocated the use of crystalate balls owing to their very great superiority over ivory balls and it says a great deal for the growing popularity of these com- position balls that they will be used in every one of the fixtures above enumerated.</p>
        <p>John Roberts will be playing regularly during the present'season. On October 31st he commences a match of 9,000 up at Edinburgh, his opponent being T.Aiken, the Scottish champion. The succeeding four weep he will be playing in Manchester with. the following well- known professionals as his opponents :—Reece,Harver- son, Inman and Bateman. Each match will be 9,000 up, and Roberts is handicapped to give Harverson 2,250, Bateman 2,500, and Reece and Inman 2,750, exactly the same points as these players receive from Stevenson and Dawson. I shall be present every day during the whole of the four weeks that Roberts is in in Man- chester, and I will give weekly notes n this column on these games. J. C. R. (Gloucester).—lf you want to see very full reports of all important matches I should advise you to get the "World of Billiards" weekly. Nothing concern- ing billiards is ever omitted. _ _ _</p>
        <p>I;Toviee (Todmorden).—There are several first-class makers of tables, but I cannot mention names of firms here. If you will send me a stamped envelope I will send you full particulars. A. E. Fleming (Derby).—A corner pocket should measure 3i at the fall of the slate. If the pockets on the table you refer to only measure 31 they are too small and should be enlarged. RISO LEVI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Princesses of Montenegro.—There were rumours not long since that the Duchess Cecile of Mecklenburg Schwerin was to marry the Grand Duke Michiel of Russia, the only surviving brother of the Tsar. He however, has since become engaged to Princess Kenia, a daughter of the Prince of Montenegro, all of whose daughters have made brilliant marriages. One—the Princess Helena—is Queen of Italy, another married the Grand Duke Peter cousin of the Tsar, a third is the wife of Prince Francis of Battenberg, and a fourth—the Princess Kenia—is to marry the Grand Duke Michiel who, until the other day, was Heir Apparent to the throne, of Russia. The Prince of Montenegro might say, like the King of Deamark, that all his daughters have made brilliant matches, and there is another similarity between the two rulers,—they both live simple, unpretentious lives at Court. The Royal Betrothal.—The marriage of the Crown Prince of Germany is expected to take place at Sch- werin about the middle of January, and the Royal pair will subsequently make their bridal entry into Berlin. Meanwhile gossip is busy, concerning the personalities of the young couple, and many amiable descriptions are appearing in the German papers of the Duchess Cecile of Mecklenburg Schwerin, to whom the Crown Prince is betrothed. She is described as a charming girl of eighteen, tall, and fair, and very like her elder sister who is married to Prince Christian of Denmark, and is greatly admired in the country of her adoption. Besides her good looks, the Duchess has vivacity and cheerful- ness to a degree, characteristics which go well with her piquant and pleasing face. Altogether it is no wonder that the Crown Prince fell in love with her, and it is understood that the engagement is purely one of mutual affection and has no political aspects. The Prince is in his twenty-second year,and has a boyish beardless face. He has neither the energy or the warlike temper of his father, and takes more after the easy-going disposition of his mother. _</p>
        <p>Fashionable Doncaster.—Doncaster has become in recent years a strong rival to Goodwood, and the throng of fashionable women there, were eagerly scanned in order to obtain ideas as to what the autumn modes will be. Time was when it was a race meeting and nothing more, but of late years the fashionable element has become almost as prominent at Doncaster as at Good- wood and Ascot. The King has seldom missed being at the meeting, but, since his accession to the Throne, the presence of his Majesty has helped to extend the circle of fashionable house parties at the many famous mansions in the neighbourhood. The King made several motor car trips during his stay at Rufford Abbey, on one occasion going with the Duke of Portland on a visit to Welbeck, and his Majesty also made calls at Clumber, the residence of the Duke and Duchess- of Newcastle, ' and at Belvoir Castle, the home of the venerable Duke of Rutland. The Buccleuch Family.—The Duke of Buccleuch, who has just celebrated his seventy-third birthday,succeedt d his father in 1834, and is the head of one of the oldest of Scotch houses. The Buccleuch family were celebrated in Sir Walter Scott's "Lay of the Last Minstrel," and owns at the present day something like 500,000 acres ip the South of Scotland. The Duke, in spite of his years, is a very imposing looking personage as is also the Duchess, who was one of the "beautiful Hamiltons." She is the third daughter of the venerable Duchess of Abercom, and consequently sister of the Marchioness of Lansdowne. The Duchess was a great favourite of the late Queen, to whom she was Mistress of the Robes and now serves in the same capacity to Queen Alexandra, her tall and distinguished appearance making her a striking figure at the Courts. People who do not get much out of life.—There appear to be many people who take theirannual summer holiday in a spirit of martyrdom and boredom. Large numbers of them have been writing to the papers complaining generally that summer holidays are a failure, that there is nothing to do at the seaside except to get miserable listening to depressing music, and putting up with many discomforts which are a common feature at lodging and boarding houses. To all such people—those who are never happy except they are miserable—holidays are, no doubt a mistake, but then fortunately they are the comparatively few, and when persons write in such a strain, it is safe to assume that they never under any circumstances get 'much out. of life. One's sympathies rather go out to kose who do not allow little discomforts or inconveniences to make them unhappy, or petulant, and who always suceeed in obtaining both pleasure and benefit from their summer holidays. The Craze for Buttons.—Buttons continue to be an important detail, and skirts, blouses, and coats are all plentifully besprinkled with specimens of every shape, size and kind. The favourite buttons however are the small ones, made of enamel gilt, or gun metal, but others are of enormous size, and are also appearing on millinery, two or three being used for trimming a hat. For the new cloth costumes, covered buttons in velvet or silk • are being utilised, and speaking generally, modistes appekr to have decided this autumn that wherever a button can be put, there it shall be found. Peacocks' Feathers.—Surely superstition is losing its hold completely on the women of these Isles, for not only is black worn freely at weddings, and Friday quite a fashionable day for such functions, but we now hear of a bevy of bridesmaids appearing in hats built of soft brown velvet, trimmed with large rosettes made of the peacock's feathers! According to leading Parisian milliners, peacock's feathers are to be exceedingly modish this autumn. Some of the newest velvet toques shew applique motifs round the brim, composed of the tip or eye of the bird's tail, and pompons made of the feathers appear at the side, or back, as may best suit the wearer. Concerning Belts.—Some of the new belts are very beautiful, and also costly, and need considerable dress- ing up to. Those in kid, on a ground of silk braid lace, are however more serviceaule, while the pretty soft silk belt, which any clever worker can bone into the Swiss shape, and) finish off in less than an hour, are still much worn. The new collars which continue down the front of the bodice or blouse, likes pointed stomacher, and turn the most simple of these garments into a dressy creation, promise to enjoy a great vogue. They are to be had in divers shapes, and fabrics, and well prove a boon to the woman, who, from choice or necessity desires quick changes. Regarding waistcoats—one of the chief features of the tailor made modes--the smart- est are in white cloth, embroidered in gold and silver, or trimmed with narrow rows of black velvet ribbon and tiny black velvet buttons.</p>
        <p>Milk for the Complexion.—The woman who values her complexion should, after washing, her face in hot water and with pure soap—when retiring to rest—rins it in clear warm water,dry it with a soft towel,and then well bathe it in milk. A soft sponge should be used for this latter process. Some women allow the milk to dry on, others .just dab the face with a soft handker- chief until it is dry, but undoubtedly the former notion is the correct one. A similar procedure is excellent for the hands.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PERFECT LEMON.AELLY.</head>
        <p>A PERFECT LEMON.AELLY.</p>
        <p>For the exquisite flavour of Ripe Lemons there are no Table Jellies to equal Eiffel Tower Lemon ~kellies• Beautifully cleat ad they always ,it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>-•. V • _24: imp . • .</head>
        <p>-•. V • _24: imp . • .</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Fashions are at present in a comatose stat And lie dormant between the two seasons ot late summer and early autumn, a period of general slacknes;; in the sartorial world. People do not sc early in the autumn bestir themselves much as to the selection of new toilettes unless they belong to that class of society which journeys north to assist in the slaughter of game, and in that case trim, well- fitting tailor-made coats and skirts of quiet hued tweeds form the only possible garb. There are, of course, many new materials to be seen, and as the English climate plays many tricks and changes abruptly from tropical heat to wintry cold without any warn- ing it is not wise to delay too long in consider- ing a cloth or tweed costume of some sort. Checks of all sorts promise to be much worn far into the season, and their continued popu- larity is assured as the early autumn models</p>
        <p>love how smart are toilettes of this kind re- lieved as well as rendered emphatically becom- ing by a touch of black velvet introduced generally in the form of a rolling collar. A smart little waistcoat self-coloured is often to be seen in checked costumes, and at present those most favoured are of white pique fastened down the front with a set of pretty enamelled buttons, these sets, by the way, forming welcome and useful birthday gifts. They can be used on all sorts of articles of dress from a blouse to a coat or waistcoat. The smart little bolero sketched is secured by a set of these buttons in red and gold enamel which greatly enhance the whole toilette as it is in these. trifles much depends, and if they are neglected the general effect of even the smartest cut dress will be marred. The costume in this instance is made of fine myrtle green serge slightly piped with red, this warm note of colour being repeated in the large red roses trimming the hat. The dainty blouse is of white silk patterned with red. The skirt, however, is lined with green silk, and a petticoat of the exact same shade of colour is worn as light coloured underskirts as those of a contrasting colour are not considered correct wear this season. , Instead, the undetskirt or petticoat must match the colour of the cos- tume which covers it as nearly as possible, and I fancy we will see the black silk skirt much</p>
        <p>.• ,\\\ _ • , k.l 4..h‘ 1114 , ' A USEFUL GOWN.</p>
