      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CHARMING BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A CHARMING BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>worn throughout winter. It is, however, veil, unwise and extremely short sighted policy to purchase a cheap silk underskirt of a dark tone of colour as somehow these are affected by the dyes used and wear only a few times before they begin to cut and are therefore useless. There are some other and more durable materials which strongly resemble silk, but of Wane they lack the soft frou-frou which is so deer to the ears of most women.</p>
        <p>My second illustration depicts a charming blouse of soft white crepe de chine made with a long yoke of lace finished by a couple of dainty little ruches. The sleeves which are very full are completed by deep frills trimmed also with a ruched edging. For afternoon or evening wear nothing could be prettier than a blouse of this description as the material em- ployed is so essentially lovely and falls in the most artistic folds requiring little trimming to enhance its natural beauty. Some of the early autumn dresses are displaying a sleeveless bolero which, of course, permits of elaborate and fussy blouse sleeves being worn as of course even the widest coat sleeve that is worn works havoc to the dainty• material it covers. Sometimes these sleeveless coats are worn of quite a different colour to the rest of the costume, but I do not think this is a mode erleich will become very popular on this side of</p>
        <p>E PRETTY HAT.</p>
        <p>the Channel. This is, of course, only the ro- viva of a very old fashion as indeed are all the latest novelties. The coat which boasts of basque will, it is almost certain, be quite a feature of dress, especially in connection with tailor-made costumes and with this style of dress it is certainly a success, especlally when the upper part of the coat opens over a pretty waistcoat. beep cavalier cuffs are also a novelty. The pretty bat depicted in my last sketch is of fine black chip trimmed with deep red roses and foliage, and the veil is tied in the fashion first introduced by motorists, namely. ovet the top of the hat covering the coiffeur. It is of white chiffon spotted with large flat spots of black velvet; on a windy day the com- fort of a veil worn in this fashion is inestim- able, especially if the hat has a wide curving brim as in this instance. There is nothing very new in millinery to record beyond the fact that the red straw toque has made its annual appearance, and when of a neat shape always looks well with a plain linen or cloth costume. The marquise shape is by no means defunct, and I fancy we shall again see it in our midst as the season advances. The high crowned hat has been seized upon so eagerly by everyone that one is somewhat wearied of this style, especially when it is to be seen everywhere worn by all sorts and descriptions of women, trimmed in too often a ridiculous f•shion. -</p>
        <p>COQUETTE.</p>
        <p>/'6ll-W1 j :4011/ .iii4h . ' Ni 1 4- 6LX \--/\)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
