      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>, - • ' •-• ••••</p>
        <p>PENKETH PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Improvement of the Recreation</p>
        <p>Ground.</p>
        <p>THE NEW RATES.-HEAVY INCREASE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Murray presided at the meeting of the Penketh Parish Council held in the Wesleyan schools on Monday evening. There were also present: Messrs. J. G. Withinshaw, D. Houghton, J. Ashcroft, J. Ditchfleld, J. Routledge, and E. Foster (clerk). THE VICAR'S BEREAVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman observed that before they went on with the ordinary business of the Council he would like the members to pass a resolution of sympathy with the Vicar of Great Sankey in his recent sad bereavement. He proposed that such a resolution be sent to him.— Mr. Withinshaw seconded and the resolution was agreed to unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FOUNTAIN.</head>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN.</p>
        <p>The Chairman reported that Mr. Woodward had made such alterations to the fountain as, he believed, would prevent any further tampering with it, and consequent damage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LIGHTING.</head>
        <p>LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>The Chairman also announced that no doubt they would see from the newspapers the question of lighting Sankev-road had been brought before the District Council and they had sanctioned the erection of a lamp by Penketb, and Sankey had also agreed to erect another lamp in Sankey-road. If the Council agreed he would arrange with Mr. Thompson for the erection of two lamps.—The Council agreed to this course.</p>
        <p>THE RECREATION GROUND. PROPOSED IMPROVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said a sub-committee was appointed at the last meeting to consider what improvements might be effected on the recreation ground. He would be glad to hear a report. The Clerk said there was a consensus of opinion amongst the members of the sub-committee that fifty shillings was an altogether inadequate sum to do what was thought desirable. In addition to the painting of the pavilion and the railings and fountain, the sub-com- mittee thought the seating accommodation ought to be improved, and they were also in favourof the provision of some gymnastic apparatus, such as horizontal bars and parellel bars. Mr. Withinshaw thought a sum of £lO would suffice to make the improvements proposed. • The Chairman asiwd whether it had ever occurred to the sub-committee that the money might be raised by subscription. Mr. Withinshaw thought that as the improvements were for the benefit of the village, the parishioners ought to find the money. The Chairman said he did not want to he pessimistic, but he was afraid the rates were going upwards. The Clerk said in accordance with instructions he had invited tenders for the painting of the pavilion, fountain and railings. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman opened the tenders which were found to be as follows :—Midwood, £4 12s.;Mainle-v, £2 175.; Hobson, £2 13s. 6d. It was unanimously decided to accept the tender of Mr. Hobson on condition that the work was done before the end of the month.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the proposed improvements to the recreation ground should be re-considered at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>THE DEARTH OF COUNTY MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Benjamin Hobson wrote referring to the dilemma that the county magistrates, Clerk and the Court were placed in at the last County Petty Sessions at Warring- ton owing to the absence of a sufficient number of gentlemen to transact the exceptional business. This matter of having one or two county magi strates!resident in Penketh had been brought before the Council by himself on several previous occasions, and the present was an opportune time to ; •new their request. It was a singular fact that most of the county magistrates for this Lancashire division lived in Cheshire. Penketh ought to make some representations to get magistrates appointed from the district. He was not prompted by any religious or political motives in bringing the matter before the Council again. He would ask them to "strike while the iron is hot."</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the Council took steps some time ago to get magistrates appointed from Penketh but nothing came of it. The Clerk said they went so far as to suggest a list of additional magistrates to the member for the division (Colonel Pilkington). He did not know whether the list frightened him. (Laughter). Mr. Withinshaw observed that a deputation was ap- pointed on the last occasion to wait upon the Clerk. Mr. Routledge said it was necessary to take some steps in the matter. In case of taking. depositions or getting summonses signed there was no magistrate nearer than Moore.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said there was no harm in taking the matter up again. It was decided that the clerk should take what steps he thought necessary in the matter.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS INCREASE OF RATES. The Chairman remarked that it was only right the Parish Council should know the position of the parish in regard to the new rates. The County precept on the overseers had just been received, and for the ensuing half year the demand on the parish for the County rate was £350 15s. 10d. That was altogether over and above the usual call for parochial work, and the expenses of the Rural District Council and Board of Guardians. He would give them some figures to show how rapidly the county rate was increasing. In September, 1903, the county call upon Penketh was £250 9s. sd. In March, 1904, it was £250 15s. 5d., and in September it had increased to £350 15s. 10d. Mr. Withinshaw : Is the increase due to education?</p>
        <p>The Chairman : -Education accounts for the larger portion of the increase, but all the county expenses are going up. Penketh was now paying £652 per year more than in 1898. In the year 1898 the call was for £839, now it was £1,491. Mr. Withinshaw pointed out that the increase in the county rate call from £250 to £350 in the past six months was about equal to the advances in salaries which had been made in the parish under the new Edu- cation act.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the overseers would have to levy a bigger rate than they had ever done before. The new rate would be 4d. or sd. more than last year. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
