      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>POLITICS IN THE OPEN-AIR, THE EDUCTIONANB LICENSING ACTS.</head>
        <p>POLITICS IN THE OPEN-AIR, THE EDUCTIONANB LICENSING ACTS.</p>
        <p>Mor.3 Views of the Conservative</p>
        <p>Labour Party.</p>
        <p>Bridge Foot was once more the meeting place of the Warrington Conservative Labour party on Thursday, when addresses were delivereg by -representatives or the party on the Education and Licensing Acts. Councillor W. J. Wilson presided, and he was accompanied on thel waggon by Mr. Frink Fearneley, Mr. R. J. Ma,cartirey, and `Mr. W. L. Williams. The AIR NI ,in calling upon Mr. Fearneley, asked the audience to give .him a patient and fair hearing. Even if everything said was not entirely, in agreement with their views, they must give Mr. Fearneley credit. for endeavouring to be fair and impartial. Mr. FF...1.1c1i ELE Y said he wished to give them facts as far as he was able. He 'did not indulge in what the Americans callett"ilapdoodle" or "bounce" about his own party or anything of that kind. He noticed that at the last meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council some members 'drew attention to a statement made by Mr.'G. D. Kelley. Mr. Kelley was reported to have said that he did not knov of any prominent trades unionists connected with the Conservative Labour party. Mr. Kelley was perfectly right, if by prominent trades unionists he meant paid officials of trades unions or labour parties. The Conservative Labour party had none such. It was a party formed about four months ago, purely consisting of workingmen and had no paid officials whatever. There were hundreds of trades unionists belonging to it, but they were not prominent trades unionists in -the sense that they attended trades congresses and similar assemblies. He did not see that this was any detriment to the Conservative Labour party so long as this party was upholding trades unionism at every possible point and encouraging its members to be more loyal to their unions. They had no paid officials as yet. He slid not use the word offensively, because he believed that every man who did work ought to be paid, and no man was any the worse for being a paid official of any organisation. At 'present the Conservative Labour party was without a paid efficial andithe statement of Mr. Kelley was to that extent correct. Proceeding to discuss the recent licensing act, Mr. Fearneley said a certain section of the community considered it' in some senses an unjust-Piece of legislation. ,(Hear, hear.) A fortnight ago he made the statement that the Conservative party, as represented by the present Government had for the last thirty years been the only temperance party for this country so far as legiSlati on was concerned, and he was going to try and prove it. In reviewing the temperance legisla- tion introduced by the Liberal party during the period mentioned, Kr. Fearneley referred to the passing of Bruce's Act, which closed public-houses at certain fixed times. That Act hack worked very well, and had been a good piece of legislation. But since then the Liberal party had made woeful blunders in the matter of tem- perance. They passed the Welsh Sunday Closing Act, which was a failure, and then therewas that unfortunate introduction of grocers licences by Mr. Gladstone. Mr. Gladstone was not to be blamed for making the experiment, but he was blameable for not look- ing all round the subject before he brought it forward. Since the passing of the Act there had been a 'large increase in female drunkenness. Mr. Fearneley proceeded to refer to. the temperance legislation passed by the Conservative party. He dilated on the act passed by the Conservatives to pre- vent men interested in the liquor trade from sitting on the licensing bench; to the greater powers given to the police with regard to the entering of public-houses; and to the black-listing system. With regard to the last-named system, he contended that while it had' not been a success in large towns it hadlworked well in small towns and villages. Allusion was also made to the legislation passed by Conservatives which made it possible to establish inebriate homes and to prevent children from going to .publio-houses for drink. In criticising the action of.teetetalers, he said he objected to their interference with the liberties and freedom of the. great mass of thepeople. To try and induce the legislature to introduce measures for the compulsory closing of public-houses, was not only a mistake on the part of .temperance people but kept a large number of fsincere well-wishers from working with them. Discussing the recent licensing act, Mr. Fearneley referred to the insecurity of tenure under the old conditions, which enabled a bench of teetotalers to shut up a public-house on the ground that there were too many. He pointed out that every citizen was a sharer in the profits of the licensed trade inasmuch as the revenue received £12,000,000 per annum from it. He had not heard of any passive resisters to this business. (Laughter.) He had not heard of a single teetotaler refusing to have his taxes reduced because of the revenue of the licensed trade. He would have more confidence in the sincerity of the would-be legislative teetotalers if they would form a society re- fusing to be relieved of the taxation, which the licensed trade paid, so that their hands might be clean of the trade. Until they did that, everyone shared in the profits of the drink trade. The Government had put the power of dealing with licenses, not in the hands Of a small bench, but in the hands of the bench at the sessions.</p>
        <p>A Voice : In the hands of the brewers. Mr. FERNLEY (continuing) said the local licensing bench had the power to close a public house if the licensee had been misbehaving or disobeying the law, but the power of closing public-houses because they were too many in a district was in the hands of the county sessions bench. When a house was closed under such circumstances, compensation was paid, and the compensation fund was provided in each district by the trade itself. That, he contended, was a very good arrangement. Compensation was to be paid Rot only to the brewers and owners of houses, but the licensee also Magistrates who had hesitated about closing public- houses in districts where there were too many would be able under the act, to close them and no wrong would be done to the owner and licensee. Mr. Fearneley proceeded to deal with the education question. He pointed out that prior to the passing of the 1370 Act, Churchpeople, Catholics and Wesleyans had to sacrifice a great deal to keep the voluntary schools going. They had to pay the difference between the meagre sum that the Government was giving and the sum that the schools really cost, with the result that there was a very heavy drain upon those religions bodies. Let anyone say what they would, the religious bodies deserved very great credit for bringing up our education to the standard that was reached long be- ' fore 1870. Havinc,eßoardalluded to the 1870 Act which intro- duced the Board School system, Mr. Fearneley said the "religions people" who had built schools in order to keep up their religious principles had such an overweening anxiety that their children should be taught their religious faith that they sacrificed up to two years ago to keep the schools, whilst the Board schools were being supported at the cost of the ratepayers. The religious people were the majority, and were the majority now. But they had never grumbled about paying for the School Board education all the time. Those who had the idea that they would like their children to be taught their religion had been paying for the Board schools,although their own children had been attending voluntary schools. They had never passively resisted. They had been pay- ing the rates all the time and they had never talked about going to the stake. (Laughter.) They were rate- payers; the law said they must pay, and they paid. At the same time they considered it was a grievance and they had been bringing pressure to bear upon every Conservative Government to alter it, in order that all schools might be put upon the com- mon basis with regard to the cost of secular education. They did not want the State to pay for religious teach- ing, and the State did not pay for it under the new Act. It simply paid for the secular teaching. The voluntary schools wanted their share of the rates, and they had a perfect right to claim it. It was nothing but justice that they should have that share, not that they should have money to build new schools out of the rates for their purposes, not that they should have money to improve their present schools—they did not get that under the new Act All that the ratepayers paid for was simply the secular education. All,that the religious people 'claimed from them was that they should have the power to nominate and to recommend a head teacher for their schools. The appointment was left to the Education Committee and if the teachers' qualifications for secular teaching were not satisfactory the committee could refuse to accept the nomination. All the other teachers in the school were appointed by the Education Committee. Generally the committee very sensibly appointed as teachers members of the denominations to which the respective schools belonged, although cases eften arose in which that practice was not followed. The religious people had to teach their religion themselves. .For that privilege they undertook to keep the school buildings up, and to pay for all repairs. If they got a new school, they built it at their own cost. Their Free Church friends were very insincere in the matter, for they agreed to the Bible being in the Board schools. Was not that teaching religion? The Education At was a good law because it put all people on a level for the first time with regard to the education of their children. In places where there was only a Church school, children could be.kept away from religious instruction if their parents desired it. In conclusion Mr. Fearneley said he did not see anything in either Act that was a ground of complaint for workingruen. (Applause.) Mr. ROBERT J. MACARTNEY also spoke in support of the measures. In endeavouring to touch the thorny question of temperance. the Conservative party was merely acting up to its traditions, for it was an amazing fact—a lamentable fact if they liked—that during the last fifty years if there had been a party that had arrogated to itself a title to be called a tem- perance party, it was the Liberal party, but as a matter of fact as in regard to social legislation generally, so in regard to temperance, the Liberal party had very little fruits to offer them after more than fifty years of temperance agitation. He protested against the bigotry and intolerance which had been introduced in respect of the Licensing Act by the teetotal people. Speaking on temperance, Mr. Macartney said they were told on eminent authority that more disease and deaths could</p>
        <p>be traced to overeating and intemperance in the matter of food than could be traced directly to over-indulgence in strong drink. He dealt at some length with the pro- visions of the Licensing Act. Mr. W. L. WILLIAMS, in moving a vote of thanks to the speakers, said the question was asked at the Trades Council meeting "Are there any trade unionists , in this van of the Conservative Labour Party" ? He said emphatically that there were. He himself had been a trade unionist for over thirteen years and for two and a half years he had occupied the position ofsecretary of the local lodge of his union. At the bye-election in the Oldham Division some years ago, one of the men who sought the suffrages of the electors was the secre- tary of the Operative Cotton Spinners—Mr. James Mawdsley. Because Mr. Mawdsley stood as a Con- servative Labour candidate the whole bag and baggage of the so-called Labour representatives in the House of Commons stumped the constituency andspoke in favour of the two capitalist candidates—Messrs. Runciman and Emmott—with the result that they were returned. One of the labour members who acted in that way happened to be a member of his (Mr. Williams') own trade union, and he was receiving pay from that union for representing it in the House of Commons. He (Mr. Williams) brought the matter before his lodge and they unanimously agreed that it was a question which ought to be placed before the Executive Committee in. London. They pissed a resolution which was submit- ted to the governing body of the union, and the answer they received was that it was not a matter for the Executive Committee, but for Mr. Broadhurst's con- stituents to deal with. He (Mr. Williams) said emphati- cally that as one who contributed to Mr. Broadhurst's expenses in returning him to the House of Commons he had a voice as to what Mr. Broadhurst should do in the cause of labour. There were other cases of a similar character that might be mentioned. The vote having been carried, Mr. FEARNELEY answered a large number of questions from the audience. One was "Is the Conservative Labour party prepared to bring out or supporta candidate favourable to Sunday closing, local veto against new licences, and shorter hours for the employment of barmaids ?" Mr. FEARNELEY replied that they were not prepared to bring out or support anyone in favour of Sunday closing until the whole of the people of the district had declared for Sundkv closing. The same thing applied to local veto. If 'the majority of the people desired that,there should be no new licences, he would support it. He was in favour of shorter hours for bar- maids provided that it did not prevent the employment of barmaids altogether. He believed women workers had not very many openings of employment, and he would notilike to support shorter hours for barmaids unless he felt sure it would not mean the employment of barmen in places where women had the work. Another query was: "Was the leader of the Con- servative party, the Right Hon. A.J. Balfour, favourable to temperance when he refused to receive a deputation from the temperance party on the question of the Licensing Bill, and when he introduced his famous "gagging " motion ?" Mr. PEARNELEY answered :1" I think Mr. Balfour has proved pretty conclusively that he is strongly in favour of reasonable temperance legislation, but I should not consider_ he was at all to blame when he did not care to be lectured by extreme teetotalers, and to have the business of the country blocked by the mere discussion of teetotal faddism." A third questioner wanted to know "why the Licensing Act was brought in. Was it because some Benches of Licensing magistrates had been reducing licences in an apparently large proportion or was it to create a vested interest in licences ?"</p>
        <p>Mr. FEARNSLEY replied to the first part of the question in the affirmative. The Act certainly was passed because certain licensing benches had been reducing licences without any rhyme or reason. He did not see how the Act could possibly create a vested interest in licences when it was for the reduttion of licences. If the interrogator meant " Was it to create security for the present licence holders? "he should say that the Act was undoubtedly brought in for that purpose. Another question was: " Why was it necessary to compensate licencees for the loss of licences when they have a large and influential insurance company of their own ? "</p>
        <p>_ The answer was: "Because they want to be certain that the compensation is assured if the licences are withdrawn from the houses. No insurance company could possibly assure every licencee of that fact." "Is it not a fact that it is not the licence holders but the large brewers who will benefit under the Act ?" was asked. - - -</p>
        <p>Mr. FEARNELEY said both would benefit equally according to their amount of interest in the concern. Another member of the audience inquired: "Why should compensation be given for unrenewed licences when they are granted for one year only ?" Mr. FEARNELEY said that technically the licences were granted for one year, but the public authorities looked upon them as licences for at least twenty-five years, for they assessed property and took rates and taxes from the licencees on that basis.</p>
        <p>Mr. FEARNELEY next had to deal with the question: "You stated at your last meeting that through the Government passing an Education Act, workingmen could send their children to school free. Are you aware that the rates in Warrington are 9s. Old. in the £ and 10d. of that sum is for educational purposes? Since the Act'has come in force in Warrington the rents of the workingclasses have gone up equivalent to 10d. in the R. lam at a loss to know how the children of the workingclasses go to school free, and a straight answer will oblige." Mr. FEARNELEY said there was power under the Act of Parliament passed by the last Conservative Govern- ment for free education for all children, but it was necessary for the local authorities to put that power in force. If they did it would necessarily require an increase in the rates. He did not find anyone objecting previ- ously to the rates being increased for the School Boards but immediately the other schools were put on a fair basis, there was a lot of groaning about the rates going I up. f they wanted improvements the rates must increase. _</p>
        <p>Another question submitted in writing was "Who is going to be the prospective candidate of the so-called Conservative Labour Party in Warrington and why is he not at this meeting to answer questions for himself ? No shuffling will oblige." Mr. FEARNELEY said he could not tell who was going to be the Conservative Labour Party's candidate in War- rington.He was not a Warrington man and he did not think the party in Warrington had yet discussed the question. "What percentage of the compensation does the licencee get in respect of a house belonging to]brewers?" was another query. Mr. FEARNELEY said the liceucee got the compensa- tion equivalent to his interest, in the house. He had to state to the magistrates what that interest was. "Has the Conservative Labour party a remedy for the unemployed problem?" was asked. Mr. FEARNELEY said the party. had a remedy and it was to compel the public authority whose business it was and whose work it ought to be, to deal with the unemployed. The Conservative Labour party's remedy— and they advised them (his hearers) to undertake it— wasto work in order to force the Board of Guardians to deal with every man unemployed not as a pauper bat as an unemployed man. "Should the tied house system be abolished?" was one of the concluding questions.. Mr. FE.A.RNELEY : In my opinion most undoubtedly yes. The meeting was then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>1:I _ –</head>
        <p>1:I _ -</p>
        <p>REV. H. DUNNICO AND COUNCILLOR</p>
        <p>STOTT. A Challenge to Public Debate In consequence of several attacks made upon the It.w. H. Dunnico by the Independent Labour Party at Bridge Foot culminating in a letterrin the Press from the pen of Councillor Stott, a challenge has been issued inviting Councillor Stott to . public debate. In the course of his letter Mr. Dunnico says that he has deliberately ignored the insulting and vulgar remarks of professional agitators who never had any reputation to lose, but now that Councillor Stott has taken up the matter, he is determined to make them prove their accusations or admit their inability to do so. He desires the matter to be settled in this way because he has neither the time nor the inclination to enter on a newspaper controversy with such an opponent. The debate is to be held in the Parr Hall and the loser to pay the costs of sante, the subjects for debate to be as follows: . _</p>
        <p>(1)- The drink trade is the greatest hindrance to the spread of the gospel and the source of more unnecessary evil tyranny, sufferinr vice than all other causes put together not excepting the present competitive system. (2) That Christian Socialism based upon the teachings of Jesus Christ is infinitely superior to the Socialism of the Independent Labour Party which is confessedly non-Christian. (3) That the Labour Representation Committee whilst nominally a trades union orgauization and supposed to be independent of all political parties is virtually in the hands of the Socialists. That the Socialist party (which is just as much a political partyas either the Conservative or Liberal party) is in accordance with its declaration of policy, making use of Trade Union funds in order</p>
        <p>to propagate its own special brand of Socialism. Mr. Dunnico urges Counzilior Stott to accept his challenge (1) in the interests of temperance reform (2) in the interests of Christianity (3) iu the interests of Trade Unions in particular and workingmen in general. With regard to the last Mr. Dunnico states that a public debate will give him an opportunity of urging the workingmen to take such steps as shall prevent their money being spent to keep men in position and luxury who have never subscribed a penny to trade union funds and to prevent men—void of principles and adepts at trickery—from making their organizations the cats-paw of a body of men who are at heart opposed to Trade Union aims. Mr. Dunnico declines to meet other than a local man with a reputation to maintain and as Councillor Stott has been pHt forth as a competent authority, he has addressed the challenge to him. Moreover he states that if the members of the Socialist organization reserve to themselves the right to attack and criticise locally it is distinctly dishonourable to hide themselves behind men who may or may not be worth a moment's consideration.</p>
        <p>HAMS, BACON &amp; CHEESE ARE DEARER.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 17, 1904.</p>
        <p>Paul's v. St. Barnabas,referee,J. Parry: Vulcan A. F. C. v. Stockton Heath Reserves, referee, F. Foulkes; Daresbury v. Penketh, referee, C. Taylor; Warrington St. Mary's Reserves, referee, H. Singleton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalists)</p>
        <p>When the Warrington crowd saw the local team trip gaily on the field for the opening match last Saturday, they missed so many familiar faces that there was considerable apprehension as to the result of the encounter with such formidable opponents as the Cup-holders. Warrington, minus Fish, Harris, Boardman, and ii7nylor, are only a moderate lot and it was-disheartening to open the season atWilderspool with auch a weak team. Still the bitterness of defeat was avoided and an honourable draw—honourable to Warrington undoubtedly under the circumstances— was recorded.</p>
        <p>Halifax brought a very strong team down und were bent on recording their initial victory at Warringt, on's expense, but there were fifteen determined defenders and truth to tell,Halifax were in no way able to prove superiority over the Warrington lot. I have never considered the " blue and whites" a clever team- judged from the scientific foot- ball standard, but they have demonstrated their ability to "get there " during the last two seasons. League champions once, and twice holders of the cup in two seasons is a record that stamps them at above the ordinary in some respects. They are a deceptive side. To watch them play as they did on Saturday one would hardly expect them to score ten points in a month of football. Yet it was obvious enough that the other side was struggling hard against a very solid defence, which frequently resolved itself in a grim, persistent attack. Halifax, often enough only need to score once to win their matches, and if they had landed a solitary goal on Saturday, they would have won in my opinion. Still, with all their strength and soundness I don't think Halifax will be anywhere near the‘.League championship this season, though they will require some reckoning with in the cup-ties.</p>
        <p>They will probably never have a better chance of squelching Warrington than on Saturday, and as they failed it is to •be presumed that Warrington might squelch them with a full team up. I have a high opinion of the abilities of the Warrington first fifteen this season, but little could be judged from the per- formance of the side doing duty on Saturday, and those spectators who did not go to Leigh will probably imagine that the team is .about the same as usual. They are mistaken, and it is a pity that misfortune and a little roughness have for the time being deprived Warrington of the power to impress friends and foes alike with their prowess. Let us get Pres- ton, Davies, and Harris back again,and we shall see— what we shall see'!</p>
        <p>The six thousand spectators could not have been vastly entertained with Saturday's match. Though keenly contested it was dull, deadly dull. There was no combination on either side worth a continental, the only features of interest being the magnificent kicking of the visitors and the fine collaring all round. Halifax had the misfortune to lose the services and 15 stone weight of Jack Riley early in the game through a leg injury, but they were never behindhand at scrummaging. The seven survivors each put in a bit extra and the balance was maintained. But there was none of the screwing and toying with the Warrington pack seen at Weaste last April. The structure is rather more 'solid than then and ought to stand the rough and tumble of a season's campaign really well. Still for cleverness and unity of action the "blue and white" forward brigade again took the honours. They are an accomplished crowd with sufficient strength to utilise their abilities to the full, and Warrington will need to "lam" before they are equal to Halifax in the scrummaging department.</p>
        <p>" If's " are not worth much in football, but nevertheless if Warrington had had the services of Fish and Harris on Saturday they would have beaten Halifax. The reserve wings, Kenyon and Brooks, made no glaring errors, but they made no headway either. I was disappointed in the first-named. He did'not show up at all well, and has a lot to learn before he will be an ornament to first-class football. It is just as well that he should realise this now. By push and perseverance he should do much better and he is getting the opportunity to improve in good company. Neither a Fish nor a Harris is made in a season!</p>
        <p>Though Warrington were somewhat in the position of Samson when shorn of his locks, they might have done better in several directions on Saturday. The half-backs were the usual pair, but they played much below par. Not once during the game did I see either of them attempt to get away from the scrum and make an opening. It is useless thr)winig the ball straight to the centres against so sound a " spltting " team as Halifax. Methods of this kind are more like passive resistance than attack, and whatever possessed the Warrington halves to resort to such feeble tactics I cannot tell. They are capable of better work and it is expected from them. Joe Riley Von the other side was twice as enterprising and really deserved some tangible reward for his hard play. Had Isherwood and Dickenson been behind him there would have been 110 pointless draw.</p>
        <p>I have some fault to find with Hallam's captainPy too. Though Warrington were being outclassed in the kicking department he entrusted the ball repeat- edly to Dickenson and Davies, when Preston would have made nearly twice the headway. It is no use having a fine and even famous kicker in the team if he is to be ignored, and it was little wonder the crowd called out " Preston, Preston ! " in exasperation more than once.</p>
        <p>Little was worth any two punters on the Warring- ton side, and his display at full back could not have been surpassed. He excelled himself almost, and on Saturday's form must be a long way the best back in the Union. What he is worth to Halifax the Cup-holders alone know.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington make the long journey to Hull, where they hope to be successful seeing that the "all blacks" have already been twice defeated. Unfortun- ately Preston, Davies, and Harris will all be absentees. The two former were suspended up to and including October Ist on Tuesday for roughness in the Leigh match, and there are some stiff engagements in the meantime. Still with Fish back, there is room for son* confidence.</p>
        <p>The team is: Hallam,back;Fish,lsherwood,Dicken- son,and Kenyon,three-quarters ; Hockenhall and Bate, hall-backs ; Shugars, Belton, Thomas, Jolley, Cook, Harmer and Lunt forwards.</p>
        <p>During the week, J. Davies of Penygraig has been put on the Warrington register, and D. Townson, struck off.</p>
        <p>heßeforees' °racially appointed for to-day are as lows :</p>
        <p>Bradford v. Salford. Referee, Mr. E. H. Smirk,Wigan Touch judges, Messrs. J. Greenwood, Hebden Bridge and W. Emmett, Leeds. Broughton Rangers v. Leigh. Referee, Mr. W. M'Cutcheon, Oldham. Touch judges, Messrs. J. H. Smith, Widnes, and W. Lancaster, Rochdale. Halifax v. Wakefield Trinity. Referee, Mr F. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judges, Messrs. S. Goldsmith, York, and J. W. Scholes, Dewsbury. Hull v. Warrington. Keferee, Mr. F. Kennedy, Broughton. Touch judges, Messrs. It. Tomes, York, and C. Ackroyd, Bramley. Leeds v. Widnes. Referee, Mr. J. Priestley,Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. P. F. Farrar, Halifax, and J. Birtwistle, Hebden Bridge. Oldham v. Batley. Referee, Mr. W. W. Taylor, Hunslet. Touch judges, Messrs. A. Roscoe, Broughton, and W. IVlainwaring, Salford. Runcorn V. Hunslet. Referee, Mr. Jesson, Hudders- field. Touch judges, Mr. A. Jones and Mr. Warrington. Swinton V. St. Helens. Referee, Mr. P. Turner, Warrington. Touch judges, Messrs. T. Anderton, Leigh, and I. P. Taylor, Oldham. Wigan v. Hull Kingston Rovers. Referee, Mr. J. Oakland, Barnsley. Touch judges, Messrs. E. C. Parr Lancaster, and J. Edwards, St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Association Football.</head>
        <p>Association Football.</p>
        <p>The matches in connection with the Warrington and District League (second division) on Saturday, resulted as follows :—Warrington Central 3, St. Ilsrnabas 0 ; Warrington Albion Reserves 5. Vulcan, A. F. C. 1, Widnes St. Ambrose 3,Warrington St. Niary's Reserves 1 The following matches will be played to-day :—First division, stockton Heath V. Orford Barracks, referee, F Clegg; Thelwall v, Earlestown Y.M.C.A, referee, Lloyd: Warrington Albions v. Warrington St. Mary's referee, T. P. Forbes. Second division, Warrington 8.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Several local clubs pay a visit to High Legh during the season and enjoy a contest on the celebrated pitch there. The High Legh Club has just issued its report. The total number of matches played was 20: wen, nine; drawn, five; lost, six. The home team scored 2,703 runs for 183 wickets (this being the record number for the ground in one season), giving an average cost of 14.77 as against 2,151 for 194 wickets at an average cost of 11.08. The highest score made for the club was 263 for eight wickets as against 193 for five wickets. The club scored two innings of over 200 and 13 innings of over 100 as against ten innings of over 100. Seven members of the home team were run out against five of the visitors. Seventy-five members of the visiting teams were caught out as against 66 of the home team.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills played their concluding match of the season on Saturday, when the Northwich Eleven visited the Lovely-lane pitch. The result was a draw, the home batsman having made 92, whilst the representa- tives of the salt town were 76 for 7 wickets. Wood- cock batted in creditable style for the Wire Mills, his effort realizing 23. Harperi(3l) was the top scorer for Northwich. In the bowling department, N. Cook rendered good service, capturing three wickets for 11 runs.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall brought a successful season to . a close with a victory over Lostock Graham by the narrow margin of four runs. The scoring on each side was very low, the wicket favouring the bowlers.</p>
        <p>The following particulars with reference to the Sankey Wire Mills Cricket Club (first eleven) for the past season will prove interesting. I shall be glad to receive averages from other clubs. Matches played 15, won 4, lost 4, drawn 6 and 1 tie.</p>
        <p>BITTING AVERAGES.</p>
        <p>No. of Times Most in Total Aver-</p>
