      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Cycling,</head>
        <p>Cycling,</p>
        <p>The season which is nearing its close will be re- membered as a highly successful one. So congenial have the weather conditions been that very few fixtures have had to be cancelled, and the country side has been seen at its best. Four runs remain on the Warrington club's card. and when the members turn their backs on quaint old Knutsford on October Ist it will be with the knowledge that not until the long 'inter months have passed will they have the pleasure of participating in another club outing. Last Saturday a very enjoyable spin to Davenham attracted a large muster. The afternoon was ideal for cycling, and the outward journey through Weaverham and Vale Royal was pleasant and exhilarating. Tea was served at the Bull's Head Inn, and several games of bowls were engaged in. The return to Warrington was safely accomplished via Northwich and Marbury.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a dozen members had a short run, Moore being the destination. A thoroughly enjoy- able evening was spent.</p>
        <p>To-day's run is to Acton Bridge and the annual fish- ing match will take place. On Tuesday the venue will be Sankey, and there should be a good turn out for the final evening spin. A smoking concert at the Chapel House has been arranged.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING-UP TIME. To-night Sunday 7•23 Monday 7.20 Tuesday 7.18 Wednesday Thursday Friday Angling. . . -</p>
        <p>The final stage of the competition for the Worsley Canal Fishery Board Shield was fished in the waters of the Bridgewater Canal at Timperley on Saturday last. Teams representing the Robin Hood A.S., of Clifton, and the Rope and Anchor A.S., of Warrington, were the competitors, and suitable arrangements had been made by the general secretary to the Board, Mr. G. S. Smeeth, and one of the assistant secretaries, Mr. F. J. Emson. The contest was, naturally, a spirited one, and sport was, at times, very brisk. Many spectators attracted by such an important event, were present, amongst whom were R. Burn, Esq., and T. Taylor, Esq., lessees of the fishing in the Canal. Appended are the names of two teams and the weight of fish recorded by each in the contest :</p>
        <p>RODIN HOOD A.S.</p>
        <p>10. oz. dr. J. Sharples 0 ... 8 .... 4 J. Duddle J. Morris E. Howarth H. Ryder W. Halliday</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>ROPE AND ANCHOR A.S.</p>
        <p>_ lb. oz. drs. J. Hackney 1 ... 4 ... 5 J. Rowell J. Nicholson 0 ... ... 8 J. Ward J. Crossley 0 ... 3 ... 0 F. Kenny Total</p>
        <p>The team recording the heaviest aggregate weight were declared the winners—the Robin Hood A.S. team —and in announcing the result, Mr. Linklater, the referee, heartily congratulated both teams.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above was also decided the individual championship contest, and two competitors had been left in for the two medals and the premier prizes. These were Mr. I. Mather of Warrington and Mr. T. Ogden of Salford.—Mr. Mather was the success- ful contestant in this, he catching but one fish, to his opponent a blank, thus taking the gold medal and £2, the second prize being a gold-centred silver medal and £1 10s., and which was announced as won by Mr. Ogden.</p>
        <p>Coming to strictly local clubs, the Perseverance A. S. fished two contests on the 11th inst. at Vale Royal, the first being the annual event and the second the President's match. In the annual match the following were the winners in the order named; Messrs. H. Owen, ldr.; T. Chadwick, F. Chadwick, Ed. Lawton, .T. Buckley, R. Bradbury W. Bradbury, W. Bate, J. Chadwick, J. Delooze, and W. Ellison. The following won prizes in the President's match : Messrs. H. Owen, lib. 12oz. 12drs. ; J. Chadwick. T. Chadwick, F. Bonnell, F. Chadwick, W. Bradbury, and H. Wilson. It will be noticed probably that thOame angler won both matches.</p>
        <p>The members of the Black Bear A. S. visited Agden on Saturday last to fish another contest, but sport was only moderate. Result, Ist T. Nixon, 7oz. Bdrs. ; followed by G. Taylor, J. Bradshaw, R. Taylor, J. Clarke, J. Cook. J. Maires, R. Looker,E. Shoebridge, T. Robin- son and W. Burton. The prizes were distributed on Monday evening last.</p>
        <p>FrEivis.—There is an open competition at Agden Bridge on Saturday afternoon next and entries may be made on the Bank and will be accepted up to three o'clock. A perch of 21bs. 2ozs. 15th-s. and two chub respectively 21b. llozs. 9drs. and 21b. Oozs. 7drs. have been registered on the association catch book by Mr. H. C. Rathbone, and Mr. J. Waywell has recorded a trout of 11b. 3ozs. 9drs. alid two chub, 21b. 3ozs 3drs. and lib. llozs. 4drs. respectively. The above fish were caught in the vicinity of Llanyraynech. Many anglers recorded excellent sport at the Old River at Lymm last week end. Fairly good roach fishing has also been spoken of as having been obtained at Acton Bridge in the canal: A perch of about lib was taken by Mr. J. Glover at Vale Royal, whilst another angler Mr. F. Jolley, cantured about 141. b. of roach from the same locality last week end. It is expected that about 30 teams will compete in the contest for the Warrington A. A. Shield</p>
        <p>at Moore to-day. Prizes won in recent contests in con- nection with the Warrington Anglers' Association will be distributed at the Norton Arms Hotel on Thursday evening next..</p>
        <p>In cnnnection with the Warrington United Anglers' Protection Society,an open contest is to be promoted in aid of the Nursing Association. This match will take place in the Bridgewater Canal at London Bridge on Saturday, October Ist. The entrance fee will be is. and anglers from any club or locality are eligible to compete. The lstprize will be £2 10s. the 2nd prize £1 10s. and the 3rd prize Ll in addition to many minor prizes. Entries may be made with the secretaries of the local clubs and they will be accepted up to Tuesday. September 27th. Any further particulars may be ob- tained from the secretary, Mr. S. R. Lodge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Water Polo.</head>
        <p>Water Polo.</p>
        <p>Warrington are carrying all before them, and on Tues- day a victory over Wigan 2nd was achieved. During the first half the wirepullers defended the deep end, but at the interval led by a goal to nil. Exciting play was witnessed,and one of the visitors was ordered out of the bath for foul play. Sutton sent in the successful shot. On resuming, Warrington again took up the attack, and Sutton registered a second goal. The visitors assailed the home goal, and Woods exhibited a fine defence. Boscow added another capital goal for Warrington, and soon afterwards Saunders scored for Wigan. Before the close Warrington's total was increased to five goals by W. Ashton and Boscow. Four of the Wigan players left the water owing, they alleged, to foul play. The result of the match was a victory for Warrington by five goals to one.</p>
        <p>The third division encounter between Warrington 2nd and Woolton Ist also ended in the home team's favour. Warrington were the first to press and soon after the swim-out. Bailey notched a point,whilst a moment later Howard scored a second. The visitors now had a turn of the pressing, and a shot by one of the forwards only just missed scoring, the ball striking the bar. Warring- ton again asserted themselves, and Turner succeeded in beating the visiting goalkeeper with a swift shot. Rathbone immediately added a fourth point. Just before the interval Turner added two more goals. Half time arrived with Warrington leading by six goals to nil. Up to this point the visitors had been playing a man short, but they were now at full strength, and the play ruled in their favour for the first few minutes. Rylands eventually opened the score for them. Martin added a second goal for Woolton. Each side scored in turn and the result was Warrington seven goals, Woolton four goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>As is usual in an amateur club of limited membership, during the summer there has been ominous rumours regarding the quality of the teams Stockton Heath would be able to place in the field. As usual, one finis that there need have been no anxiety, and given average enthusiasm among the members, the club should place a very strong first team in the flald, and the second string should certainly be above the average.</p>
        <p>It will be curious to note in the first practice match, which takes place this afternoon, how L. Fairclough and E. Pierpoint will acquit themselves. One is almost afraid that these two players, who were so good in their respective fpositions of last season, will not benefit by the change made.</p>
        <p>At a general meeting held at the Lion Hotel some time ago, it was resolved, but not without opposition: "To appoint a committee to approach the cricket club to see what terms could be procured. and then to sub- mit same to an extraordinary general meeting."</p>
        <p>It is hoped that all the members will turn out if possible, so as to ensure good form in the first serious match. The first practice is fixed for this afternoon, Bully off 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Since writind the above we hear that upon minute inspection the football portion of the cricket field is very unlevel, and would require a lot of money being spent upon it, before being fit for hockey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES</p>
        <p>The lessons of the recent trials for small cars are very obvious. Far more attention must be given to the per- fection of detail. •</p>
        <p>There was no amateur class,entries being desired from the makers rather than from the public. Consequently, if bad results are obtained by the very men who are in a unique position for knowing every virtue and fault in the vehicles they have undertaken to drive, the chances of an ordinary purchaser enjoying immunity from trouble appear to be a little remote.</p>
        <p>The result of the last competition of the season pro- moted by the Motor Cycling Club has been most grati- fying. A fifty guinea Cup was presented by Mr. Albert Brown for the winner of a 150 mile run, taken in two heats of 100 and 50 miles respectively, the stipulation being that entries should be strictly limited to British built bicycles throughout. As this rule naturally included coil, accumulators, carburetter, &amp;c., there was at one time some doubt as to whether sufficient entries would be received to make the event an interesting one.</p>
        <p>As it happened, however, no fewer than seventeen were received, and of these sixteen actually started. So well did the British-built bicycles behave over a trying course, gloomy predictions to the contrary notwith- standing, that eight survivors were in at the finish, To find the winner, a stopping and starting test, arranged on the same lines as that for the Passenger Trials pre- viously, was carried out, and this resulted in a win for the "King's " bicycle, which was stopped and restarted at the specified spots in a particularly smart manner.</p>
        <p>We should like to see some effort made towards the better silencing of motor cycles. At present, many of these are far noiser than large cars, and are undoubtedly a source of great annoyance to the inhabitants of houses on any main road.</p>
        <p>Indeed, between the noise of the cycles and the clouds of dust raised by the cars, life on a main road must be anything but pleasant just now. Some of the chemical dust-laying preparations for treating the road surfaces appear to be efficacious so far as they go; but, as they are extremely expensive to use this is not far—certainly not more than a mile or two at a stretch. The fact of the matter is that an efficient remedy must be found by some means through the cars themselves, so that, wherever they are driven, they shall raise no more dust than would an ordinary horse drawn vehicle.</p>
        <p>There is a device recently placed upon the market having this end in view, and a trial trip.made lately over a dusty road upon a car fitted with the contrivance certainly seemed to us to point to a. successful solution of a serious difficulty. The Albany Silencer and Dust- Preventor is so constructed as automatically to cool the exhaust gas before it leaves the engine, the effect of this being that, in its cooled condition, it tends to fall downwards instead of to rise as it is bound to do when heated. In falling, it actually keeps the dust down, whereas, when rising, it drives this up before it. As this patent Silencer can be fittc!cl to any existing make of car, the Albany Manufacturing Co. Ltd., have evidently devised a practical scheme for the abatement of the dust nuisance—for which relief, much thanks!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>EVENING CLASSES.—Evening classes for the youth of both sees are being formed in Stockton Heath, and will commence on September 26th in St. Thomas' Schools. The following are the sub jects,days and times of teaching :—Arithmetic and writing. Mr. Moss, Mon- day, 7 to 8; geography and history, Mr. Rose, Monday, 8.5 to 9.5: theoretical machanism and electricity, Mr. Rose, Wednesday, 7.30 to 8.30; shorthand, Mr. E. H. Spinks, Thursday, 7.30 to 8.30. THE POTATO MARKET.—At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Peter Heaps, potato dealer, Leigh, was summoned for obstructing the free passage of Grappen- hall-road on August Bth, by allowing a horse and cart to remain therein from 4.30 to 4.55 p.m. Defendant said he had done the same kind of thing for 12 years at the potato market.—The Deputy Chief Constable of the county said the carts had been allowed to stand in the place named for a considerable number of years, but owing to the numerous complaints of obstruction which had been received, the Chief Constable had instructed the local police first to warn offendersand subsequently if necessary to summon them. The defendant had been repeatedly warned. The Chairman (Mr. W. Long), said that what was allowed five years ago could not be allowed now owing to the fact that the centre was a busy one.—Fined in the costs.—Peter Massey, of Lymm. charged with a similar offence, was also fined in the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>CITY OF BRADFORD EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>CITY OF BRADFORD EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>To the great satisfaction of all concerned in the management of the Bradford Exhibition the attendance during the early days of September has been larger than it was in July and August. It was expected, naturally, that the record crowds would be obtained in the beginning of Nray,when the Exhibition was entirely novel, and at Whitsuntide and in the first week of August, when the great public holidays were on. But what has actually happened has been a continuous growth of average daily numbers which goes on just as steadily now that the holidays are over, whilst, as late as Saturday, September 10th, the best previous record for any day—Bank holidays included—was beaten with a figure of 40,023, bringing the grand total to that date U 1 to 1,638,166. The most gratifying feature of the increase is that it does not merely represent a more frequent attendance of season ticket holders, but chiefly relates to the admissions by payment,and a larger influx of people from distant Places. The meaning of these movements is obvious. Visitors as they have come and gone have recommended the Exhibition to their friends. The Exhibition has proved so attractive and successful that it is to be kept open until October 29th</p>
        <p>PROVISIONS ARE DEARER.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. --- Conservative Labour Party Repudiated. THE LABOUR REPRESENTATION COMMITTEE'S METHODS.</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. --- Conservative Labour Party Repudiated. THE LABOUR REPRESENTATION COM- MITTEE'S METHODS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Podmore, jun., presided at the monthly meet- ing of this Council which was held in Bold-street Wes- leyan schools on Tuesday evening. There was a fair attendance, including Councillor Stott (secretary). THE M UNICIPAL BOWLING GREEN. The SECRETARY called the attention of the delegates to the opening of the municipal bowling green at Victoria Park. He thought much credit was due to the Council in the matter,as it was almost entirely owing to their agitation that the green had been secured. He hoped the workingmen of Warrington would do their utmost to make the experiment a success. The Mayor had stated that if success was attained the committee over which he presided would no doubt take steps to provide greens in other parts of the town. He (Coun- cillor Stott) therefore hoped that the success of their first green would be such that people in other parts of the town would soon enjoy the privileges which had been extended to the residents of Latchford. A DELEGATE asked if it were not a fact that the Mayor was one of the greatest opponents of the bowling greea agitation, and stated that grass would not grow in Warrington. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN : it is a fact that the Mayor made that remark. On being questioned he said it was moss that grew in Warrington. Continuing Mr. Podmore said he well recollected the matter of municipal greens hefty-,b first mentioned by Mr. A. C. Binks, the late presi- dentof the Council. He also remembered the letters that were sent and the snubbing the Council received at that time. The matter was hardly entertained at all,but he thought the agitation which had occurred in regard to local affairs of late had stirred up the councillors to see that the people of Warrington were not to be put off with any kind of evasion. It ought to be recognised by the public that the Trades Council had played a not unimportant part in the pro- vision of the green. He was very glad that the Mayor had been converted, and he hoped his Worship would convert the whole of his brethren on the committee, and that Warrington would, like many other towns, have many municipal greens. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>AUDITORS.</head>
        <p>THE AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Rathbone and VOW --v'vere elected auditors for the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>NOMINATIONS OF OFFICERS. At the October meeting of the Council the annual election of officers will take place, and the following gentlemen were nominated for the various positions : —President, Messrs. Podmore, York, and Plinston ; vice-president, Messrs. Rathbone, Plumb, Randles, Jolley, Plinston, Bullock and Kendrick ; treasurer, Mr. A. Warburton; secretary, Councillor F. Stott ; com- mittee, Messrs. Smith, Plumb, Kendrick, Eaton, Buckley, Potts, Randles, Jolley, Plirk.ston, Podmore and Bullock; delegates to Labour Representation Committee, Coun- cillor Stott and Mr. Buckley ; delegate to Federation meetings, Councillor Stott. THE CONTRACTS QUESTION. Councillor STOTT presented a report of the proceed- ings at the recent annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of Trades and Labour Councils. One of the most important questions discussed was the fair contracts resolution recently submitted to the Lancashire and Cheshire County Councils. The Feder- ation and a number of local Councils approached their representatives on the authorities with a request that they would adopt a standing order, drawn up by the Federation, which would compel the Councils to accept tenders only from those flrrns which adhered 0 trade union conditions. When the resolution was voted upon it was,defeated at both the Lancashire and Cheshire County Council meetings. At the Federation meeting a dis- cussion arose as to the best course to adopt and it was thought advisable to defer the matter for six or perhaps twelve months.</p>
        <p>THE CONSERVATIVE LA BOUR PARTY. The SECRETARY pointed out that at the Federation meeting Mr. Clynes, of Oldham, asked Mr. G. D. Kelley, the secretary, for information concerning the officials of the so-called Labour Association. Mr. Kelley was one of the most experienced trade union officials in Lancashire, having held office for thirty years, and knew practically every prominent trade union official in Lancashire. His reply to Mr. Clynes' question was that he did not know a single official of the Conservative Labour Association who was a prominent trade unionist, in fact he had never heard the officials' names before the organization was formed. The delegates took it that those men who were calling themselves leaders of the organization which was going to do such a great deal had not taken a prominent part in trade union work—in fact they were absolute nonentities as far as active trade union work was concerned. He thought it was very important that the Council should remember that the men who were calling themselves trade union leaders and were advising trade unionists on a certain course of action had never been known to take a prominent part in trade union work in Lancashire.</p>
        <p>EVENING MEETINGS OF LOCAL AUTHORITIES ADVOCATED. cou'scu.Lort STOTT AND THE DIFFICULTY IN WARRINGTON. Councillor STOTT, continuing his report, said that at tha Federation meeting a resolution was adopted "That the Federation be urged to take such steps as may appear necessary in respect of meetings of public bodies hairy,' held in the evenings instead of during the day." The difficulty in regard to the time of meetings was, he said,being felt in Warrington. At the present time they had many capable men who would make excellent councillors but who could not devote their time to the work owing to the meetings of committees being held during the daytime. Trade unionists were in that way prevented from being represented to the extent that they ought to be. He (Councillor Stott) voted for the resolution, believing that many more meetings could be held in the evenings. To instance his own case, he had some- times to lose two hours wages for a meeting that would last ten minutes or a quarter of an hour. When he contested Orford Ward, one of the strongest objections to his candidature was that, being a workingman, he would not be able to devote the time necessary to Council work. He had endeavoured to show by his attendance at committee and Council meetings that it was possible for workingmen to look after the business quite as well and perhaps better than some of the men who regarded the work as a kind of pastime. Mr. BUCKLEY disagreed with the Secretary's remarks. He had often considered it would be advantageous to labour men if the meetings were held in the day-time. When a man had done a day's work and was called upon to attend a meeting afterwards he had not the same brain power to devote to the business. He did not think the difficulty in regard to getting off work for a short time would always stand in the way. As times improved and the labour man became better recognized he would be able to obtain leave of absence much more conveniently. Mr. BULLOCK said there were good men who could not possibly leave work in the day-time without placing their situations in jeopardy.</p>
        <p>WORKINGMEN AND JUSTICE. At the next Feeeration. meeting a resolution will be submitted urging the desirability of appointing stipen- diaries in all boroughs. Councillor SToTT said the opinion was that some magistrates were so prejudiced that they could not g,ve a straight-forward decision. It was felt that it would be much better, and that working- men would have better justice dealt out if stipendiaries were appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>UPHOLDING _FREE TRADE.</head>
        <p>UPHOLDING _FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY read a circular letter from Mr. G. D. Kelley,secretary of the Federation,stating that state- ments had appeared in the public press to the effect that the majority of trade' unionists and workers in general were in favour of the proposals put forth by the Tariff Reform League, and that the great principles of Free Trade were being !discarded. So far as the knowledge and observation of the E.C. of the Federation could be relied upon, such statements were utterly incorrect and the reverse of being true, and although contradictions had from time to time been published in the form of resolutions passed by many Trade Unions and Trades Councils affirming their adhesion to Free Trade principles it had been felt that something more should be done to give public effect to the opinions of the workers on this question. It had therefore been resolved to afford an opportunity to the Trades Councils of Lancashire and Cheshire to appoint representatives who should meet in conference for the purpose of fully discussing the ques- tion of" Free Trade "v. "Protection," and arriving at an opinion which would carry with it the weight of the members of the Trades Councils whose representatives voted in favour of the resolution. A conference would be held on October Bth, at 3 p.m., in St. James' schoolroom. Major-street, Manchester. Councillor STOTT thought the Trades Council should show the workingmen of Warrington that it was not in favour of the arguments used at Bridge Foot and other places. It was decided that Councillor Stott should represent the Council at the conference, and support a resolution against Protection.</p>
        <p>A DEMONSTRATION POSTPONED. THE FORTHCOMING ELECTIONS. Mr. 13ucKury presented a report on the past month's work of the Labour Representation Committee. Unfortunately it had been found advisable to postpone the demonstration which it had. been arranged to hold. After considerable discussion it . was decided that a letter be sent to the affiliated socipties asking them if they were in favour of the committee contesting the municipal elections in November, and if so how many wards they were in favour of contesting. Each society was also asked if it was prepared to nominate a candi- date. A member of the committee had given notice of a motion to delete the rule that "any affiliated society shall have power to nominate a candidate for an elec- tion providing it pays the whole of the expenses and obtains the committee's endorsement." The Council instructed its representatives to vote in favour of the retention of the rule. It was decided to hold a private meeting next Tues- da'y evening to discuss the course to be taken in regard to the elections and the suggested reframing of the sixth clause of the fair contracts resolution which was rejected by the Town Council. THE MEETINGS OF THE LABOUR REPRESENTATION COMMITTEE. THE QUESTION OF SECRECY. Considerable discussion ensued on a resolution</p>
        <p>to be submitted to the committee that in future each delegate to the Labour Representation Committee should be allowed to bring a visitor to the meetings providing the visitor was a paying member. Mr. PLUMB said the proposition seemed to be an attempt on the part of some societies to get to know all the business that was going on and spread it in the political camps as soon as they could. Mr. KENDRICK said there would be no necessity to appoint delegates if societies could send "any Dick, Tom or Harry "to the meetings. Mr. SMITH was in favour of the introduction of visitors for the simple reason that they were allowed to attend meetings of the Trades Council. They had even allowed non-delegates to speak at Council meetings. The SECRETARY pointed out that the Labour Repre- sentation Committee's business was politics and theY had a political game to play. He did not think it would be advisable to admit visitors at the present juncture, although a time might come when they could be admitted. The delegates were supposed to report the business of the committee to their respective branches, and the rank and file of the members could therefore be in touch with what was going on. The gentleman whol the last meeting of the committee applied for admission and thus brought the question foxward, did not agree with the action of the committee. They did not allow the Press to attend their meetings and occasions arose when it was not thought fit to communicate the whole of the business to the branches. If the admission ef visitors was sanctioned there was one society alone which might send seventy or eighty members. Mr. JOLLEY thought the proposal was altogether unbusinesslike.</p>
        <p>Mr. BUCKLB-Y objected to it on the same grounds. It would imply that societies were sending spies on the actions of the delegates to see what was really done. Paying members of the committee had just as much right to know the business as the officials and delegates. There was no reason why the business should not be communicated as soon as final decisions were arrived at. Mr. PLUMB asked why Mr. Buckley had not reported the whole of the business transacted to the Trades Coun- cil which was affiliated with the committee. Mr. BUCKLEY : When business has not been com- plete I have not been in a position to report. If anY, delegate can mention an instance where I have kept bac. business purposely, I am willing to resign. The CHAIRMAN said politics were a very different thing from the business of the Trades Council. lie pre- sumed every political party had an executive to whoa!, was entrusted the negotiations and "wire-pulling. they allowed visitors to attend the committee meetings they might have the presence of an individual Who would " give away" the negotiations before they Were complete. On a vote being taken the Council instructed its delegates to oppose the resolution to admit visitors.</p>
        <p>THE HOUSING QUESTION. Mr. T. HonnocKs, who represented the Council at the recent conference on the housing question at Leeds, presented an interesting report of the proceedings. He was cordially thanked on the proposition of 31r- BUCKLEY seconded by Mr. JOLLEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTnICT LEAGUE. A main in connection with the Warrington and PIS: trict League took place on the Warrington CrickTd, Ground on Saturday. The green was in a splendl condition, and there was some good bowling. There were no fewer than 171 entries,but only about 70 garnes were played. The main will be continued on Saturdal• Mr. Harry Boyle, Manchester, and Mr. W. J. Shaw', Warrington, acted as referees. The following were the scores of the</p>
        <p>FIRST ROUND. 13 T. Harrison, 2 H. Perry 5 l5 J. T. Harrison,4 ......... 14., C. Roberts, s c l5 J. Bramall, 5 ............ 0a R. Baker,3 l5 J. Worsley,l l6 J. Wood, 5 l5 W. Fidler, 4 A. Greenhalgh, sc l5 J. Sankey, 2 ............ ~.r 51. Tobin, 2 l5 W. Butterworth, 4 ...... j, W. Worsley,l l5 B. Tomlinson, 1 ......... a 10 A. Scott, 1 H. Fogg, 1 J. E. Harrison, 2 . 1% T. Hackett, sc l5 W. Shaw,4 W. Holloway, 4 15 F. Turner, 3 ... ............ „ A. J. Jones, sc l5 W. Povey, 3 ............... ; Jos. Weston, 9 S. Davies, 5 l5 G. Brooks, 4 .... 14 J. Jackson, 2 J. Davies, 2 W.O. J. Foster, 4 absent ,3 W. Forster, 3 J. Barton, '8 15 T. Hackett, 4 ........... -1-7; J. E. Jefferson, 1 VV. Rostron, 4 l5 H. Houghton, 2 ......... 14 A. Stringer, 2 l5 G.Caddock, 3 ............ E. A. Sanders, 3 l5 R. Kerr, 2 .................. 2,:f J. Higham, 2 J. Hall, 1 l5 W. Gibson, 2 ............... ~) J. Massey, '-&gt; T. O'Brien, 2 'G. Simcock, 5 l5 T. Handles ...... 9 ,H. Rowles, 2 El. Williams, 3 J. .Tolley, 4 W. Hatch, 4 l5 W. Turner, 3................ 'J. Goulden, 1 17 W. Daintith, 3 J. Allison' sc. ~,, J. Houghton, 2 'J. Ditchfleld, 3 6 J. Willett, 9 l5 G. Holbrook, 1 J. Jennings, sc. .1-o0 P. Berry, 1 J. Simister, 3 l5 R. Antrobus, 1 ........... i!!, F. Dean 3 1. J. Hill, '2 15 W. J. Daintith, 3 .......12 15 T. Wells, 1 ................ I J. Wareham, 1 C. Wright, 5 l5 T. Ellison, sc ........... Joseph Harrison, 1 17 W. Eyes,9 T. Humphreys, 2 ~,, G. Ball, son., sc l5 J. Wilcox, 5 ............... Lu, W. Carter, 2 l5 A. Woods, 1 ............... -.lg. Joseph Unsworth, 1 E. Taylor, 1 V. W. Jolley, 5 W w.o J. . Kaye, 5 ......... a J. W. Wrench, 2 bsena 15 C. Grant, 3 ............... ...7 R. Young,2 l5 R. Fairbrother, 1......... If T. Rigby, 2 l5 T. Bridge, 1 . .............. J. Hardman, 2 l5 S. Shaw, 2 .................. -11 W. Stevenson," F. J. Jefferson, Sc l5 T. Cooper, 1.................. 4 S. Clare, 2 J. Smith, 3 T. Lawton, 2 W. Moos, 1 J. Sudlow,s l5 J. Smith, 5 .........