        <p>WONDILRFUL DISCOVERY.—This is tbe age LI of research and experiment, when all nature, so to speak, is ransacked by the scientific for the comfort and happiness of man. Science has Indeed made giant strides during the past century, and among the—by no means least Important—discoveries in medicine comes that of THRRAPION. This preparation it unquestionably one of the most genuine and reliable Patent Medicines ever introduced, and has, we understand, been used in the Continental Hospitals by Ricord, Roston. Jobert, Velpeau, Maisonneuve, the well- known Chassaignac, and indeed by all who are regarded as authorities in such matters, including the celebrated Lallemand, and Roux, by whom it was some time since uniformly adopted, and that it is worthy the attention of those who require such a remedy we think there is no doubt. From the time of Aristotle downwards, a potent agent in the rernoml of these diseases has (like the famed philosopher's stone) been the object of search of some hopeful generous minds and far beNond the mere power—if such could ever have been discovered—of transmuting the baser metals into gold is sutely the discovery of a remedy so potent as to replenish the failing energies in the one case, and in the Other so effectually, speedily and sately to expel from the system the poisons of acquired or inherited disease in all their protean forms as to leave no taint or trace behind. Such is the new French Remedy THRRAPION, which may certainly rank with, if not take precedence of, misty of the discoveries of our day, about which no little ostentation and noise have been made, and the extensive and ever-increasing demand that has been created for this medicine wherever introdneed appears to prove that it is destined to cast into oblivion all those questionable remedies that were formerly the sole reliance of medical men. Therapion may be obtained of the principal chemists and merchants throughout the world.—" Diamond rie7ds Advertiler."— Kimberley.