        <p>Warrington Technical Institute. A Winwick Lunatic's Reply. In the examination in manual training held by the A deputation from the Burnley Board of Guardians City and Guilds of London Institute in April last, two or three days ago visited the Win wick Asylum, John Edward Morgan who was taught at tbe evening and on reporting upon this at a meeting of the board classes held in this subject at the Warrington Techni- on Thursday a laugh was created by one of the deputa- cal Institute has been awarded the First Class tion saying that a colleague on being appealed to by Teachers' Certificate. an inmate, stated he would do what he could with a The Proposed Cantilevers at Warrington view to his discharge, whereupon the lunatic answered, "It's no use telling new lies to an old Bridge. liar." A special meeting of the General Purposes Coin- i SUGAR KEEPS DEAR.—Advt. mittee is to be held on Monday for the purpose of , , considering the tenders received for the erection of Picnic from the Barracks. cantilevers at Warrington Bridge. The wives and children of the soldiers stationed at SUGAR Is DEARER. —Advt. Orford Barracks had their annual picnic on Thursday Rate Increases in Cheshire. and a very successful event it proved. The party , numbering about 400 was conveyed to Heatley in There has been a serious increase in the Cheshire , buses supplied by Mr. E. Connor, and spent a pleasant poor rate, and it is now seen that the next rate for i tre. i i n a field near the Saracen's head. The band of the half-year ending March will be still higher. A supplementary rat; has had to be granted to some u t e depot was n attendance and played selections. / townships. The estimate of expenditure in the Maccles- Sergeant Major Dalmage had charge of the arrange- field Union is the largest the Clerk says he has had to ments which were,in every way satisfactory. submit for many years. In the Bucklow Union the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel. estimates show an increase of £4,600, and it is stated 1 Special services were held on Sunday in celebration that the rate will be increased by 2d. in the 1. lof the chapel anniversary. There were large con- Military Changes. ! gregations, the preacher morning and evening being The Loyal North Lancashire Regiment is about to the Rev. Alfred Freeman, formerly of this circuit. In lose two of its officers, each of whom has seen active I the afternoon the choir rendered a service of song service in South Africa. One of them is Lieutenant ' entitled "More than conqueror," Mr. F. Johnson of E. G. Case, who is.transferring to the 16th (Queen's) ! Sankey, presided, and Mrs. Johnson gave the connec- tive readings. At the evening service the anthem Lancers, and the other, Lieutenant A. S. G. Cattell, ; who commenced his career in the third South Lanca- i "Give ear 0 Lord "was creditably sung. Mr. E. G-. shires, at Warrington,in October, 1899, and was given , Rickers ably presided at the organ. The collections his commission in the' Loyals over three years ago. I were on behalf of the trust fund. Captain E. M. Beall has been appointed an instructor The Park of musketry to the Militia at Warrington. He has had over eight years' service, and took part in the I Transvaal campaign. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK rOV Bas 1 The rark Aquarium. Visitors to Bank. Park will have noticed that the / tanks in the aquarium have been emptied of their con- I tents, and the choice specimens of fish which delighted 1 /so many sightseers are seen no more. The reason 800 TSin LANCASFIIRE.—Advt. I assigned for this action is the low level of the water in The Proposed Soldier's Memorial. ! the reservoir at Appleton. The tanks at the aquarium A meeting of the Workingmen's Committee which is i require something like 14,000 gallons per day. and as putting forth its best endeavours to raise finds for the •it is with difficulty that the Corporation are able to proposed memorial to the South Lancashire Regiment I meet the demands of manufacturers for Appleton was held at the Town Hall on Thursday evening. Mr. I water, it was thought advisable to partially close the D. Davies presided. The main business was the . aquarium for the present. The fish have been trans- arrangement of details for the concert which is to be I ferred to the pond. held in aid of the funds on November sth. A letter was read from Captain Goren, who, on behalf of Major flartley,the commanding officer of the trocps at Orford Barracks offered the services of the South Lancashire band to play at the concert. He thankedthe Working- men's Committee for their services in connection with the fund. The concert promises to be a great success. Many prominent local artistes have .I:ltemised their services. The committee has already raised consider. ably more than £lOO for the memorialfund. SUGAR KEEPS DEAR.—Advt. Local Druidism. At a meeting of the " Trafalgar " Lodge of the Order of Druids held on Saturday, heaity congratula- tions were offered to Bro. David Houglton, who las been appointed by the A.M.D. to the position of Vice- Grand Master of the Order of Druids, comprising some 80,000 members. Notwithsttnding a very serious illness from which he is only just recovering Bro. Houghton attended the meeting md was accom- panied .by Past District Masters 1 Eden and S. Dunbobbin. After Bro. Houghton hEd addressed the meeting, and given some very intereEting and instruc- tive advice, P. A. Shaw in a few wards congratulated Bro. Houghton upon his election to sich an important and dignified office—one which wirking men were rarely called upon to fill. Therefore the more honour was attached to Bro. Houghton's eloction, and it was a credit to him and the town to wlich he belonged. Congratulations also followed from I.D.M. Bebbington P.D.M. Duabobbin, and P.D.M. Eder, who pointed out that the Druids Society was the only friendly society in Warrington which had got a loca. man so near to the highest office in the order. Bro. Houghton acknowledged the compliments. SUGAR Is DEARER.—Advt. Warrington Poachers Caught. A gang of Warrington peachtrs visited Lord Newton's estate at Newton-le-Willtws on Thursday night last week and made a good Web of rabbits by working their way a considerable dstance with four sixty-yard nets and other impedim3nts. They, how- ever, were being followed by two keepers, who also informed the police. At daybreak, is the gang were making tracks for Earlestown, they were surprised whilst crossing Ashton-road near de Hut. Police and keepers darted out from behind a ledge, and captured two of the poachers, whilst the otter four dashed off, leaving their nets, pegs, Bcc., ard sacks containing thirty-three rabbits. The two mm secured gave their names as James Clare and Alfred France. The other men were chased but got clear. The prisoners were brought up at Newton-le-Willows Police Court on Monday charged with night poaching. Evidence was given by Police-sergeant hacks, Police-constable Hughes, Samuel Watkins (lead gamekeeper), and Geoffrey Barrow, assistant, and prisoners, pleading guilty, were each sent to gaol for three months' hard labour. They were also ord3red to be bound over in £lO and two sureties of £5 eich for twelve months; in default three months' hardlabour. BASSMETTES AND MAII CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Suicide at Nineteen—Coroner and " Unsound Mind " Verdict. At the Police Station on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner leld an inquest touching the death of Arthur Bentley aged nineteen, a forge worker, living at 3, Back Haydock-street. Theresa Bentley, deceased's widow, said deceased left home at five o'clock on Saturday evening when he appeared to be as usual. When he returned at 8.30 he was strongly under the influence of drink, and commenced to be quarrelsome because she spoke to him about coming home in such a cindition. Witness left the house, thinking deceased would go to sleep. She re- turned at nine o'clock and found the door fastened. Having forced an entrance she saw deceased hanging by the neck by a cord attached to a nail in the beam of the kit Chen ceiling. She called for assistance and Mrs. Thompson, a neighbour, cut deceased down. Dr. Hodgkinson was summoned and pronounced life to be extinct. Deceased had been out of work for weeks and had been very low-spirited, but he had never threatened- to take his life. They had been married nine months.--Mary Thompson, 26, Haydock- street, deposed to cutting deceased down.—The Coroner remarked that the law assumed everyone to be of sound mind until the contrary was proved. The only evidence in that direction was that deceased was depressed. .If instead of hanging himself he had killed his wife, the jury would never have dreamt of saying he was of unsound mind. Cases of Suicide were becoming very frequent indeed. His view was that they would be more frequent if juries continually returned verdicts of "Unsound mind" when there was very little evidence to support that conclusion.—A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind" was returned.</p>
