        <p>/1) left hand "side shouTd be used. In position A this is generally called check side, and in position B running 'side. Yet in both positions the side used is K.entical, and in each case is running side off the opposing angle of the side cushion. In position ,‘, how- ever, the side is check side as Lne ball runs along the top cushion. In strokes of this nature a better and less misleading term would be pocket side, this being the side necessary to carry the ball into the pocket should it strike be opposing angle. (To be Continued.) Mr. Riso Levi will be pleased to answer in tl'ese eolumns all questions relative to the _article appearing -wt;ekly, and as far as possible any questions on other points in connection with the game of Billiards. Envelopes should be marked "Billiards."</p>
