        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions. TUESDAY.—Before Messrs. William Long (in the chair) and F. A. Frost. REFUSING TO QUlT.—Roger Grady, an Irish labou rer living at Norton, was charged with refusing toqu the Red Lion Inn, Moore, when requested to do so on August 28th.—James Jiggens, barman, said the defedant came into the house between six and seven p.m. and had three or four glasses of rum. Witness then thought he had had sufficient and asked him to leave. He refused and witness put him out, but he came back and caused a disturbance.—Fined ss. and costs. HOME TROUBLES.—John Miller, labourer, Appleton, was charged with persistent cruelty so his wife, in con- sequence of which she had been compelled to leave him. —The complainant, who is an old woman, appeared to be reluctant to give evidence against her husband. She had to be closely questioned by the Bench before she would admit that her husband "had turned her out of the house and locked the door."—Defendant denied the offence, and said he "was quite willing to keep the house." It was not all his own fault ; other people were to blame.—The case was adjourned for two months. MINOR OFFENCES.—Edward Tyman, of Croviley. was fined ss. for riaing on a lurry without reins at Grappen- hall,and James Millwood,for being asleep on his cart on the highway at Stockton Heath,was fined in the costs.— For allowing their respective dogs to be at large with- out collar or muzzle the following persons were fined in the costs :—Fred Smith, Richard Dunn,George Jones Lillie Machell and James Ashton.—Fora similar offence William Barlow (who did not appear) was fined 2s. 6d. and costs.—Edward Connor, Sankey-street, Warrington, was ordered to pay the costs for allowing two horses to stray on the highway at Grappenhall on the 16th August. Mr. William Connor stated that the animals got out of a field owing to a defective hedge which had been since repaired.—Alexander Lawton and Reginald Lawton, Frederick-street, Latchford, were summoned for riding bicycles on the highway at Grappenhall at 9.30 p.m. without a lighted lamp. The son was fined in the costs, 3s. 6d., and the father who refused to appear and who, Superintendent Ennion said, had attempted to bribe the police not to report the case, was fined 3s. 6d. and the costs.—John Bell, Stockton Heath was charged with a similar offence at Antrobus and was fined in the costs.—For using obscene language in Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, on August 6th, Edward Robinson, Latchford, was fined ss.—Joseph Brocklehurst, farmer, Antrobus, was summoned for using threats to Robert William Baxter,also of Antrobus, but the case was dismissed with a warning from the Bench.—For failing to send his child regularly to school Joseph Howarth,Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, was fined ss. For the past quarter of a century there has barn continua flow of letters bearing testimony to the truly wondorfu cures effected by CLARKE'S WORLD_FAMED BLOOD MIXTURE, "the finest Blood Purifier that science and medical skill have brought to light." Sufferers from Scrofula Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs,Skin and Blood Diseases. Pimples and Sores of any kind, are solicited to give It a trial to try its value. Sold everywere at 21 Sd per bottle, beware of worthless imitations andsubstitutes.—Advert. 6. CLARKE'S B 41 PILLS are warranted to cure, in sex, all acquired or constitutional discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the tack. Free from I Mercury. Established upwards of 30 years. In boxes 4., 6d each, of all chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors through- out the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers. The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Company. Lincoln.— Advt. o FLOBILINE I—Fox THE TEETH AND BREATH.—Thoroughly leanses the teeth from all parasites or impurities, hardens he gums, prevents tartar, stops decay, and gives to the teeth a peculiar pearly whiteness and a delightful fragrance to the breath. Price 2e. 6d. for the liquid, or is. per jar for the Floriline " Pow